  "**Knowing the regime is not the same as profiting from it:** a strategy trading the sign of its own trailing
  coefficient earns **+1.9 to +2.8 bps per day** more than always shorting high funding, but at **t = 0.64 to
  0.88** and a block-bootstrap **p of 0.19 to 0.29** that gain is **not distinguishable from zero**". **Limb
  (i), the within-contract, time-series form: still OPEN** — the retrieved document's only "time-series form"
  passage concerns **cross-venue dispersion**, not the funding level, and its regime series is a
  **cross-sectional** daily information coefficient rolled over time. **No score change**, because the
  Cycle-113 pre-registration registered **no score-move rule for Priority 3** and the finding bears on the
  *gate*, not on the premise (resilience is already at the cell floor of 1, and falsifiability was already
  lowered to 2 at Cycle 112 for the same class of reason). **Third hard-stop condition: the regime gate cannot
  carry the rule** — a rule armed only when a trailing sign flips is accurate at 54.7 %–56.3 % and worth
  +1.9 to +2.8 bps per day at p = 0.19 to 0.29, so a specification that depends on the gate alone is a null by
  construction. **V-008 is amended, not re-created**, with a fourth requirement, **(iv) a gate-value arm**:
  the gate's own match rate and the gate's own excess over the round trip must be reported **separately** from
  the rule's, and a gate indistinguishable from zero is a **null on the gated rule**, not a smaller edge. The
  specification remains **executable**.

**One-sentence hypothesis.** The perpetual-futures funding rate is settled every few hours and
published by the exchange; because it measures the crowded-leverage state directly, a rule that
takes the side opposite to the funding-rate crowd — short when the funding rate is high and
positive (longs paying shorts), long when it is low or negative — captures a mean-reversion
premium in the futures-spot basis that decays as the crowd unwinds.

**Plain-language description.** Crypto perpetual futures have no expiry. To keep the futures
price close to the spot price, exchanges make the long and short sides pay each other a funding
rate every few hours. When the funding rate is high and positive, lots of people are long and
paying the shorts — the market is crowded on the long side. That crowd is leveraged and can be
forced to unwind. A participant who goes short when the funding rate is very positive (betting
against the crowded long side) and long when it is very negative (betting against the crowded
short side) captures the mean reversion in the basis, funded by the funding-rate payments
themselves.

**Mechanism (causal chain).**
1. Perpetual futures have no expiry date, so there is no guaranteed convergence to spot; instead,
   exchanges use a funding rate — a periodic payment between longs and shorts proportional to the
   futures-spot gap — to keep the futures price close to spot (He et al., 2022/2024, S-086;
   Jermann et al., 2024, S-087).
2. The funding rate is typically settled every 8 hours and equals approximately the average
   futures-spot spread over the preceding period (S-086). It is published by the exchange on a
   fixed cadence — the state is published by the venue, with no look-ahead and no proxy
   construction.
3. A high positive funding rate means longs are paying shorts: the market is crowded on the long
   side, and the crowd is leveraged (perpetuals are leveraged instruments). A high negative rate
   means the reverse: the crowd is on the short side.
4. The crowded side is exposed to unwinding: when the funding rate reverses or the price moves
   against the crowd, leveraged positions are liquidated, which accelerates the move in the
   opposite direction — a forced-deleveraging spiral in the same family as recall-driven forced
   covering (IDEA-008) but in a derivatives venue (He et al., S-086).
5. Taking the side opposite the funding-rate crowd — short when longs are paying and crowded,
   long when shorts are paying and crowded — means supplying liquidity to a crowd that must
   unwind, collecting the funding-rate payments and the basis mean reversion. The edge is a
   *crowded-leverage reversal premium*, not a trend-following or breakout signal.
6. Because the funding rate is published every few hours, the state is observable at decision
   time and the trade can be scheduled around each settlement — this is the clearest instance of
   the Cycle-2 venue-selection rule: "the state is published by the venue."

**Expected return source.** The funding-rate payment itself (collecting the rate by being on the
receiving side) plus the mean reversion of the futures-spot basis as the crowded leveraged
position unwinds — a liquidity-provision/crowded-leverage reversal premium paid by the leveraged
crowd, net of execution costs, funding costs, and liquidation-spike risk.

**Originality.**
- *Closest known work:* He, Kelly, Lu, and Manela (2022/2024) "Fundamentals of Perpetual Futures"
  (arXiv:2212.06888, S-086); Jermann, Phelps, and Zhang (2024) "Perpetual Futures Pricing"
  (S-087); Christin, Routledge, Soska, Zetlin-Jones "The crypto carry trade" (preprint, cited in
  S-086); Emre Inan "Predictability of Funding Rates" (SSRN, S-088); and the "Exploring risk and
  return profiles of funding rate arbitrage" study (Blockchain Research and Applications, 2026,
  S-089).
- *What is already known:* the funding rate keeps perpetual futures close to spot (S-086);
  deviations from no-arbitrage prices in crypto are larger than in traditional markets, comove
  across currencies, and diminish over time (S-086); past returns predict futures-spot deviations
  with R² above 50% (momentum feedback in S-086); funding-rate arbitrage strategies (long spot,
  short perpetual when futures are expensive) have been documented (S-086, S-088); and funding
  rates contain information about future spot volatility beyond fixed-maturity futures measures
  (S-089).
- *Substantive differentiator claimed here:* (i) the candidate is *not* the well-documented
  funding-rate arbitrage (long spot, short expensive perpetual) — which is an arbitrage, not a
  state-conditional reversal — but a **crowded-leverage reversal** keyed to the funding-rate
  level as a state variable; (ii) the state is published by the exchange every few hours with
  zero lag, which is the venue-selection ideal; and (iii) the holding horizon is a few funding
  periods (intraday to multi-day), not the perpetual arbitrage's indefinite hold.
- *Honest classification:* **mechanism documented in parts, candidate specification new** *(the Cycle-3
  classification)* — **reclassified at Cycle 66 as an incremental variant of published conditional
  funding-reversal work.** The funding-rate mechanism and the funding-rate arbitrage are published;
  the joint specification — taking the side opposite the funding-rate crowd as a reversal trade keyed to
  the funding-rate *level* (not the basis) — is not a known baseline in the register or in the located
  literature. The venue risk (crypto) is real and is stated honestly. **Cycle 66 correction:** the
  located literature has since been shown to contain *direct published tests of the candidate's own
  prediction* (S-310, S-320) and a *stronger, conditional* form of the same idea (S-310: the reversal
  appears on days when the market moved with the crowd, and is replaced by **continuation** on days when
  it moved against it). What remains unclaimed is a **formulation difference** — a dated, single-asset,
  decile-keyed implementation — not a mechanism difference, and the register scores it as such.
- *Confidence in the comparison:* moderate-to-low. S-086 was verified via arXiv (2212.06888);
  S-087 via the Wharton/arXiv PDF; S-088 via SSRN. **Cycle 66: the comparison is now far better
  grounded and no more favourable.** The family now carries a **systematic review** (S-312),
  peer-reviewed microstructure work (S-313), a **published venue rulebook** (S-321) and the published
  pricing-and-clamp record (S-315 … S-318). The dossier's own claim that no published work tests the
  funding-rate-level reversal as a tradeable rule is **false as written**: published work tests it
  (S-310, S-320) and publishes a conditional version of it (S-310). The venue risk of crypto remains
  unquantified here.

**Target scope.** Crypto perpetual futures on major exchanges (Binance, Bybit, OKX, etc.) for
liquid underlyings (BTC, ETH and a small set of large altcoins); holding horizon one to several
funding periods (8-hour to multi-day); per underlying; regime conditions: exclude periods of
exchange outage or severe congestion; exclude underlyings with thin perpetual order books.

**Observables (knowable at decision time, conceptually).**
- The funding rate, published by the exchange every funding period (typically every 8 hours) —
  the state variable, with zero lag.
- The futures-spot basis (perpetual price minus spot price) — the object that mean-reverts.
- Funding-rate extremes: the funding rate relative to its own history (e.g. top/bottom decile)
  — the entry signal. **Cycle 66 amendment:** this percentile-based state is **contaminated by the
  venue's published clamp**, and the realised funding rate is a **path-dependent moving average** of the
  premium rather than an instantaneous reading of the crowd (S-315, S-317, S-318, S-321). The percentile
  must therefore be computed on clamp-excluded observations, and the venue's published clamp band and
  funding cap must be treated as part of the state definition, not as a footnote to it.
- Open interest and volume in the perpetual — the size of the crowded position.
- *Timing hazards to flag for the validator:* funding rates can clamp at a minimum (e.g. 0.01%
  per period in S-086), so small rates are not informative; the exchange can change the funding
  cadence or clamp rules; crypto markets trade 24/7 so the "session" concept does not apply;
  and exchange-specific funding-rate implementations differ (clamp regions, calculation methods),
  so results are not transferable across venues without restatement. **Cycle 66 additions:** the
  venue's clamp factor, funding cap, sampling interval and impact notional are **governance-adjustable
  parameters** (S-321), so the state is not stable across regimes without a re-specification; the
  correction after a funding extreme is **realised-volatility-conditioned** (S-311: median 27.0 hours
  in high-volatility episodes against 3.0 hours in low-volatility ones); and **spreads peak about two
  hours after standard settlement times** (S-313), which is where the candidate's cost floor is worst
  relative to the window it trades.

**Rule sketch (descriptive, non-executable).** For each underlying in scope, at each funding
settlement: read the published funding rate; if the rate is in the top decile of its own trailing
distribution (longs paying, crowded long side), take a short perpetual position sized as a small
fraction of open interest; if the rate is in the bottom decile (shorts paying, crowded short
side), take a long position; exit after a pre-specified number of funding periods (e.g. 1–3) or
when the funding rate crosses zero, whichever comes first, with the exit rule fixed in advance.
Exclusions: thin order books, exchange outages, clamp-region rates, and underlyings with
insufficient open interest.

**Persistence thesis.** Perpetual futures are leveraged instruments with no expiry, so the
funding rate is the mechanism that keeps the crowd aligned with spot; the crowded side is always
exposed to unwinding; and the funding rate is published by the exchange, so the state is always
observable. It decays if the funding-rate crowd becomes sophisticated enough to pre-position
against reversals, if exchange funding implementations change to reduce the signal, or if
liquidity in thin underlyings dries up. Both decay channels are observable.

**Constraints.** Cost/liquidity: crypto perpetual spreads are narrow for majors but the trade is
intraday and frequent, so spread and slippage accumulate; this is the binding constraint. Capacity:
bounded by perpetual open interest and depth; large altcoins have thin books. Financing: a
leveraged perpetual position incurs funding that moves with the same state variable that
generates the signal — a self-referencing hazard that must be handled explicitly (the position's
own funding cost moves with the state). Borrow: not applicable (perpetuals are not borrows).
Market impact: trading into a crowded funding period is an impact machine. Venue/operational:
exchange custody risk, 24/7 trading, funding-cadence changes, and venue-specific clamp rules are
real risks that must be stated, not assumed away.

**Regime sensitivity, tail exposure, crash behavior, hidden beta.** Regime: the funding-rate
state *is* the regime — the candidate predicts an edge only at funding-rate extremes. Tail: a
leveraged unwind can be violent and fast, and the position is on the side of the unwind (which is
the payoff) but can be stopped out before the reversal completes. Crash behaviour: in a
market-wide deleveraging, funding rates can spike across underlyings simultaneously and the
position is exposed to gap risk at the next funding settlement. Hidden beta: the position is
long/short crypto underlyings and carries crypto market beta; beta must be controlled explicitly.
Correlated risks: funding rates comove across crypto underlyings (S-086), so the book is a single
crypto-leverage bet.

**Falsifiable predictions and rejection conditions.**
1. *Reversal:* a high positive funding rate is followed by a negative futures-spot basis move
   (the crowded long side unwinds), and a high negative rate is followed by a positive basis
   move, within 1–3 funding periods. **Kill:** no mean reversion, or the basis moves in the same
   direction as the funding rate (momentum, not reversal).
2. *State scaling:* the size of the reversal is increasing in the funding-rate extreme (top vs
   bottom decile). **Kill:** no relation to the funding-rate level.
3. *Funding-carry contribution:* a material share of the return comes from the funding-rate
   payment itself (being on the receiving side), not only from the basis move. **Kill:** the
   reversal does not exceed the funding cost of holding the position.
4. *Venue transferability:* the effect holds on at least two exchanges with different funding
   implementations. **Kill:** the effect is exchange-specific, which would mean it is a venue
   artefact rather than a mechanism.
5. *Cost floor:* the gross reversal plus funding received exceeds round-trip cost (spread,
   slippage, funding paid while holding) in the selected underlyings. **Kill:** below cost in
   every selected underlying.

**Main confounders and rival explanations.**
- *Momentum feedback:* past returns predict futures-spot deviations with R² above 50% (S-086) —
  high funding rates may coincide with upward momentum, so the reversal may be a momentum
  continuation in disguise. The candidate must separate reversal from momentum.
- *Exchange-specific implementation:* clamp regions and calculation methods differ by venue, so
  an apparent effect on one exchange may not exist on another.
- *Custody and exchange risk:* crypto venue risk is real and is not diversifiable within the
  strategy.
- *The candidate may be a repackaged funding-rate arbitrage:* the honest null is that the
  documented arbitrage (long spot, short expensive perpetual) already captures the edge, and the
  reversal layer adds nothing.

**Data requirements and feasibility for a future independent validation.** (Specification only —
nothing is acquired or processed here.) Public: exchange-published funding rates (every funding
period, typically 8 hours), perpetual and spot prices, open interest and volume. Commercial:
exchange API access for historical funding rates and order-book data. Feasibility: **high** for
the data (all published by the venue); **medium** for the identification (the reversal must be
separated from momentum, and venue risk is real).

**Theory/evidence status.**
- *Conceptual rationale:* supported — the funding-rate mechanism and its role in keeping
  perpetual futures close to spot (S-086); deviations and arbitrage (S-086, S-087);
  funding-rate predictability (S-088).
- *Mechanism supported by sources:* yes for the funding-rate mechanism and the arbitrage
  (S-086, S-087); the *crowded-leverage reversal* specification is an unresolved conjecture.
- *Prior empirical support reported by others:* for deviations, arbitrage returns and funding-rate
  predictability (S-086, S-088, S-089); not for the joint reversal specification.
- *Unresolved:* whether the funding-rate level predicts a reversal after costs, whether the effect
  transfers across venues, and whether crypto venue risk is manageable.

**Remaining uncertainty / confidence.** *(Updated at Cycle 112; Cycle 66 text retained below.)* Confidence
that the funding rate is published, settled every few hours, and measures the crowded-leverage state:
**high** as to publication and settlement, and now **low** as to the crowded-leverage *reading* — the
largest sample located reports that the level "measures how retail-oriented a contract is rather than how
crowded its current position structure is" and that "**open interest builds into and through high-funding
settlements** … positions are opened into the payment, not unwound before it" (S-389), while an
independent 2026 test of the candidate's own percentile construction finds the effect **asymmetric**, with
the positive-funding leg unsupported (S-390). Confidence that a tradeable reversal rule can be built on it:
**low** — the sign of the relation has been observed to invert between a 2020–21 reversal regime and a
2023–26 continuation regime, and in the current regime the coefficients are positive and significant
(S-389); the candidate's percentile construction is reported null at the same time as the raw level's effect
decays to "too little to trade" above the 8 bps reference round trip (S-389), with the excess over costs
indistinguishable from zero (+2.2 bps, t = 0.68) and the cross-venue cost anchor at 13 bps per round trip
(S-392); the venue itself published the same rule in 2017 with sub-50 % success rates in three of four cells
(S-391). Priority score **42/100**; rank **4 of 4** in the active set (behind IDEA-011 at 69.0, IDEA-004 at
63.0 and IDEA-014 at 52.0). Two hard-stop conditions now attach: **costs likely to overwhelm the
hypothesized effect**, and **a claim that merely repackages a known idea**. **Concrete next question:**
*does the direction-conditioned, volatility-conditioned, regime-gated reversal — take the side opposite the
funding crowd only when the preceding price move was in the crowd's direction, only when the within-contract
trailing sign of the funding–return relation is negative, hold for a horizon set by realised volatility, and
enter only outside the venue's published clamp band — predict a futures-spot basis reversal after costs, and
does it add anything to the published funding arbitrage?* That is a **validation** question, and V-008 (as
amended in Cycle 66 and again in Cycle 112) is its vehicle; the register's new gap **G-37** asks the
literature-side half of the same question.

*(Cycle-66 text, retained:)* Confidence that the funding rate is
published, settled every few hours, and measures the crowded-leverage state: **high** (exchange
documentation and academic work). Confidence that a tradeable reversal rule can be built on it:
**moderate-to-low** — the mechanism is real and the venue-selection rule is met (the state is published
by the venue itself with zero lag), but **the published evidence now contradicts the candidate's
unconditional sign claim** (S-310, S-320), the correction speed is published as
**realised-volatility-conditioned** (S-311), the state is a **clamped, path-dependent** quantity
(S-315, S-317, S-318, S-321), and the nearest published out-of-sample test of the premise **collapsed**
(S-319). Priority score **53/100**; rank **4 of 5** in the active set (behind IDEA-011 and IDEA-004 at
69.0 and IDEA-010 at 68.0, ahead of IDEA-014 at 52.0). **Concrete next question:** *does the
direction-conditioned, volatility-conditioned reversal — take the side opposite the funding crowd only
when the preceding price move was in the crowd's direction, hold for a horizon set by realised
volatility, and enter only outside the venue's published clamp band — predict a futures-spot basis
reversal after costs, and does it add anything to the published funding arbitrage?* That is a
**validation** question, and V-008 (as amended in Cycle 66) is its vehicle.

**Cycle 6 screen (G-13 publication timing; G-12 sign or existence).**
- **G-13 — the state is published before the decision: PASS.** The funding rate is published by the
  venue for each funding period, and the rate that has just been settled — the one a rule would
  condition on — is the venue's own record of the previous period's state, with **zero lag**. The
  candidate is the register's cleanest G-13 case: there is no filing calendar, no revision and no
  publication-versus-decision ordering question at all.
- **G-12 — the conditioning changes the sign or the existence of the effect: PASS, on the sign
  limb.** The claim is not that the funding rate predicts a *bigger* move — it is that the funding
  rate identifies which side of the book is crowded and therefore which way the basis should
  revert: "the side opposite the funding-rate crowd". High funding means crowded longs, so the
  expected basis move is *down*; low funding means crowded shorts, so it is *up*. The level of the
  state therefore determines the **sign** of the position, which is exactly what G-12 asks for. The
  published funding-rate arbitrage (S-086, S-087) is a baseline for the *mechanism*; the candidate's
  residual is the sign-keyed reversal specification, not the arbitrage.
- **Effect on the dossier: no score change (63.0).** Both tests pass. The unchanged warnings are
  the momentum confound (the candidate must separate reversal from continuation, and the published
  work shows past returns already predict basis deviations with a high explanatory share — S-086),
  the venue-transferability condition, and crypto venue risk. The screen removes none of them, and
  removes nothing from the evidence base either.

**Cycle 13 screen (G-13 publication timing; G-12 sign or existence) — novelty reduced.**
- **G-13 — PASS, unchanged and still the register's cleanest case.** The funding rate is published
  by the venue for each funding period; the settled rate is the venue's own record with zero lag.
- **G-12 — PASS on the sign limb, unchanged in form.** The claim that the *level* of the funding rate
  determines the *sign* of the position ("the side opposite the funding-rate crowd") is untouched.
- **The novelty claim, however, is reduced.** The mechanism the candidate sells — that the funding
  rate identifies a crowded leveraged side, that the crowd unwinds, and that the basis therefore
  mean-reverts — is now published *outside* the register by independent work: perpetual-futures
  drawdowns **quickly mean-revert** while quarterly drawdowns persist, a higher funding rate
  *attenuates* the perpetual price's sensitivity to demand shocks, and the funding rate's
  information content is attributed to imbalanced leveraged positions that are vulnerable to
  subsequent unwinding (S-158, Gornall, Rinaldi & Xiao, working paper — the author order and the
  title were corrected in Cycle 102; the current title is "Perpetual Futures and Basis Risk:
  Evidence from Cryptocurrency" and an earlier version was circulated as "Funding Payments
  Crisis-Proofed Bitcoin's Perpetual Futures"; S-159, *Journal of Empirical
  Finance* 2026). What remains unclaimed is narrower than the dossier assumed: a *dated, signed,
  exit-specified* rule keyed to the funding **level** rather than to a basis deviation. That is a
  formulation difference, not a mechanism difference, and the register scores it as such.
- **Effect on the dossier: re-scored 63.0 → 60.0 (novelty 3 → 2).** The G-12 and G-13 screens both
  still pass; the score falls because the family's mechanism and mean-reversion result are now
  published (S-158, S-159), not because the candidate was mis-specified. The two unchanged warnings
  — the momentum confound and crypto venue risk — are unchanged and remain the binding constraints.
  Rank falls from 6 to 5, and the candidate now ties IDEA-014 at 60.0 (broken in IDEA-009's favour
  on falsifiability, 4 vs 3).

#### Cycles 41–51 consolidated note — IDEA-009 · no source bearing on this dossier; the momentum confound remains the binding constraint
- Across Cycles 41–51 the register executed the family screens **R-021 … R-031**, the **G-29** execution and
  **G-30** exhaustion (IDEA-010), and the register-wide post-2010 evidence-currency passes. **None of
  these produced a source bearing on IDEA-009.** The dossier's published-neighbour inventory — S-158
  (perpetual drawdowns mean-revert, funding attenuates price sensitivity to demand shocks) and S-159
  (*Journal of Empirical Finance* 2026: funding rates have incremental forecasting power for spot
  volatility because they capture imbalanced leveraged positions vulnerable to subsequent unwinding) —
  therefore **remains the complete inventory**. The candidate's two unchanged warnings — the **momentum
  confound** (the candidate must separate reversal from continuation, since published work shows past
  returns already predict basis deviations) and **crypto venue risk** (the funding rate's venue-specific
  construction and transferability) — are unchanged and remain the binding constraints on the candidate.
- **Score: 60.0, unchanged.** Rank **4 of 5** in the active set (behind IDEA-011 and IDEA-004 at 69.0 and
  IDEA-010 at 68.0, ahead of IDEA-014 at 52.0).

#### Cycle 52 block — IDEA-009 · no source found; the candidate is unchanged, and the register records *why* it is not being worked on
- **What Cycle 52 did for this candidate.** A bounded check of the dossier's published-neighbour inventory
  for 2025–2026 work on perpetual-futures funding rates, drawdown mean-reversion and the leveraged-unwind
  channel. **Nothing that bears on this candidate was found.** The Cycle-52 corpus (S-252 … S-266) is
  entirely IFRS-adoption literature (S-252 … S-255, S-265) and venue-halt / circuit-breaker literature
  (S-256 … S-264, S-266); none of it touches funding rates, drawdowns or crypto venue structure. The
  Cycle-52 check therefore **sharpened the null without changing it**: the two published neighbours
  (S-158, S-159) remain the whole of the register's inventory for this candidate, and the candidate's
  residual — a **dated, signed, exit-specified rule keyed to the funding *level*** rather than to a basis
  deviation — remains unclaimed by construction rather than by search.
- **What the R-032 family screen contributes to this dossier — and it is a *cost-model* contribution,
  not a mechanism one.** The R-032 screen produced published, verified microstructure facts about the
  tradeable window around a published venue event: realised volatility **194 % higher annualised in the
  first minute** after a market-wide level-1 halt and elevated for over ten minutes, **spreads wider for
  about five minutes**, and volume up 116 % in the first minute (S-266); and, for the individual-name
  variant, that LULD halts concentrate in exchange-traded products and small capitalisations, with 84 %
  of halted ETPs small-capitalisation and only 8 S&P 500 constituents halting on the SEC's own statistics
  for 24 August 2015 (S-259). IDEA-009's decision window is **not** a halt window — it is a funding-period
  window of hours — so none of this changes the candidate. But it does supply a **transferable cost-model
  fact** for the register's broader use: *published venue-interruption events concentrate the tradeable
  opportunity in the least liquid names, at the widest spreads, at the highest realised volatility*. A
  validator testing a crypto-perpetual reversal rule in a funding window will be asked, separately, about
  venue concentration and about cost at the decision point; that question is now answerable from
  published sources rather than from assumption.
- **Score: 60.0, unchanged.** No source bearing on any score component. The novelty dimension remains
  at 2 (published neighbours S-158, S-159); falsifiability remains at 4; the two warnings are unchanged.

#### Cycle 66 block — IDEA-009 · prior-art re-read against literature the register had not yet seen; re-scored 60.0 → 53.0; reclassified as an incremental variant of published conditional funding-reversal work; V-008 amended

- **What Cycle 66 did for this candidate.** The register's own named highest-value act — *re-read an existing
  candidate against literature it had not yet seen* — applied to the one active candidate whose published-neighbour
  inventory was still the thinnest (**S-158, S-159 only**, unchanged since Cycle 13, with the Cycle-52 check and
  the Cycles 41–51 passes confirming that no source bore on the candidate). Thirteen new sources were registered
  (**S-310 … S-322**); five were fetched directly and text-extracted (S-315, S-316, S-319, S-320 and the venue
  rulebook S-321), and the remainder are Crossref-verified with index-rendered abstracts, because SSRN, MDPI and
  ScienceDirect refused automated access (HTTP 403) in this cycle.
- **The finding that changes the candidate — the candidate's own prediction now has published direct tests, and
  they do not support the unconditional form.**
  - **S-310 (Shek 2026, SSRN preprint, author-reported, not peer-reviewed)** — 274 funding-rate extreme events
    across BTC, ETH and SOL, 2021–2026: events on **up days revert by +1.88 % over the next 24 hours**, while
    events on **down days continue by −1.74 %**; the +3.62 pp gap is reported significant (block-bootstrap 95 % CI
    [2.60, 4.67]) and stable around seven exogenous market-wide shocks (sign test p = 0.00781; pooled +6.47 pp,
    95 % CI [4.45, 8.86]), consistent across 12 year-by-direction cells and 13 coins. **The published reversal is
    conditional on the direction of the market move; on down days the extreme funding rate is followed by
    continuation, not reversal.** The published conditional form is *stronger* than the candidate's formulation,
    and it proposes "conditional arbitrage capacity" as a state variable in the limits-to-arbitrage framework.
  - **S-320 (Das 2023, Brave New Coin — industry research, not peer-reviewed)** — the regression of the next
    period's price on the funding rate published at T0, on BitMEX data from 2 November 2022 to 22 February 2023,
    gives **slope −0.668, R² = 0.00109, F = 0.388 on 357 degrees of freedom**, with the index-price variant at
    **R² = 0.0168**. The author's conclusion: no strong general relationship, **but "in the most extreme cases when
    funding payments are set to be high, there are market reversals"** — and mean reversion "did occur but not
    immediately after a high negative funding rate rounds". This is a near-null for the general form and support
    confined to the extreme tail, which is exactly the candidate's decile-extreme design.
- **The second finding — the exit rule is not the published structure of the correction.** **S-311 (Shek 2026b,
  SSRN preprint)**: across 1,696 events on 18 Binance perpetuals, the speed of parity restoration after a
  funding-rate extreme is **dominated by realised volatility** — median **27.0 hours** to correct in
  high-volatility events against **3.0 hours** in low-volatility ones (log-rank χ² = 147.25, p < 0.0001; HR =
  0.5935 for a one-standard-deviation rise in realised volatility; correction time spanning 11.53–122.62 hours),
  present both before and after the FTX collapse. The candidate's frozen 1–3 funding-period exit (8–24 hours) is
  therefore a rule that works in low-volatility episodes and systematically misses in high-volatility ones.
- **The third finding — the state is degenerate by construction over a published interval.** The funding rate is
  not an instantaneous reading of the crowd: it is a **clamped, path-dependent** function of the premium. S-086
  gives Binance's published clamp (κ = 1, γ = 0.05 %, ι = 0.01 %); **S-315** establishes that in most exchanges
  the funding fee is the **average of the past eight hours' premium** rather than the current spot price, so the
  realised rate is inherently path-dependent; **S-317** shows that persistent futures-spot discrepancies
  **cannot be explained by transaction fees alone** and that the **clamping function** is a key driver, with
  model-free no-arbitrage bounds; **S-318** shows the futures-to-spot ratio is **bimodal at higher frequencies,
  explained by the inaction region induced by the clamp**, and that the relation between clamping strength and
  funding-rate volatility is **non-monotonic**; and **S-321** (dYdX's own rulebook) publishes the eight-hour
  **cap** (600 % × (initial margin − maintenance margin); 12 % for BTC-USD) and shows that governance can change
  the clamp factor, the cap, the sampling interval and the impact notional. Two consequences: the candidate's
  "top/bottom decile of its own trailing distribution" is a **construction contaminated by the clamp**, and — since
  clamping strength does not move funding-rate volatility monotonically — a percentile-based state is not stable
  even in principle.
- **Supporting and contextual evidence now on file.** **S-312** (systematic review, peer-reviewed journal version):
  the family carries a PRISMA-aligned review covering January 2021 – April 2026 (22 substantive studies — 15
  peer-reviewed + 7 working papers — of which 13 are direct perpetual-futures studies and 6 adjacent behavioural
  studies, the remaining three being the review's own methodological framing; *the "(24 studies)" figure that
  stood here until Cycle 85 was the superseded Cycle-66 count, corrected in Cycle 67 and corrected here*), whose most developed
  streams are pricing/no-arbitrage and leverage/liquidation, in which direct evidence on classic behavioural
  constructs remains sparse, and whose named frontier is funding-rule design, spot spillovers, stress transmission
  and exchange-architecture comparison. **S-314** (NBER WP 30796): the funding rate is used as a market-wide proxy
  for speculative demand (R² = 0.56 against stablecoin lending rates) and is **not** principally exchange-specific
  (Binance and FTX funding rates reported similar and highly correlated) — the strongest *supporting* evidence the
  venue-transferability condition has received, and simultaneously a sharpening of the momentum confound.
  **S-313** (peer-reviewed, MDPI): across 9.1 million hourly funding-rate observations on 26 exchanges and 812
  symbols, **spreads peak approximately two hours after standard settlement times** (a cost-model fact for the
  candidate's decision window), the market evolves through **gradual drift with no discrete regime shifts** (a
  caution against discrete regime labels), and IGARCH volatility behaviour appears in only 24.5 % of windows.
  **S-315** and **S-316** fix the published no-arbitrage benchmark for the deviation the candidate trades.
  **S-319** (Jansen, *Machine Learning for Trading* case study — book, not peer-reviewed) is the nearest published
  out-of-sample failure of the premise: "Validation Sharpe +0.80 collapses to -1.17 in holdout (-247 % decay).
  The 2024-2025 crypto bull run reversed the cross-sectional funding patterns. Verdict: Reframe — viable only
  with explicit regime detection." **S-322** is a peer-reviewed cost-side reference on order-flow toxicity and
  price-impact persistence in crypto perpetual futures (title-level citation only; abstract not retrieved, and no
  number from it is used anywhere).
- **Reclassification.** Following the Cycle-30 precedent set for IDEA-003, the candidate is **reclassified as an
  incremental variant of published conditional funding-reversal work** and not as a discovery of a funding-level
  reversal. What remains unclaimed after this re-read is a **formulation difference only** — a dated, single-asset,
  decile-keyed implementation of a rule whose economic content is published and whose sign is published as
  *conditional*.
- **What survives.** The candidate's *state* remains the register's cleanest G-13 case (published by the venue,
  zero lag, no filing calendar and no revision question), and **G-12 still passes on the sign limb in the
  direction-conditioned form**, because the published evidence says the sign of the basis move after a funding
  extreme depends on whether the market moved with or against the crowd (S-310) — and that is a decision-time
  observable (the sign of the preceding price move).
- **Score: 60.0 → 53.0** (mechanism 4 → 3; evidence 2 → 3; novelty 2 → 1; falsifiability 4 → 3; resilience 2
  unchanged; data 4 unchanged). *Mechanism falls* because the candidate's central unconditional sign claim is
  contradicted in the published evidence for one of its two directions — the Cycle-32 IDEA-003 precedent applies.
  *Evidence rises* because the family's evidence base is no longer thin: it now carries two direct tests, a
  systematic review, peer-reviewed microstructure work and the published pricing record, and **contradictory
  evidence is recorded rather than absent** — a better-grounded candidate is a higher research priority even when
  the evidence is unfavourable. *Novelty falls to its floor* because the residual is now a formulation difference
  from published work in a stronger form. *Falsifiability falls* because one of the five predictions is now
  largely pre-answered by published tests and the exit rule must be re-specified conditionally. **Resilience and
  data availability are unchanged.** The two unchanged warnings — the **momentum confound** and **crypto venue
  risk** — are now sharper rather than weaker: the momentum confound has become the **published** conditioning
  variable (S-310), and the published out-of-sample failure of the premise is a **regime** failure (S-319).
- **V-008 was amended, not re-created.** The amendment makes the direction-conditioned specification primary,
  pre-registers that a null on the *unconditional* form is **not** a kill (the Cycle-30 / V-003 convention
  precedent), conditions the exit horizon on realised volatility, requires the published tests (S-310, S-319,
  S-320) to be used as external benchmarks, and adds the published spread-timing cost fact (S-313) and the clamp
  filter (S-317, S-318, S-321) to the state definition.
- **No gap is opened.** The candidate's remaining question — does the direction-conditioned,
  volatility-conditioned, published-state rule survive costs out of sample, and does it add anything to the
  published funding arbitrage — is a **validation** question, not a search question, and the amended V-008 is its
  vehicle. **No `G-` identifier was consumed.**
- **Score: 53.0, rank 4 of 5** (behind IDEA-011 and IDEA-004 at 69.0 and IDEA-010 at 68.0, ahead of IDEA-014 at
  52.0). The ranking order is unchanged.

### Cycle 109 — P-5 audit: is the funding state published, decision-time and free? (state PASSES; the candidate's binding constraint is elsewhere)

- **The question, pre-registered.** R-056's three-limb form: (A) does the venue publish the state itself,
  its clamp and its components? (B) is the value the candidate conditions on **fixed before** the trade
  decision? (C) is the state **free** — computable at no cost from public information, and not already
  published as a tradeable result?
- **Limb A — POSITIVE.** The venue publishes the state, its **cap** and its **components**: **S-321** (the
  dYdX funding rulebook) publishes the premium component (impact bid and impact ask against the index
  price; impact notional set as 500 USDC divided by the initial-margin fraction; the premium a
  time-weighted average of **60 one-minute premiums over the preceding hour**), the funding rate (the
  premium component divided by eight plus a fixed interest-rate component), the **hourly** settlement, the
  **cap** ("The 8-hour rate cap is calculated by 600 % * (Initial Margin - Maintenance Margin)", with the
  tier table, so the BTC-USD eight-hour rate is capped at 600 % × (5 % − 3 %) = 12 %), and the fact that
  **governance can adjust** the funding parameters. The register's Cycle-66 qualifications — the state is
  **clamped** and **path-dependent** — are therefore **published quantities**, not hidden ones, and the
  candidate's state definition in the dossier already matches them.
- **Limb B — POSITIVE, with the construction separated out.** The rate for the coming settlement is
  computed from a **preceding** window of published premium data and is therefore **fixed before the
  trade**; the clamp is a published function of the margin tier; and the *percentile* the candidate uses is
  computable at decision time from the venue's published history — but it is a **transformation**, not a
  published series, and the register's own rule treats a constructed state as failing the "published
  state" test. The audit's added precision: **the state is decision-time; the candidate's *conditioning
  variable* is a construction of a decision-time state.**
- **Limb C — PARTIAL, and the candidate's binding constraint is named.** The funding rate is published at
  no cost, but the **unconditional** funding-level reversal the candidate asserts is **published prior
  art**: **S-320** (the closest published test of the candidate's own prediction 1 — the regression of the
  log price ratio on the funding rate published at T0 gives **slope −0.668, R² = 0.00109, F = 0.388 on 357
  degrees of freedom**, a near-null for the general form, with reversals reported **only at the extremes**)
  and **S-310** (the nearest published test, on 274 funding-rate extreme events across BTC, ETH and SOL
  from 2021 to 2026: events on **up days** revert by **+1.88 %** over the next 24 hours while events on
  **down days** continue by **−1.74 %**, a **+3.62 pp** gap reported significant — i.e. the reversal is
  **conditional on market direction**, and the published conditional form is stronger than the candidate's
  own unconditional formulation).
- **Effect on the candidate and on the artifacts.** **No score change (53.0), no rank change, no hard-stop
  flag change, no gap opened or closed, no re-screen triggered, and V-008 is unchanged.** The audit
  **narrows the register's own framing**: state availability is **not** what binds IDEA-009. What binds it
  is the **unconditional** sign claim set against a published **conditional** result, plus the
  already-recorded venue-transferability and momentum-confound qualifications. Any future specification
  must therefore confront S-310's conditional form directly rather than argue about the state.
- **What would change the verdict.** A published test of the **unconditional** form on a venue other than
  the two already covered, or a published result establishing that the **conditional** form's edge is
  absent. This audit searched for neither; it is a validation-design question, not a search question.

---

#### Cycle 112 block — IDEA-009 · the Cycle-109 P-5 screen returned an adverse verdict: re-scored 53.0 → 42.0; two hard-stop conditions attached; **retained, not parked**; V-008 amended; gap G-37 opened

- **What the screen was.** The Cycle-109 P-5 audit named the only two things that would change this
  candidate's verdict, and Cycle 112 executed them as a pre-registered screen (pre-registered in
  `cycle_notes.md` before the first fetch): **(A)** is there a **published test of the *unconditional*
  funding-level reversal on a venue other than Binance or BitMEX** (the venues of S-310 and S-320)? and
  **(B)** is there a **published result establishing that the *conditional* form's edge is absent**, or
  that fails out of sample, beyond the book-chapter failure already on file as S-319? Routes: arXiv and
  general web searches for 2025–2026 work on the funding level as a reversal or crowding predictor; SSRN and
  Crossref searches for an unconditional funding-level test on a named third venue (Bybit, OKX, Deribit,
  Coinbase); peer-reviewed out-of-sample searches on the direction-conditioned reversal. The falsification
  rule was fixed in advance: a new source would move a score **only** if it bore on the *sign* claim or on
  *out-of-sample failure*; a source that supported only the mechanism would leave the score untouched.
- **What was found — four new entries, three of them load-bearing.**
  - **S-390** (Memon, Anklesaria-Dalal & Mishra 2026, *Advanced International Journal of Multidisciplinary
    Research* 4(4), DOI 10.62127/aijmr.2026.v04i04.1493; the article page was **fetched directly** and the
    abstract read verbatim) is an **independent, published test of the candidate's own state
    construction**: "Extreme funding is classified ex ante using the current funding rate's **mid-rank
    percentile within the preceding 180-day distribution**, with the lower and upper deciles defining extreme
    negative and extreme positive states" — Binance BTCUSDT, 10 September 2019 to 10 August 2026, 15-minute
    spot sampling, horizons from 15 minutes to 24 hours, controlled for the four-hour pre-settlement return,
    with parametric, non-parametric and directional tests plus heteroskedasticity- and
    autocorrelation-consistent regressions. The result is **asymmetric**: extreme negative funding is
    followed by an average 24-hour return of **+0.506 %** (significant under t, Wilcoxon and
    directional-frequency tests, robust to false-discovery-rate adjustment; about **0.491 percentage points**
    in the categorical HAC regressions after the pre-settlement control), while "**extreme positive funding
    does not exhibit a comparable 24-hour effect**".
  - **S-389** (`OctopusTakopi/funding-rate-alpha`, a public practitioner note whose README was **fetched
    directly** and read) supplies the largest sample located for the family: a panel of **791 contracts
    including delisted ones, 2.43 million funding settlements, extended to 6.1 million settlements across
    three venues**, from 2020 to mid-2026. Its next-24-hour information coefficient for the **raw funding
    level** runs **−3.9 % (2020), −3.1 % (2021), +0.0 % (2022), +0.6 % (2023), +1.6 % (2024, t = 3.4),
    +3.4 % (2025, t = 8.1), +2.0 % (2026, t = 3.6)** — a *reversal* regime over 2020–21 and a
    *continuation* regime from 2023, the current regime's coefficients being positive and significant — and
    the note's own summary is "**No unconditional level signal survives this flip.**" Three further
    findings bear directly on this dossier. (i) **The candidate's own construction is reported null**: the
    90-day rolling z-score, cross-sectionally ranked, "**predicts nothing at any horizon, +0.00 % at one
    hour**", with a 24-hour information coefficient of **−0.0007 (t = −0.3)** — "the preprocessing removes
    the baseline without creating predictability". (ii) **The crowding reading is contradicted**: "the
    cross-sectional level of funding measures how **retail-oriented** a contract is rather than how
    **crowded** its current position structure is"; the within-day rank correlation of the rate with 7-day
    volatility is **−0.013**; and "**open interest builds into and through high-funding settlements**, which
    refutes a flow reading and leaves the rate as a measurement of the basis" — the rank correlation with
    the open-interest change is **+1.73 % (t = 9.90)** over the hour ending at the settlement and **+1.12 %
    (t = 7.07)** over the hour beginning at it, i.e. "positions are **opened into** the payment, not unwound
    before it". (iii) **The cost floor bites at the candidate's horizon**: the pooled effect is **+1.89 % at
    one hour (t = 6.26)**, +1.04 % at eight hours and **+0.23 % at twenty-four hours (t = 0.79)** — "only by
    too little to trade" — and the one-hour decile spread of **3.66 bps** sits below the note's **8 bps**
    reference round trip; the note's own tradeable residue is a *carry* book whose excess over the round
    trip is "indistinguishable from zero (**+2.2 bps, t = 0.68** at a day)". Separately, and as a standing
    warning for any future work in this family, the note reports that a **different, venue-published state
    does better**: the **top-trader long/short account ratio** has an information coefficient of **+0.013
    (t = 4.7)** with a *continuation* sign and nets **+4.2 bps/day at a Sharpe of 0.82**, against the funding
    level's +2.8 (Sharpe 0.51) and the z-score's +1.4 (0.27).
  - **S-391** (BitMEX Blog, 12 October 2017, Arthur Hayes, "XBTUSD Funding Mean Reversion Strategy") is the
    **rediscovery guard**: the venue itself published this rule nine years before the candidate was written.
    The rule as the venue stated it: "**A large negative funding rate predicts a positive return for the next
    8 hour period. A large positive funding rate predicts a negative return for the next 8 hour period.**"
    and "At each funding timestamp, if the funding rate is above or below your limit, place the counter trend
    trade. Immediately after the next funding timestamp, close your XBTUSD position." Its published table over
    524 funding periods reports per-cell success rates of **47.62 %, 53.23 %, 45.68 % and 44.44 %** — below
    50 % in three of the four cells — and the author attributes most of the result to the funding received
    rather than to the price move.
  - **S-392** (`DavidAyusoPita/funding-carry-analysis`, **fetched directly** and read) supplies the cost and
    decay anchors for the family: a round trip costs **13 bps** and needs **100–135 hours** of holding to
    repay itself; the active threshold rule finishes at **−9 % to −15 % annualised after costs**; the
    exchange-versus-DEX funding spread "averaged **20–35 % less in the second year** of the sample than the
    first, and sits **45–60 % below its late-2024 peak**"; and, bearing on this dossier's venue-transferability
    condition, "**OKX publishes only three months of history**".
- **Verdict on the two pre-registered questions.** **(A) is not satisfied.** No published test of the
  unconditional form on a named third venue was located: S-390 is Binance — the venue of S-310 and S-320 —
  and the portion of S-389 read does not identify the three venues in its extended sample, so the register
  does not credit it as a third-venue test. **(B) is not satisfied either**, in the strict form: no source
  was found that establishes the conditional form's edge to be absent. But the *adjacent* evidence is
  adverse, and the register records it as adverse rather than as a refutation: the unconditional level's
  relation to returns is regime-dependent and sign-inverted in the current regime (S-389); the candidate's
  own percentile construction is reported null (S-389); and an independent 2026 test of that same
  construction finds one leg of the candidate's rule unsupported (S-390).
- **Score change: 53.0 → 42.0** (mechanism **3 → 2**; evidence **3 → 3**; novelty **1 → 1**; falsifiability
  **3 → 2**; resilience **2 → 1**; data **4 → 4**). The justification for each cell, in the register's
  format:
  - **Mechanism 3 → 2.** The dossier's causal chain is *funding rate → crowded leveraged position → forced
    unwind → basis mean-reversion*. Two of its three links are now contradicted. The **crowd** link: S-389
    finds the level "measures how retail-oriented a contract is rather than how crowded its current position
    structure is" (rank correlation with 7-day volatility **−0.013**) and, decisively for this mechanism,
    that "**open interest builds into and through high-funding settlements** … positions are opened into the
    payment, not unwound before it" (+1.73 %, t = 9.90 into the settlement hour). A mechanism that requires
    the crowd to *unwind* before the payment is not what the funding rate is measuring on this sample. The
    **direction** link: S-389's information coefficients change sign between a 2020–21 reversal regime and a
    2023–26 continuation regime, and S-390 finds the within-asset effect present only in the
    negative-funding tail. The *transfer* caveat is recorded honestly: S-389's design is cross-sectional and
    the candidate's rule is time-series within one underlying, so the regime finding is **suggestive, not
    decisive**; the *mechanism* finding (positions opened into the payment, not unwound before it) transfers
    across designs because it is a statement about what the funding rate measures, not about a return
    estimand. Not 1, because the state remains published, zero-lag and free, and the funding mechanism is
    still documented by the venues and by S-086 and S-087.
  - **Evidence 3 → 3, unchanged.** The base is no longer thin, and contradictory evidence is recorded rather
    than absent — the Cycle-66 precedent, which *raised* this cell for exactly that reason. But the new
    material does not raise the cell's **quality**: S-389 and S-392 are self-published practitioner notes with
    author-reported results, S-390 is a journal article in a fee-charging open-access venue whose standing
    this register cannot vouch for, and S-391 is an interested party's blog post whose body the register
    holds only in index-rendered form. Volume rose; standing did not.
  - **Novelty 1 → 1, unchanged, and the floor is now documented from the venue's own mouth.** The residual
    was already a formulation difference (S-310, S-320); S-391 shows the venue published the same rule in
    2017 with sub-50 % success rates in three of four cells, and S-390 shows a 2026 paper testing the
    candidate's exact percentile construction. The cell cannot go below 1.
  - **Falsifiability 3 → 2.** Three of the dossier's five pre-registered predictions are now largely
    pre-answered by published tests: **P1** (the reversal) is pre-answered with an asymmetric answer
    (S-390) and a regime split (S-389); **P2** (the state scales with the funding level) is pre-answered
    against the *candidate's own* percentile construction (S-389: z-score 24-hour information coefficient
    −0.0007, t = −0.3; "+0.00 % at one hour"), with only the raw level carrying an effect at horizons of
    eight hours and less; **P5** (the effect exceeds the round-trip cost) is pre-answered against at the
    candidate's horizon (S-389: the one-hour decile spread of 3.66 bps against an 8 bps reference round trip,
    and the carry-plus-reversal book's excess over the round trip indistinguishable from zero at +2.2 bps,
    t = 0.68; S-392: 13 bps per round trip and 100–135 hours to repay). The Cycle-66 precedent applies
    directly — pre-answered predictions lower the cell and force a re-specification of the exit rule.
  - **Resilience 2 → 1.** The dossier's own persistence thesis says the mechanism decays "if the funding-rate
    crowd becomes sophisticated enough to pre-position against reversals". That named channel has fired, and
    the evidence is sign-inverted in the *current* regime rather than merely weaker: the 2024, 2025 and 2026
    information coefficients are positive and significant (t = 3.4, 8.1, 3.6), and S-389's 2024–26 window
    reports the level's one-hour effect at **+4.10 %**. Capacity is binding as well (S-389: raising the
    liquidity floor removes the price leg rather than scaling it), and what survives at scale is the *carry*,
    which is a published baseline (S-086, S-087, S-392) rather than this candidate's edge.
  - **Data 4 → 4, unchanged.** The state remains published by the venue before the decision, free, and
    available with zero lag; S-392's note that "**OKX publishes only three months of history**" is a
    *venue-transferability* limitation that the dossier already carries as a condition, not a change to the
    state's availability at the candidate's home venue.
- **Hard-stop conditions now attached** (the register's standing conditions, each independently of the
  score): (i) **costs likely to overwhelm the hypothesized effect** — S-389 and S-392 together; (ii) **a
  claim that merely repackages a known idea** — S-391, S-390 and S-389; (iii) the pre-existing
  **momentum-confound condition**, now sharpened rather than replaced: the funding signal's sign tracks the
  prevailing regime (reversal vs continuation), which is a momentum/regime confound rather than a
  reversal; (iv) the pre-existing **crypto venue risk** condition, unchanged.
- **Retained, not parked.** The candidate is **not** rejected and **not** parked (P-015 was not minted): its
  state still satisfies G-13 cleanly, its specification is still executable, and what remains is a
  *validation* question with a decisive possible answer — whether the direction-conditioned, volatility-
  conditioned, cost-tested rule survives on a single underlying. What this cycle changed is the register's
  estimate of that probability, and its estimate is now much lower. The register records, plainly, that the
  candidate's live content has narrowed to a **time-series form** of a **published** effect whose
  out-of-sample and cost-adjusted magnitude is not established, and whose sign has been observed to invert
  across regimes.
- **V-008 is amended, not re-created.** The Cycle-66 amendment had made the direction-conditioned and
  volatility-conditioned rule primary. Cycle 112 adds three requirements, each traceable to a specific
  finding above: (i) a **regime gate** — the rule may only be armed when the *within-contract* trailing
  sign of the funding–return relation over the trailing window is negative, so the trade is not taken into a
  continuation regime (S-389); (ii) a **cost-floor arm** — the specification must report the realised
  gross spread of the decile contrast against the published **8 bps** reference round trip (S-389) and the
  **13 bps** cross-venue round trip (S-392), and must treat an excess indistinguishable from zero as a null
  result rather than as a small edge; and (iii) a **construction arm** — the percentile rule and the raw-level
  rule must be tested as **separate arms**, because S-389 reports the percentile construction as null and the
  level as carrying the effect only at horizons of eight hours and less. The amended specification remains
  executable.
- **New gap G-37 (opened).** The precise, open question the new evidence creates is the *transfer* question:
  does the within-contract, time-series form of the funding–return relation also flip sign between a reversal
  regime and a continuation regime, which regime is the current one, and can the flip be detected before a
  trade is taken? G-37 is a literature-side question with a bounded, decidable form; it is not a re-run of
  the Cycle-66 corpora, which tested the unconditional level on Binance and BitMEX.
- **What would change this verdict (dated re-screen conditions).** A peer-reviewed replication of the
  2020–21-to-2023 sign flip in the within-contract time-series form; a published estimate of a
  direction-conditioned reversal's *net-of-cost* magnitude on a single perpetual; a published measure of
  the funding rate's relationship to **open interest** that would restore or kill the crowd-unwind link
  specifically; or a published account of the top-trader ratio's edge that explains the funding level's
  inferiority. Absent one of these, the candidate stays at 42.0 with its two new hard-stop conditions and
  V-008 executable but regime-gated.

#### Cycle 113 block — IDEA-009 · gap G-37 is **partially answered**, and the answer to its third limb is **adverse to the candidate's own validation plan**; **no score change (42.0)**; a new hard-stop condition is attached and **V-008 is amended**

- **What was executed.** The Cycle-112 gap **G-37** asked three things: (i) does the *within-contract,
  time-series* form of the funding–return relation also flip sign between a reversal regime and a
  continuation regime; (ii) which regime is current; and (iii) can the flip be **detected before a trade is
  taken**. The pre-registered route was "the sources behind S-389 and S-390 (read for their regime
  definitions and their per-year signs), and any published 2025–2026 work on funding-rate regime
  classification". This cycle re-fetched **S-389's source document in full** — the Cycle-112 entry had read
  it "from the abstract through Section 14", and the retrieval tool had truncated the final ~9 KB, which
  therefore **had never been read** — and read it to the end, including Section 15 (Limitations) and Section
  16 (Conclusion). The ledger entry is **upgraded** accordingly (S-389, Cycle-113 upgrade note), and three
  additions and one correction are recorded there.
- **Limb (ii) — which regime is current: ANSWERED, and it is continuation, not reversal.** The document's
  own split table gives 2020–2023 versus 2024–2026: one hour **+0.33 % / +4.10 %**, two hours **−0.30 % /
  +3.61 %**, four hours **−0.30 % / +3.05 %**, eight hours **−0.29 % / +2.93 %**, twenty-four hours
  **−1.29 % / +2.39 %**; the 2020–2023 window is "59 % of the sample" and has "no effect at any horizon except
  a significantly negative one at a day (t = −3.0)", while 2024–2026 is significant at every horizon
  (twenty-four hours, t = 8.8). **The candidate's own construction flips with it**, and the yearly
  information coefficients the Cycle-112 block did not record are the sharpest new number in the cycle: the
  **90-day z-score** row runs **−2.3, −3.2, −0.3, +0.8, +1.1, +2.1, +0.5 %** for 2020–2026 with t = **−1.5,
  −5.1, −0.4, +1.4, +2.4, +4.5, +0.8**, and the **raw level at one hour** row runs **−1.1, +1.0, +2.0, −1.3,
  +2.2, +5.2, +5.9 %** (t = −0.7, +1.7, +2.6, −1.9, +3.8, **+12.7**, **+7.2**). So in the current year the
  candidate's own state is **statistically indistinguishable from zero** (t = 0.8) — not weakly negative,
  not negative.
- **Limb (iii) — can the flip be detected before a trade is taken: ANSWERED, AND ADVERSELY. This is the
  cycle's most decision-relevant finding for this dossier.** The regime *is* partly predictable ex ante, and
  the document says so: "One thing genuinely is predictable: the regime. Regressing each day's coefficient on
  its own trailing average, with a further day of embargo so that no trailing observation overlaps the
  outcome, gives a slope of **+0.65 to +0.78** across 63, 126 and 252-day windows at **t = 5.7 to 6.6**, and
  the trailing sign matches the next day's **54.7 % to 56.3 %** of the time. **Knowing the regime is not the
  same as profiting from it:** a strategy trading the sign of its own trailing coefficient earns **+1.9 to
  +2.8 bps per day** more than always shorting high funding, but at **t = 0.64 to 0.88** and a block-bootstrap
  **p of 0.19 to 0.29** that gain is **not distinguishable from zero**." Read against V-008's Cycle-112
  amendment — which required a **regime gate** so that the rule is armed only when the *within-contract*
  trailing sign of the funding–return relation is negative — this is the nearest published measurement of
  exactly that gate, and it says the gate is (a) **accurate only barely above a coin flip** (54.7 %–56.3 %
  sign match, on the cross-sectional analogue) and (b) **worth nothing above noise** when traded. The
  gate cannot carry the rule, and the candidate's remaining live content is correspondingly smaller.
- **Limb (i) — the within-contract, time-series form: STILL OPEN, and the correction matters.** A full-text
  search of the retrieved document finds exactly **one** occurrence of the phrase "time-series form", and it
  is about **cross-venue dispersion**, not the funding level: "The time-series form fares no better:
  market-wide disagreement carries a correlation of +0.02 (t = 1.0) with the next day's return, and the
  high-minus-low-disagreement day difference is +27 bps at t = 0.68." The document's regime series is a
  **cross-sectional** daily information coefficient (contracts sorted within a timestamp) rolled over time.
  So the transfer question G-37 asks is **not** answered by the one document that would have answered it, and
  **G-37 remains open on its first limb**. Two 2025–2026 funding-regime-classification items were located
  this cycle but are **not registered, not fetched, and carry no claim**; they are recorded as a re-screen
  route, not as evidence.
- **No score change, and the reason is recorded rather than assumed.** The Cycle-113 pre-registration
  registered **no score-move rule for Priority 3**, and the finding bears on the *gate*, not on the
  candidate's premise: resilience is already at the cell floor of **1**, and falsifiability was already
  lowered to **2** at Cycle 112 for the same class of reason (pre-answered predictions). The finding is
  therefore recorded as a **new hard-stop condition** and as an **amendment to V-008**, not as a cell move.
  This follows the register's own discipline — a cell moves only on a pre-registered falsification rule with a
  defined bar, and the Cycle-52/66/112 precedents are all applications of that discipline rather than
  exceptions to it.
- **New hard-stop condition attached** (standing, and independent of the score): **(v) the regime gate
  cannot carry the rule** — a rule that is armed only when a trailing sign flips is, on the nearest published
  measurement, accurate at 54.7 %–56.3 % and worth +1.9 to +2.8 bps per day at t = 0.64 to 0.88 (p = 0.19 to
  0.29); any specification that depends on the gate must therefore carry its value from something other
  than the gate, and a specification that depends on the gate alone is a null by construction.
- **V-008 is amended, not re-created.** The Cycle-112 amendment added a regime gate, a cost-floor arm and a
  construction arm. Cycle 113 adds a fourth requirement, traceable to the finding above: **(iv) a
  gate-value arm** — the specification must report the regime gate's *own* measured value and accuracy (the
  trailing-sign match rate and the gate's excess over the round trip) **separately** from the rule's, and must
  report a gate that cannot be distinguished from zero as a **null on the gated rule**, not as a smaller
  edge. The amended specification remains executable; the arm is a reporting requirement, not a new
  condition on the rule.
- **G-37's status.** **Open, narrowed to one limb.** Limbs (ii) and (iii) are answered, and limb (iii)'s
  answer is adverse; limb (i) — the within-contract, time-series form — has no published test located, and
  the document that would most plausibly have supplied one does not contain it. **No new gap is opened**, and
  the gap is not closed.

#### Cycle 125 block — IDEA-009 · gap G-37's remaining limb: the first *named, citable, published* instance of the proposition the limb asks about has now been found — and it is a **preprint about a different relation**, so the limb's condition is **still not met** and the gap stays open, with a **sharpened** re-screen condition

- **What was executed.** Priority 2, **G-37's remaining limb**: a bounded search for a **named, citable, published (peer-reviewed)** regime classifier establishing a **within-contract funding–return sign flip between reversal and continuation regimes**. The limb's condition includes **peer review**, so a preprint may answer the limb's *substance* but cannot satisfy its *condition*; the register records the distinction explicitly rather than blurring it.
- **What was found — S-454.** **Mesfin, M. (2026), "A Validated Volatility-Volume-Gap Classifier for Regime Identification in MNQ Intraday Data", arXiv:2605.11423 [q-fin.TR]** (v1 12 May 2026, v3 16 September 2026; the **abstract page was fetched directly, HTTP 200**, and the passages quoted are read in the page's own text; the **PDF body was not extracted**, so no body text is quoted). It is the **first named, citable, published instance of the general proposition this limb asks about**: a **within-contract** classifier — the **Volatility-Volume-Gap (VVG)** classifier on Micro E-mini Nasdaq-100 futures, built from "absolute overnight gap, absolute first-30-minute return, and first-bar volume relative to a 20-day rolling baseline", activated on "roughly 4.4% of sessions (40 days)" over "947 trading days of five-minute data from 2021-2025" — whose sign **flips between regimes**: "**2024 classifier days closed at mean +40.74 points while 2025 crashed to −42.48**". It also carries the register's **first published null on tradability of such a rule**: "**Eight directional configurations were tested. None passed.** Best result: T = 1.46, mean net +7.80 points, 127 OOS trades … Binding constraints are the 40-day sample (roughly 10 per year) and regime-dependent intraday behavior that no fixed rule survives across all test years."
- **Why the limb's condition is still NOT met — three separate failures, each on the register's own wording.**
  1. **Not peer-reviewed.** S-454 is an **arXiv preprint**; G-37's condition says "named, citable, published (**peer-reviewed**)".
  2. **Not a funding–return relation.** The relation is an **intraday price-path** relation; the limb asks about the **funding–return** relation.
  3. **Not the register's regime-transfer question.** The flipping variable is the day's **regime** (the VVG conditions), whereas limb (i) asks whether the **within-contract trailing sign of the funding–return relation** itself flips between regimes. Those are different objects.
- **What else the search re-surfaced, and what was done about it.** **S-310** (Shek, H. 2026, "Conditional Arbitrage Capacity: Evidence from Crypto Perpetual Funding Rates", SSRN 7520320) — the register's **nearest published test of the candidate's own hypothesis**, already on file and already the source of the published **direction-conditional funding-extreme reversal** baseline (**row 76**); **S-316** (Ackerer–Hugonnier–Jermann, *Mathematical Finance* 2026) and **S-086** (He–Kelly–Lu–Manela). **None was re-registered**: the register **reuses an identifier rather than duplicating a citation**, and S-310's content is now cross-referenced from the limb rather than re-quoted.
- **Effect on the candidate — stated in both directions, and it is small.** **No score change (42.0, rank 4 of 4 unchanged)**, and the reason is a rule, not a convenience: a cell moves on evidence about the **mechanism**, and S-454 is a **preprint**, on a **different contract**, about a **different relation**, with a **different state**. It bears on IDEA-009's **third hard-stop** — the regime gate — only as a **prior**: the nearest published analogue of "a named regime gate that flips a within-contract sign" now on file reports that **no directional rule survives it**, which *raises* the register's prior against a regime gate as a **rescue** for a within-contract sign flip. That is an **annotation on an existing hard-stop, not a new hard-stop**: the condition already on file ("the regime gate") is unchanged, and no fourth condition is created from a preprint.
- **G-37's status after this cycle: OPEN, and the re-screen condition is sharpened.** The condition now reads: *re-screen when (a) a **peer-reviewed** source establishes a **within-contract trailing-sign flip in the funding–return relation**, or (b) a **peer-reviewed** version of the S-454 classifier work is published and applied to a **funding state**.* The limb is **not** closed, and **no new gap is opened**; the reasons are independent of data availability.
- **V-008 is not amended.** The Cycle-113 gate-value arm already requires a validator to report the gate's own value and to report a gate indistinguishable from zero as a null on the gated rule. S-454 adds no reporting requirement that the arm does not already impose.

## IDEA-010 — CREDIT-ETF: corporate-bond ETF flow against dealer balance-sheet capacity

- **ID / short name:** IDEA-010 / CREDIT-ETF
- **Date added:** 2026-10-19 · **Cycle:** 4 · **Status:** **parked (P-014, Cycle 78) — terminal** · **Version:** 1.3 (terminal)
- **Change notes:** v1.0 — initial dossier. New mechanism family not previously in the register.
  **Cycle 6 — re-scored 68.0 → 65.0:** the mechanism's core (AP inventory and balance-sheet
  constraints driving ETF–NAV dislocations) is published institutionally (S-123), so novelty falls
  3 → 2; G-13 passes only partially, because the *differentiating* state is commercial and the
  published substitutes are unvalidated (gap G-16). Retained, not shortlisted; no validation
  specification until G-16 is closed.
  **Cycle 32 — re-scored 65.0 → 68.0 and V-010 written:** G-16 closed in the candidate's favour on a
  reformulated validation target (S-194's AP regulatory capital ratios, disclosed in SEC regulatory
  filings), with three published rivals (S-196, S-197, S-199).
  **v1.1 (Cycle 35) — G-23 closed in the candidate's favour:** the capital state is quarterly and
  lagged (S-225, S-226), but the claim never required the state to change inside the holding window,
  so the identification is re-anchored to a cross-sectional contrast, the 1–10 session horizon is
  preserved, the signedness becomes the first pre-registered killable test, and G-27 (effective sample
  and per-AP filing comparability) is opened. V-010 amended; no score change (68.0).
  **Cycle 52 — no score change (68.0), rank 3 unchanged.** G-30's status was **re-derived** rather than
  carried forward: all four sub-routes remain closed (regulatory — Cycle 41; sponsor-published lists —
  Cycles 42–43; non-US/UCITS issuers — Cycle 42; exchange listing rules — Cycle 43), no new route was
  identified in Cycles 44–51 or in Cycle 52, **no statutory filing was re-run**, and **no crowding proxy
  was substituted**. G-30 is therefore **exhausted**, not open, and **V-010 remains non-executable**
  against the corporate-bond ETF universe. The Cycle-52 R-032 screen added a sixth hard-stop condition:
  **the state used must not be a public real-time by-product of the flow it is supposed to predict**
  (S-260, S-261). The state remains **annual** (9–14 months stale at any decision date).
  **Cycle 69 — no score change (68.0), rank 3 unchanged** *(note backfilled in Cycle 76; the Cycle-69
  block was written to the dossier body in that cycle but the change notes were not updated, so the
  dossier's own header understated its history — a state-integrity repair recorded in the ledger's
  Cycle-76 repair block)*: an evidence-currency re-read added **S-323** (Dinger et al. 2025, FoFI) —
  corporate bond ETFs carry **lower liquidity risk but higher intermediary risk** than their underlying
  bond portfolios, a **one-standard-deviation deterioration in active AP balance sheets raises ETF–NAV
  differentials by nearly 10 % of a standard deviation**, ~30 registered APs of which only ~4 are active
  in a year with the top three handling ~95 % of creation/redemption volume, and an **intermediary-risk
  premium** (average expected excess return 3.5 % versus 2.0 % for the underlying portfolios, with
  realised returns nearly identical). V-010 remained non-executable and G-30 remained exhausted.
  **v1.2 (Cycle 76) — the trigger-based re-read Cycle 75 handed on was executed; no score change (68.0),
  rank 2 unchanged.** **S-331 was line-verified** (the Cycle-75 extraction failure was a tooling gap, not
  a document defect), which removed the register's stated precondition, and the re-read was run: the
  published result is a **neighbour of the candidate's mechanism, not the candidate's own relation**, and
  it supplies a **rival explanation** for the candidate's effect; **S-195 was line-verified** and yields a
  **published competitor conditioner** (market volatility, bond-market illiquidity, trading costs —
  available at decision time where the candidate's own state is not) and a **published sign-reversal
  mechanism**. **Two new hard-stop flags** were attached and **gap G-34 was opened** with a kill condition.
  V-010 received a Cycle-76 note and **remains non-executable**; G-30 was **not** re-probed; the
  register's "evidence currency complete for this generation" claim is **restored** for this candidate.
  **Cycle 77 — G-34 executed as a bounded literature operation; the pre-registered kill condition FIRED.**
  No published work was located that decomposes a corporate-bond ETF's ETF–NAV discount into a transient
  and a mechanical component, and none that identifies a published, decision-time state discriminating
  between the two in the corporate-bond ETF universe without the ETF→AP mapping. Per the decision rule
  registered in Cycle 76, the candidate's residual is therefore **unmeasurable as specified** and the
  candidate is to be **re-classified (downgraded or parked), not re-specified**. No score changed in
  Cycle 77 and the candidate's re-classification was left to the next cycle (gap **G-35**).
  **v1.3 (Cycle 78) — the kill condition is executed: the candidate is PARKED (P-014) with a terminal
  score of 55.0.** The decision is **park, not downgrade**, on three grounds that the register's own
  precedents support: (i) the candidate's state is **not attachable to its own universe at all**
  (G-29: 0 corporate-bond ETFs with a published AP set; G-30 exhausted across all four outside routes),
  which is the register's IDEA-002 rejection-grade fact (R-038); (ii) the candidate's residual — the
  transient-versus-mechanical discriminator — is **unmeasurable as specified** (G-34 kill condition
  fired in Cycle 77), which is the IDEA-003/P-012 parking precedent; and (iii) no published result
  **shows the candidate's own effect**, so the register rejects only on that ground (R-011's rule) and
  the candidate is parked, not rejected. The terminal rubric is **mechanism 4, evidence 4, novelty 1,
  falsifiability 2, resilience 2, data 2 = 55.0** (data 4 → 2 because the state is unattachable in the
  candidate's universe; falsifiability 4 → 2 because neither distinguishing claim can be tested as
  specified; resilience 3 → 2 because five published rivals and a published sign-reversal mechanism now
  stand against the candidate and the mechanical reading cannot be excluded). **V-010 is marked
  never-to-execute.** The candidate is unranked; its mechanism is a **known baseline** (S-194, S-195,
  S-196, S-198, S-323) and the rediscovery guard now names it.
  **Cycle 88 — no score change (55.0 terminal, parked), no status change, V-010 remains
  never-to-execute.** A **neighbour family** was screened and written to the known-baseline table as
  **row 87**: the **closed-end-fund / BDC traded price-to-NAV discount family** — listed vehicles with a
  fixed share count and **no continuous creation/redemption** (R-050). It is recorded here because it is
  the **structural mirror** of this candidate's residual, and the two must not be confused: an ETF's
  NAV gap is a **mechanical widening** inside a vehicle that *has* a creation/redemption mechanism, so a
  published, decision-time state might in principle discriminate its transient from its mechanical part,
  whereas a closed-end fund's or a BDC's discount is **permanent by design** — the vehicle has no
  creation/redemption route, the discount is a published trade-off between the liquidity benefit and the
  manager's fees (S-337), and a BDC's below-NAV issuance needs shareholder approval and carries a
  statutory quarterly repurchase offer at NAV (S-339, S-342). **Row 87 is therefore a rediscovery guard,
  not a re-specification of IDEA-010**, and it neither re-opens this candidate nor changes its terminal
  score, its status or V-010's never-to-execute marking. The published account of the ETF–NAV gap's
  *mechanical* component (S-196, S-198) and of the closed-end discount's *permanent* component are
  different mechanisms and are not interchangeable evidence.

**One-sentence hypothesis.** When corporate-bond ETF redemptions force authorised participants (who are also bond dealers) to absorb illiquid bonds onto already-constrained balance sheets, the ETF–NAV discount widens and the underlying bonds cheapen; the state (AP balance-sheet stress and ETF flow) is published, and the trade supplies the liquidity the AP cannot.

**Plain-language description.** Corporate-bond ETFs create and redeem shares through authorised participants — typically big bond dealers. When investors redeem ETF shares, the AP has to take the underlying bonds onto its own balance sheet temporarily. If the AP's balance sheet is already tight (dealers are constrained), the AP will demand a bigger discount to absorb those bonds, so the ETF trades at a wider discount to NAV and the underlying bonds cheapen. A participant who supplies the liquidity the AP cannot — buying the cheapened bonds or the discounted ETF — earns the concession when the AP's balance-sheet stress eases and the discount narrows.

**Mechanism.** APs use creations/redemptions to manage balance sheets; when balance-sheet space is scarce and bonds are costly to intermediate, APs become liquidity seekers in the ETF, widening the ETF–NAV gap and cheapening the underlying bonds (Pan & Zeng 2019). Dealer balance-sheet costs and interdealer capacity are published/measurable proxies (Eisfeldt, Herskovic & Liu 2024). The decision-time state is observable: ETF creation/redemption baskets and AP-level balance-sheet proxies are reported with known lags; the flow itself (ETF shares outstanding change) is published daily.

**Expected return source.** Compensation for bearing intermediary balance-sheet risk in the bond–ETF basis, harvestable when the gap exceeds the cost floor; the basis mean-reverts as AP capacity recovers.

**Originality.** Closest work: Pan & Zeng 2019 (AP balance-sheet constraints limit ETF arbitrage); Eisfeldt et al. 2024 (interdealer price dispersion / dealer capacity). Differentiator: a *state-conditioned, two-legged* specification keyed to published AP-stress proxies and a pre-scheduled exit, rather than a static basis trade. Honest classification: informed synthesis; the joint conditioning is an unresolved conjecture. Confidence: moderate.

**Target scope.** US investment-grade and high-yield corporate bond ETFs vs their creation baskets; daily state; holding 1–10 sessions; regime conditions: stressed dealer capacity, normal-to-high redemption flow.

**Observables (decision time).** ETF shares outstanding change (daily, published); ETF–NAV discount; AP balance-sheet proxies (dealer positions, interdealer price dispersion, SRF take-up); bond liquidity proxies (TRACE volume, dealer inventory); macro release calendar.

**Falsifiable predictions.** (1) ETF–NAV discount widens when AP-stress proxies are high and ETF flow is negative. (2) The discount mean-reverts within 1–10 sessions. (3) The effect is absent when AP capacity is ample (control). (4) Gross discount exceeds round-trip cost in selected ETFs. Kill: no state-dependence, no reversion, or effect below cost.

**Confounders.** Fundamental bond re-pricing during stress; index-rebalance flows; redemption-driven forced selling that is permanent, not temporary; dealer inventory accounting changes.

**Data requirements.** ETF prices/NAV, creation/redemption baskets, TRACE bond data, dealer position proxies, AP balance-sheet data. Feasibility: medium (TRACE is public; AP-level data is commercial).

**Theory/evidence status.** Conceptual rationale supported (Pan & Zeng 2019; Eisfeldt et al. 2024). Joint relation unresolved conjecture — **and as of Cycle 78 the joint relation is also unmeasurable as specified, because the state is not attachable to the candidate's universe (G-29/G-30) and no published discriminator separates the transient reading from the mechanical one (G-34 kill condition fired in Cycle 77)**. **Terminal score 55/100, parked (P-014), unranked.**

**Constraints.** Cost/liquidity: corporate bond ETFs and their underlying bonds are less liquid than equities; the spread is crossed twice (ETF and bond); the discount itself is the edge, so transaction costs are first-order. Capacity: bounded by the size of the ETF's creation/redemption flow and by the liquidity of the underlying bonds; high-yield ETFs have the widest discounts but the thinnest underlying markets. Financing/margin: bond positions require margin; a leveraged basis trade consumes balance sheet exactly when dealer capacity is tight — the same constraint that generates the signal. Venue/operational: TRACE is public but with a lag; creation/redemption baskets are published daily by the ETF sponsor; AP-level balance-sheet data is commercial or inferred from dealer disclosures. Shorting constraints: not a borrow-constrained trade, but shorting corporate bonds is costly and recall-risk exposed.

**Regime sensitivity, tail exposure, crash behavior, hidden beta.** Regime: the candidate predicts an edge only when dealer balance-sheet capacity is constrained; in normal times the ETF–NAV basis is narrow and the edge is absent. Tail: a credit stress event widens spreads permanently (fundamental re-pricing, not temporary discount), and the position is long illiquid bonds into a falling market. Crash behaviour: in a dealer balance-sheet crisis (e.g. March 2020), the basis can blow out and not mean-revert within the holding window; the AP may stop making markets entirely. Hidden beta: the position is long corporate credit and short ETF shares — it loads on credit spread duration and on the dollar (for USD-denominated bonds), and can carry event risk from issuer-specific news. Correlated risks: all bond ETFs in the same credit tier share the same dealer-capacity state, so the book is a single credit-cycle bet.

**Remaining uncertainty / confidence.** Confidence that AP balance-sheet constraints widen ETF–NAV discounts: **moderate** (Pan & Zeng 2019 documents the mechanism; dealer capacity is documented by Eisfeldt et al. 2024). Confidence that a tradeable two-legged rule can be built on published AP-stress proxies: **low-to-moderate** — AP-level data is commercial, the discount mean-reversion is unproven at the daily frequency, and the cost floor in thin bond markets is binding. **As of Cycle 78 that confidence is downgraded to *low*, and for a structural reason rather than an evidentiary one: the state is not attachable to the candidate's own universe (G-29 coverage 0, G-30 exhausted) and the transient-versus-mechanical reading cannot be discriminated (G-34 kill condition fired).** Terminal score **55/100**; **parked (P-014), unranked**. **Concrete next question (now a re-admission question, not a research question):** what published work would make the candidate's state attachable to a corporate-bond ETF and its transient reading separable from the mechanical one — see the unblocking condition in `rejected_and_parked.md`, **P-014**.

**Cycle 6 screen (G-13 publication timing; G-12 sign or existence).**
- **G-13 — the state is published before the decision: PARTIAL PASS, and the partial pass is
  decisive for the score.** The *flow* leg is clean: ETF shares outstanding are published daily by
  the sponsor, and creation/redemption baskets are published daily by the sponsor, so the flow
  state is a decision-time published quantity. The *differentiating* leg — **AP balance-sheet
  stress** — is not published by any institution in the form the dossier specifies (AP-level
  balance-sheet data is commercial). The dossier's own Observables line already substitutes
  *proxies* for the AP state (dealer positions, interdealer price dispersion, SRF take-up); those
  proxies are computable from published data (TRACE-based measures; the Federal Reserve's standing
  repo facility usage), but they are **different constructs** and have not been validated against
  any dealer- or AP-balance-sheet measure. Under the register's own rule, re-specifying around an
  unvalidated proxy is not permitted, so the candidate is retained only with an explicit
  precondition — gap **G-16**: validate the published proxy set against a dealer-balance-sheet
  measure *before* any specification is written.
- **G-12 — the conditioning changes the sign or the existence of the effect: PASS on the existence
  limb, but the mechanism's core is already published.** Prediction (3) is an existence claim —
  "the effect is absent when AP capacity is ample" — and prediction (1) is a joint conditioning
  claim, so the candidate passes G-12 in form. But the *mechanism* it conditions is not new:
  the FCA's own study documents the connection between ETF mispricing and AP inventory (S-123),
  which is already in the register's **baseline** table, and the baseline table's own note records
  that the FCA had to use a regulator's MiFID II database to see AP inventory at all. So the
  candidate's residual is the *joint state plus a trade specification*, not a new mechanism.
- **Score: 68.0 → 65.0.** Novelty **3 → 2** — the mechanism's core (AP inventory and balance-sheet
  constraints driving ETF–NAV dislocations) is published institutionally (S-123), which is what a
  novelty score of 3 was implicitly denying. Mechanism 4, evidence 3, falsifiability 4, resilience 3
  and data 3 unchanged. The unchanged hard-stop flags are the **cost-overwhelm condition** (the
  spread is crossed twice, in thin bond markets, and the discount *is* the edge) and the
  **AP-data-commercial condition**.
- **Effect on the dossier: retained as a candidate at rank 5, with two preconditions.** No
  validation specification is to be written until G-16 is closed; and the "Originality" line is
  re-stated as a refinement of a published institutional baseline rather than as a new mechanism.

**Cycle 13 screen (G-13 publication timing; G-12 sign or existence) — no score change; G-16
narrowed.**
- **G-13 — PARTIAL PASS, unchanged.** The flow leg (ETF shares outstanding and baskets, published
  daily by the sponsor) remains clean; the AP-balance-sheet leg remains commercial.
- **G-12 — PASS on the existence limb, unchanged.** The "effect is absent when AP capacity is
  ample" prediction remains an existence claim.
- **G-16 is narrowed, not closed.** Cycle 13 established that *dealer balance-sheet cost* is now a
  published, modelled channel with a public, decision-time measurement set — the Federal Reserve's
  own notes name the SOMA, the ON RRP facility and the TGA as the channels and use the public
  **TGCR − IORB** spread as the funding-stress measure (S-162), with a second note quantifying the
  sensitivity of repo rates to Treasury issuance as the Fed's balance sheet shrinks (S-161) and an
  IMF working paper identifying TGA flows as a driver of repo-spread swings (S-160). **What still
  does not exist is a validated proxy for *credit*-dealer balance-sheet stress**; the published
  proxies are Treasury-repo funding measures, which are a different construct. G-16 therefore stays
  open with a sharper statement: *validate a public proxy for credit-dealer balance-sheet stress,
  or show that Treasury-repo funding measures are a valid substitute in the credit ETF–NAV
  discount.*
- **Effect on the dossier: no score change (65.0); rank 6 → 4** (rank change is an artefact of
  IDEA-001's rejection and IDEA-004's re-score). No validation specification is written, exactly as
  G-16 requires.

### Cycle 32 (2026-10-20) — G-16 closed in favour on a reformulated validation target; the candidate is
re-scored to 68.0, enters the top three, and receives V-010

- **The gap closes, and it closes better than the register framed it.** G-16 asked whether the public
  dealer-stress proxy set could be *validated against disclosed dealer funding value adjustments*. Cycle
  32 shows that the register had picked the **wrong validation target**, and that the validation it
  wanted has in fact already been done — by independent researchers, on a better target, on exactly the
  link the candidate claims.
  - **The FVA target is unusable, for reasons the register's own ledger already contains.** S-155 records
    that a dealer's funding value adjustment is a peer-reviewed construct but that "the paper is a theory
    of FVAs; it does not publish a continuous, public FVA series, and no such series exists". Disclosed
    dealer FVAs are OTC-derivatives-specific, disclosed quarterly or annually, and frequently immaterial
    at the firms that disclose them. A *daily* public proxy cannot meaningfully be validated against a
    quantity that is published once a year and measures something else. **G-16's original framing was
    wrong; the register says so rather than leaving a gap open on a bad premise.**
  - **The right target is already published and it is public.** Raddatz (S-194) links each US corporate-bond
    ETF to its authorized participants "using U.S. SEC regulatory filings", connects the APs to their
    financial statements, and measures **APs' regulatory capital ratios**. The finding is that the AP
    capital ratio *is* the operative state: ETFs linked to APs at the 25th percentile of regulatory capital
    ratios saw a **0.69 percentage-point decline in arbitrage intensity** during the March 2020
    dash-for-cash, against **0.39 percentage points** for 75th-percentile APs, and "other AP
    characteristics, such as equity-to-assets ratios, cash-to-assets ratios, total assets, or AP activity
    concentration, **do not play a significant role**". This is a *published, independent validation of a
    disclosed state against the ETF-premium/arbitrage relation* — i.e. exactly the exercise G-16 demanded,
    performed with a better construct than the register proposed, and it exonerates the candidate's
    proposed proxy set by showing which AP characteristics do and do not matter.
  - **The register's own proxy set is narrowed by two further published facts.** The dispersion proxy the
    register's dossier leans on (Eisfeldt, Herskovic & Liu 2024 = S-200) is a *monthly* measure —
    "given the infrequent trading of bonds, calculating interdealer price dispersion daily is not
    feasible" — which conflicts with a 1–10 session holding rule; and the same paper finds that
    "equity-weighted averages of dealer-level financial soundness **fail** to capture effects from
    interdealer frictions", so an aggregate dealer-balance-sheet (FR Y-9C-style) leverage proxy is
    published but *invalid* for this purpose. A public aggregate dealer-inventory proxy does exist and has
    been used to infer AP inventories: FR 2004 primary dealer activity disclosures (S-198).
  - **Three published rival explanations now stand against the candidate's interpretation**, and they are
    a direct consequence of G-16 closing: (i) **fractional baskets** — an ETF–NAV gap can arise from
    basket composition with no AP balance-sheet constraint involved (S-196); (ii) **funding segmentation** —
    arbitrage spreads respond to idiosyncratic balance-sheet shocks and intermediaries specialise, so the
    funding source rather than the AP's bond-inventory capacity may drive the spread (S-197); and
    (iii) **client-sourced liquidity discounts** — dealers pay relationship discounts to investors who
    supply liquidity, which is a published market structure of the same "concession for liquidity"
    shape the candidate claims (S-199).
  - **A citation correction.** The register's "Pan & Zeng 2019" is the SSRN revision of a **working
    paper** (S-195: ESRB Working Paper No. 59, December 2017, SSRN 2895478). Its empirical datasets are
    **proprietary** — proprietary AP lists from two ETF sponsors covering ~83% of passive corporate-bond
    ETF assets, a proprietary TRACE build with dealer identifiers, and proprietary creation/redemption
    baskets. That is the empirical provenance of the register's belief that "AP-level data is commercial":
    the *published evidence* is proprietary-based even though the *state construct* is publicly
    reconstructable (S-194).
- **Effect on the dossier: score 65.0 → 68.0; rank 4 → 3; G-16 CLOSED; V-010 written.**
  - **G-16 — CLOSED IN FAVOUR.** The precondition "a validated, public, decision-time proxy for dealer
    balance-sheet stress" is met by published work; the validation target is the AP's filing-disclosed
    regulatory capital ratio. The register's own rule ("no validation specification is to be written
    until G-16 is closed") is therefore satisfied, and **V-010 is written in this cycle**.
  - **Novelty 2 → 1.** The state-to-premium relation that constitutes the candidate's differentiator is
    now published (S-194 identifies the state; S-195 supplies the mechanism), the mechanism's core was
    already published in Cycle 6, and the family is published three more times over (S-196, S-197, S-199).
    The candidate's residual is the *trade specification and the cost floor* — the same "incremental
    variant" classification the register already applies to IDEA-009 and IDEA-003. A novelty score of 1/5
    is the floor and is recorded as such.
  - **Evidence 3 → 4.** Five independent sources now bear on the mechanism and the state (S-194, S-195,
    S-196, S-197, S-198, S-199, S-200), including one peer-reviewed identification of the state (S-194).
  - **Data 3 → 4.** The register's recorded blocker was that "AP-level data is commercial". The *state* is
    not commercial: it is disclosed in SEC regulatory filings. What remains is a **bespoke ETF↔AP mapping**
    from filings, a **filing lag** (quarterly reports against a 1–10 session rule), and a mapping that
    covers only the APs that file. The dimension is raised, not maximised, and the residual costs are
    recorded here and in G-23.
  - **Mechanism 4 → 4, falsifiability 4 → 4, resilience 3 → 3 (unchanged).** The mechanism is unchanged
    and is now *published*, which raises the bar for the candidate's claim rather than lowering it; the
    cost-overwhelm kill condition (thin bond markets, two legs, ETF and bond) is unchanged and is now
    harder to beat, because the published family already trades the same spread.
  - **New gap G-23.** (i) Is the filing-disclosed AP regulatory capital ratio usable at the cadence the
    candidate's rule requires, and (ii) is it a **signed** state rather than only a moderator, given the
    three published rival explanations and the monthly frequency of the published dispersion proxy?

### Cycle 35 (2026-10-20) — G-23 closed in the candidate's favour: the cadence is resolved by re-anchoring the identification, the horizon is preserved, the signedness becomes the first killable test, and the effective sample is registered as the residual (G-27)

- **The cadence facts, established this cycle.** The state is refreshed only when an AP files.
  **S-225** (SEC Form 10-Q General Instruction A.1, with 17 CFR 240.13a-13 and 240.13a-11): a Form 10-Q
  is due for each of the first three quarters within the period set by General Instruction A.1 — 40 days
  after quarter end for large accelerated and accelerated filers, 45 days for all other registrants —
  and the annual report is due 60 / 75 / 90 days after fiscal year end by filer status; 240.13a-13 also
  exempts investment companies, foreign private issuers and asset-backed issuers. **S-226** (Federal
  Reserve FR Y-9C): quarterly, due 40 calendar days after the March, June and September as-of dates and
  45 after December, with the public data released later and revisable until the deadline. So the state
  is **at best quarterly and lagged** — exactly the constraint G-23 was opened for.
- **Why the cadence is *not* fatal, and what it costs instead.** The candidate's claim never required
  the state to change *inside* the holding window: it requires the **level** of the AP set's capital
  ratio to govern the **size** of the flow-induced discount widening and its reversion. A state that is
  constant between filings is fully usable for a claim of that form — and the published relation itself
  (S-194) identifies the state at filing frequency, so the published evidence was never a
  session-frequency state either. Two consequences are now binding on V-010:
  1. **The primary identification is cross-sectional** — weak-capital ETFs versus strong-capital ETFs on
     redemption days, with the state read as of the last filing date and held constant between filings.
     The within-ETF time-series reading is demoted to a **low-power ablation**, to be reported as such
     with its own effective-sample count.
  2. **The effective sample must be reported and "untested" remains a permitted verdict.** The
     informative units are (ETF, filing-date) state observations and the redemption days inside them;
     the counts are small and the March 2020 dash-for-cash episode is the episode in which the published
     state bound (S-194). A null in a small sample must be reported as *untested*, not upgraded into a
     kill.
- **The horizon is preserved and the candidate is not parked.** The 1–10 session exit governs the
  **reversion of the flow-induced widening**; the state's refresh cadence does not enter the exit. No
  horizon extension is authorised and no parking follows from the cadence. The V-010 Cycle-34
  amendment's conditional instruction — "if the 1–10 session rule proves infeasible due to cadence, the
  horizon must be extended or the candidate reconsidered for parking" — is therefore **resolved in the
  candidate's favour** and superseded by the Cycle-35 amendment.
- **Signedness: the literature half closed, the specification half closed.** S-194 establishes a
  **signed, monotone** relation between the AP-set capital state and arbitrage intensity (a 0.69pp
  versus 0.39pp decline in arbitrage intensity for bottom- versus top-quartile capital ratios), so the
  state is signed in the mechanism's direction and not merely a moderator. The residual was never a
  literature question but a specification obligation, and it is closed as one: the signed prediction —
  *on redemption days, weak-capital ETFs' discounts widen more than strong-capital ETFs' discounts* — is
  now the candidate's **first pre-registered, killable test**, measured on the *change* in the ETF–NAV
  discount with ETF fixed effects absorbing the level, and the three rivals are now specified as controls
  on the **interaction** rather than on the level: S-196 (fractional baskets) as a level explanation
  absorbed by fixed effects; S-197 (funding segmentation) controlled for with public funding-state
  variables; S-199 (client liquidity discounts) controlled for by requiring the bond-side cheapening to
  be specific to the ETF's own AP set and redemption days. S-200's monthly dispersion proxy remains a
  robustness check, and aggregate dealer financial-weakness averages remain a documented inferiority.
- **Hard-stop flag added.** The candidate must not be presented as a discovery of the state-to-premium
  relation (published, S-194, S-195), and must not be presented as evidence that a *session-refreshed*
  capital state is tradeable: the state is a **slow filter** and the rule is a cross-sectional contrast.
- **New gap G-27.** Two residual data questions: the **effective-sample counts** above, and the
  **per-AP filing frequency and cross-regime comparability** of the capital ratio (broker-dealer versus
  bank holding company versus asset manager — the firm-level public availability of a broker-dealer's
  regulatory capital ratio was not resolved this cycle). The register must not assume a single
  quarterly cadence for a heterogeneous AP set.
- **Score: 68.0, unchanged.** Mechanism 4 → 4, evidence 4 → 4 (unchanged — no new source bears on the
  state-to-premium relation itself; the new sources establish the *cadence* and the *signedness*), novelty
  1 → 1 (unchanged — the published relation is not the candidate's discovery), falsifiability 4 → 4
  (unchanged — the re-anchoring sharpens the test but does not create a new one), resilience 3 → 3
  (unchanged — the cost-overwhelm kill condition is unchanged), data 4 → 4 (unchanged — the state is
  public in principle; the residual costs are now registered in G-27 rather than in G-23). **Rank 3,
  unchanged.**

#### Cycle 37 block — IDEA-010 · the state-availability residual is CLOSED IN THE CANDIDATE'S FAVOUR

- **What Cycle 37 did.** It executed the check that Cycle 36 declined to execute, and found that
  **Cycle 36's central negative claim was wrong**. Cycle 36 recorded the firm-level public
  availability of broker-dealer regulatory capital as untested, on the stated ground that FOCUS
  reports "are not generally disseminated as regular public datasets in the same way that FR Y-9C or
  call-report data are disseminated". That is a statement about a *dataset*, and it was used as a
  statement about the *data*. It is false: **Form X-17A-5 is a public EDGAR form type, and the
  net-capital figures of broker-dealers are in publicly indexed documents** (S-231, fetched directly;
  6,131 public X-17A-5 documents contain "excess net capital").
- **The route, precisely.** (i) **Private, non-listed APs** — Form X-17A-5 annual filings on EDGAR:
  Citadel Securities (three CIKs; the FY2025 Institutional filing `CSIN_BS_Only_FS_2025.pdf` is
  publicly indexed and matches "excess net capital"), Virtu Americas (FY2017–FY2025, file
  descriptions "PUBLIC REPORT" / "PUBLIC ANNUAL REPORTS"), KCG Americas, AllianceBernstein
  Investments (whose FY2018 document has file type **"COMP NET CAP"**), RFQ-Hub Americas, IEX
  Services (S-231, S-232). (ii) **Listed broker-dealers** — quarterly, XBRL-tagged disclosure of net
  capital, required net capital and excess net capital in Exchange Act periodic reports (S-233:
  LPL Financial's Form 10-Q exhibit R27, as of 31 December 2024). (iii) **Bank APs** — FR Y-9C and
  call reports, already in the register (S-226). (iv) **Routes that are closed**: FINRA's BrokerCheck
  does not release firm-level net capital and FINRA publishes excess net capital only in aggregate by
  firm-size bucket (S-234) — so a specification must use the filings, not FINRA's tables.
- **G-27 closed in the candidate's favour on availability, with three qualifications that are part of
  the verdict.** (a) **Cadence is firm-dependent**: annual for private broker-dealers (fiscal
  year-end, filed February–March, so 40–90 days stale at any decision date), quarterly for listed
  broker-dealers and bank holding companies, and quarterly for at least some private filers (S-231
  records a period ending 30 September 2024) — the register must *measure* it per firm rather than
  assume it. (b) **Format is document-level**: the route requires parsing per firm per year from
  EDGAR, using the public full-text index to locate the documents; this is why the data score does
  not move (available in principle, but not a tidy dataset). (c) **Comparability**: FOCUS net capital
  is a liquidity-style quantity and is not commensurable with a bank holding company's risk-based
  ratios, nor does an asset-manager AP have an equivalent — so the cross-sectional contrast must be
  built within a comparable capital regime, or the mapping must be justified explicitly.
  **Cycle-98 annotation — qualification (a) is confirmed in the documents, and the exhibit citation
  must be tightened.** The **listed-broker-dealer** case was checked directly against the filings this
  cycle (S-233, upgraded to line-verified): for LPL Financial Inc. the **net-capital table is published
  quarterly in the Form 10-Q body** — "March 31, 2025 LPL Financial LLC Net capital $494,111 Less:
  required net capital $17,926 Excess net capital $476,185" — while the **XBRL-tagged exhibit is filed
  with the Form 10-K** (the 31 December 2024 figures, $443,742 / $19,426 / $424,316, are confirmed
  exactly). Two practical consequences for this dossier, neither of which moves the verdict: (i) the
  quantity IDEA-010 needs is **available quarterly** for a listed broker-dealer, so qualification (a)
  holds as stated; and (ii) the **XBRL exhibit route is annual** for this registrant and the exhibit's
  **file name is reused across filings for unrelated XBRL exhibits**, so any future specification must
  cite the net-capital table **by form type, accession number and filing date** (and may prefer the 10-Q
  body) rather than by exhibit name. **G-27 stays closed in the candidate's favour; no gap is opened,
  closed or re-scoped, and IDEA-010's park and terminal score are unchanged.**
- **Cycle-100 annotation — provenance only: the two state-availability limbs of this dossier are now
  line-verified against primary text, and neither moves the verdict.** (a) **G-23's cadence limb is
  strengthened.** The FR Y-9C submission deadlines, which this dossier had carried as index-rendered,
  are confirmed verbatim from the **Federal Reserve's own FR Y-9C instructions (June 2026 edition,
  618 pages, fetched directly through the Board's reporting-forms application)**: "The submission date
  for holding companies is **40 calendar days** after the March 31, June 30, and September 30 as-of dates
  unless that day falls on a weekend or holiday (subject to timely filing provisions). The submission date
  for holding companies is **45 calendar days** after the December 31 as of date." The form's index page
  confirms the cadence — "Frequency: Quarterly, as of the last calendar day of the quarter" — so the
  state is **at best quarterly and lagged**, exactly as this dossier states. **One correction is carried
  into the ledger entry (S-226)**: the public-availability wording this dossier repeats ("with the public
  data released later and revisable until the deadline") is **not** confirmed by the fetched documents —
  the Board's own public-release statement names the Federal Reserve Bulletin, the BHCPR and the Board's
  Freedom of Information Office, not the FFIEC National Information Center — and is therefore marked
  unverified in S-226. The **cadence conclusion is unchanged**; the *release-lag* sub-claim is now
  explicitly unverified rather than silently carried. (b) **G-27 limb 1's negative half is
  strengthened.** FINRA Rule 8312, fetched directly, enumerates the BrokerCheck release set and
  **contains no firm-level net capital** (nor any IAPD item), which confirms that a candidate cannot use
  BrokerCheck for this state — the register's route selection. **No gap is opened, closed or re-scoped;
  no score changes; the park (P-014) and the terminal 55.0 are unchanged.**
- **G-29 opened — the ETF → AP-set mapping and the coverage count that now bounds the candidate.**
  IDEA-010's identification is a *cross-sectional* contrast, so the state must be attached to an ETF
  through the identity of the firms that arbitrage it. Two facts are needed and only one is
  established: **which corporate-bond ETFs name their authorised participants in a public filing**
  (trade press reported in April 2024 that the iShares Bitcoin Trust prospectus names its APs —
  a lead, not a verified source, and deliberately not entered in the ledger as one; the
  corporate-bond ETF summary prospectuses read in Cycle 37 disclose authorised-participant *risk*
  generically and do **not** name them), and **how many (ETF, AP) cells have both a published AP-set
  membership and a public capital ratio**. The register must not re-specify around a weaker proxy
  (S-026's Days-ADV crowding, S-157's comomentum, S-222's crowding factor): those measure all 13F
  institutions, i.e. directional positioning, not the arbitrage capital the candidate's state is.
- **V-010 amended** (see `validation_handoffs.md`): the capital state must be reconstructed from
  filings with the per-firm cadence measured, the comparison held within a comparable capital regime,
  and the G-23 effective-sample report extended with the G-29 coverage count.
- **Score: 68.0, unchanged. Rank 3, unchanged.** No score moves: the state was already scored as
  public *in principle* (data 4/5), and the finding changes the route's *specificity*, not the
  candidate's novelty, falsifiability or evidence. What changes is the register's knowledge: the
  candidate is no longer blocked by a false negative, and is now blocked by a **countable** one
  (G-29) plus the effective-sample condition already registered.
- **Hard-stop flags added in Cycle 37.** (i) The state may not be described as a tidy public dataset;
  it must be described as **filings**, with per-firm cadence. (ii) The cross-sectional contrast may
  not mix capital regimes without an explicit mapping argument. (iii) The candidate may not proceed
  until the G-29 coverage count is produced, because the effective-sample count depends on it.

### Cycle 40 block — G-29 EXECUTED as a calibrated document-level search; the route is closed, the
### candidate is not; and the state is **annual**, which the candidate's identification now assumes

- **What was executed.** Cycle 40 did not re-read prospectuses. It built a *detector* and checked it
  against a known true positive first. The detector phrase is the naming sentence used by the ETF
  trusts that do publish their APs — "the Authorized Participants that are able to conduct …" — and
  the true positive is the **iShares Bitcoin Trust ETF** (CIK 1980994), whose Form 424B3 prospectuses
  name their APs verbatim: the 9 January 2024 filing names **five** (ABN AMRO Clearing USA LLC, Jane
  Street Capital LLC, JP Morgan Securities LLC, Macquarie Capital (USA) Inc., Virtu Americas LLC), the
  11 April 2024 filing names **nine**, the 5 August 2024 filing names **twelve**, and the 31 July 2025
  filing names **twelve cash APs** (adding BMO Capital Markets Corp., BofA Securities Inc., Citadel
  Securities LLC, Citigroup Global Markets Inc., Jefferies LLC, JP Morgan Securities LLC, Macquarie
  Capital (USA) Inc. and UBS Securities LLC) and **four in-kind APs** (Jane Street Capital, Virtu
  Americas, JP Morgan Securities, Marex Capital Markets Inc.) — **S-239**, now in the ledger, so the
  Cycle-37 trade-press lead is a verified source rather than a lead.
- **What the detector found.** Searching that naming sentence across every prospectus form type in
  the EDGAR full-text index returns **five documents in four filers** — iShares Bitcoin Trust ETF,
  iShares Ethereum Trust ETF, iShares Staked Ethereum Trust ETF and iShares Bitcoin Premium Income
  ETF — and **no corporate-bond ETF**. The complement of the detector was run as well: (i) the
  *phrase-first* route over 591 bond-worded filings containing an AP-naming sentence, in which every
  document that actually names APs in the naming position is a **commodity or crypto trust**
  (iShares S&P GSCI Commodity-Indexed Trust, iShares Gold Trust Micro, World Gold Trust, WisdomTree
  Bitcoin Fund, the Invesco Galaxy, 21Shares, Morgan Stanley digital-asset and T. Rowe Price Active
  Crypto trusts); and (ii) the *name-first* route over bond-worded filings containing any of thirty-one
  AP firm names, in which the names occur **only** as securities-lending "percentage of ownership"
  entries and in the standing "an Authorized Participant may hold of record more than 25 % of the
  outstanding shares" disclosure, and as **revenue-sharing and placement payees** (verified: the
  Pershing, RBC, Schwab, TD Ameritrade, MSWM and Fidelity entries in the SPDR Series Trust SAI). Two
  sponsor product pages were probed directly (iShares AGG, Vanguard BND): both use the generic
  formulation and name no AP; the **unit-investment-trust** class was checked as a separate structure
  (First Trust corporate-bond 497K filings of 13 February 2025 and 2 March 2026) and names no AP firm
  either; and a **second, independent detector** — every filing containing an authorised-participant
  naming position followed by corporate entity names, across sixteen filing form types — was read
  document by document: **3,628 of 4,409 documents read at the end of the cycle, of which 434 name APs,
  and every one of the 434 is a commodity or crypto trust**.
- **The coverage counts the gap asked for.** Corporate-bond ETFs with a published AP set: **0**.
  (ETF, AP) cells with both a published AP set and a public capital ratio: **0**. (ETF, AP, episode)
  cells: **0**. **Verdict: not specifiable from public filings — untested, not dead.** The register
  must not substitute a published crowding proxy (S-026's Days-ADV, S-157's comomentum, S-222's
  crowding factor): those measure all 13F institutions, i.e. directional positioning, not the
  arbitrage capital this candidate's state is.
- **The capital state, re-derived at filer level (G-27 closed).** The phrase corpus contains **6,131
  X-17A-5 documents from 2,419 distinct broker-dealer filers** — a **lower bound**, because the
  filer-level index of the same firms shows how far wording-counts and firm-counts diverge (Citadel
  Securities LLC files 24 X-17A-5 documents over 23 periods and contains the phrase in **one** of
  them). Listed broker-dealers are covered by Form 10-Q (**1,151 documents, 66 filers, quarterly**)
  and Form 10-K (**737 documents, 96 filers, annual**). Bank holding companies are covered by Form
  FR Y-9C, but **FR Y-9C is not indexed by EDGAR's full-text search at all** (zero hits for "capital",
  "net capital" and "Federal Reserve" restricted to that form), so that leg must be enumerated from
  the Federal Reserve's own National Information Center rather than from EDGAR — S-226 restated
  accordingly.
- **Correction to the register's own cadence statement (important for this candidate).** The Cycle-37
  statement that the private-broker-dealer capital state is "annual for most private broker-dealers
  … quarterly for at least some private filers" does not survive filer-level checking. Over the 2,419
  filers in the corpus, **2,414 disclose once per year and five disclose twice per year; not one
  discloses three or more times a year**, and the single quarterly document that produced the
  exception (Agentis Capital Markets US, LLC, period ending 2024-09-30) does not appear in the corpus
  at all. Filer-level enumeration of the **thirteen named AP firms** and of **fifteen major private AP
  firms** found **annual cadence in every case** (fiscal year-end, filed February–March). The state
  this candidate uses is therefore **annual**, and at any decision date it is **9–14 months stale**.
  This *reinforces* rather than undermines the Cycle-35 re-anchoring: the state never changes inside a
  holding window, so the identification must be cross-sectional between AP sets and the time-series
  reading stays a low-power ablation.
- **The arithmetic, on the one ETF whose AP set is published.** 13 distinct named AP firms; **13 of
  13** with a public Form X-17A-5 filing; **285 (AP, filing-date) episode cells**; cadence **annual**
  for all thirteen; **none of the thirteen files a Form 10-Q or 10-K**, so Form X-17A-5 is the *only*
  public capital route for the entire published set. This is the register's first worked example of
  the (ETF, AP, episode) count, and it is also the first demonstration that the private-AP capital
  state is a **slow filter** — exactly the register's standing rule.
- **What is now opened: G-30.** The route closed is the **statutory filing** route. It is not closed
  for all primary sources: a sponsor's own published participant list, an exchange or regulatory
  disclosure requirement, or a non-US issuer's prospectus could still disclose a corporate-bond ETF's
  AP set. **G-30 asks for a specific route, not another re-reading of prospectuses.**
- **Effect on V-010.** V-010 is **amended, not withdrawn**: it may not be executed against a
  corporate-bond ETF state, because no such attachable state exists; it remains executable only if a
  specifiable route emerges (G-30), and it must report the effective-sample denominator in the form
  established here (cells per (ETF, AP set) per filing date, with the cadence stated).
- **Score: 68.0, unchanged. Rank 3, unchanged.** No score moves. The Cycle-37 finding was a negative
  about the *route*; Cycle 40 converted it into a **calibrated, population-level** negative and
  corrected two of the register's own supporting statements. What changed is the register's knowledge,
  not the candidate's merit.
- **Hard-stop flags added in Cycle 40.** (i) The candidate may not describe the AP capital ratio as
  attachable to a corporate-bond ETF from public filings — **no such attachment exists**. (ii) The
  candidate may not describe the capital state as quarterly: for the private AP set it is **annual**,
  9–14 months stale, and a quarterly reading of it is false. (iii) The candidate may not present the
  state-to-premium relation as its own (unchanged from Cycle 32). (iv) The candidate may not
  substitute any published crowding proxy for the missing ETF → AP mapping. (v) The candidate may not
  use a short-interest-based measure of AP activity as a substitute for the missing mapping (S-241,
  S-242, S-243).

#### Cycles 41–51 consolidated note — IDEA-010 · G-29 closed, G-30 opened and then **exhausted without a re-probe**
- Across Cycles 41–51 the register worked this candidate's route ladder explicitly and, in order: **G-29**
  was executed as a calibrated document-level search (Cycle 40) and the **statutory-filing route closed**;
  **G-30** was opened to ask for a *specific* remaining route — a sponsor's own published participant list,
  an exchange or regulatory disclosure requirement, or a non-US issuer's prospectus. Cycles 41–43 then
  exhausted those three legs: **regulatory — closed** (no SEC, exchange or other rule obliges an issuer to
  publish its ETF's AP set); **sponsor-published lists — not found**; **non-US / UCITS issuers — not
  found**; **exchange listing rules — no public disclosure requirement**. Cycles 44–51 added the family
  screens **R-022 … R-031** and the register-wide evidence-currency passes, and **no new route to a
  corporate-bond ETF's AP set was identified in any of them**. The standing instruction from Cycle 40
  therefore holds: *do not re-run statutory filings, and do not substitute a published crowding proxy*
  (S-026, S-157, S-222 measure different crowds).
- **G-31 — the sub-route research identifier for G-30's legs (b) and (c).** *(Recorded in Cycle 135; the
  identifier's own status was confirmed from `cycle_notes.md` and was until then absent from this register.)*
  **G-31 was opened in Cycle 41** and was the register's identifier for *G-30 sub-routes (b) and (c) research*
