- **Score: 68.0, unchanged. Rank 3, unchanged.**

#### Cycle 52 block — IDEA-010 · G-30 **exhaustion confirmed, no re-probe**, and V-010 remains non-executable against the corporate-bond ETF universe
- **What Cycle 52 did.** It **re-derived** G-30's status rather than carrying it forward in prose: all four
  legs remain closed (regulatory: Cycle 41; sponsor-published lists: Cycles 42–43; non-US/UCITS issuers:
  Cycle 42; exchange listing rules: Cycle 43), and **no new route was identified in Cycles 44–51 or in
  Cycle 52**. **No statutory filing was re-run in Cycle 52**, and no crowding proxy was substituted.
  **G-30 is exhausted; V-010 remains non-executable against the corporate-bond ETF universe.** V-010 stays
  executable only against a specifiable route, and when it is eventually executed it must report the
  effective-sample denominator in the Cycle-40 form (cells per (ETF, AP set) per filing date, with the
  annual cadence stated).
- **What Cycle 52 added that is *not* about G-30, and it is a rediscovery-guard point for this candidate.**
  The R-032 family screen established the register's general **"published state ⇒ no informational edge"**
  principle on a second, independent body of evidence: the LULD bands are republished to the public every
  30 seconds (S-260) and the EU regime *requires* publication of trading halts (S-261), so the state is a
  broadcast and not a discovery. Applied to IDEA-010 this is a **guard on a substitution the candidate has
  already been warned against**: a hypothetical ETF → AP mapping built from a **published, real-time
  by-product** — for example a venue's published AP identifier codes on executed ETF creations — would not
  be a state that produces an edge; it would be a public record of what has already happened. The
  candidate's edge is claimed to come from **AP balance-sheet capacity**, not from *knowing* the AP set, so
  this does not close the candidate. But it does add a sixth hard-stop condition: **the state used must not
  be a public real-time by-product of the flow it is supposed to predict.**
- **Score: 68.0, unchanged. Rank 3, unchanged.** No source bears on any score component; G-30's status
  changed from "open" to "**exhausted without a re-probe**", which is a change in the register's knowledge
  about the *route*, not about the candidate's merit.

#### Cycle 69 block — IDEA-010 · evidence-currency re-read (2025–2026); adds published intermediary-risk channel and quantifies AP-capital effect
- **New source S-323 (Dinger et al. 2025, FoFI):** Corporate bond ETFs exhibit **lower liquidity risk but higher intermediary risk** than underlying bond portfolios; ETFs are more exposed to intermediation supply shocks, bonds to demand shocks. Using an **AP-capital factor**, a **one-standard-deviation deterioration in active AP balance sheets raises ETF–NAV differentials by nearly 10% of a standard deviation**. Structure is highly concentrated: **~30 registered APs**, only **~4 active per year**, top-3 typically primary broker-dealers handling **~95% of creation/redemption volume**. The paper documents an **intermediary-risk premium** for ETFs (average expected excess return 3.5% vs 2.0% for underlying portfolios) with realized returns nearly identical (negative alpha ~ -1.4%, insignificant).
- **Effect on dossier:** Strengthens the *state's* sign-determining power (AP capital) and adds a published **intermediary-risk premium** baseline the candidate must beat; concentration numbers reinforce fragility/capacity constraints already noted. No score change (68.0), rank 3 unchanged. G-30 remains exhausted (no public AP mapping exists). V-010 remains non-executable. Hard-stop flags unchanged.

#### Cycle 76 block — IDEA-010 · the handed-on trigger-based re-read is executed (S-331 line-verified); no score change (68.0), rank 2 unchanged; two new hard-stop flags; gap G-34 opened
- **What this cycle did.** Cycle 75 registered a **trigger** for this candidate — a published result that
  touches the candidate's flow-impact premise (S-331) — and **handed the re-read on** because S-331 was
  index-rendered and the register's rule forbids using an index-rendered source as a verification basis.
  Cycle 76 removed that precondition: **S-331 was line-verified** (the author-hosted PDF fetched directly,
  HTTP 200, 978,520 bytes, 63 pages, text extracted with `pypdf` — the same method already used for S-323
  in Cycle 75; the Cycle-75 failure was a **tooling gap, not a document defect**), and the re-read was
  then executed. The cycle also ran a **verification pass over this candidate's load-bearing published
  neighbours**, which upgraded **S-195** (Pan & Zeng) to partly line-verified and produced a
  **quotation-provenance correction** (recorded in the ledger), and recorded a documented **earlier
  working-paper version of S-194** with a *different* state variable.
- **What S-331 publishes, and it is now quotable from the document.** "Mutual fund flows generate
  substantial price impact, amplify fragility, and trigger fire sales. We document a stark contrast for
  separate accounts—the dominant investment vehicle for institutional investors: separate account flows
  generate essentially no price impact, fragility, or fire-sale risk." For the mutual-fund sector: "when
  the aggregate mutual fund sector buys 1% of a stock's shares outstanding in response to flows, the stock
  experiences a 1.64% contemporaneous price run-up (t-statistic = 5.80), followed by a significant
  reversal". For the intermediated channel: "we find that these trades executed by the transition manager
  generate statistically insignificant and economically negligible price impact"; "these specialized
  intermediaries reduce trading costs by 80% to 90%"; "transaction costs for transition programs are only
  about 10%–20% of those borne by mutual funds". And the principle the candidate turns on: "the price
  impact of investor demand depends critically on institutional structure". **Domain limit, confirmed in the
  document itself:** the paper's object is **US equities** and the **mutual-fund versus separate-account**
  comparison — the strings "ETF", "exchange-traded" and "corporate bond" occur nowhere in the extracted
  text outside the reference list — so the transfer to a **corporate-bond ETF's creation/redemption
  channel is the register's inference, not a result of the paper**.
- **What the re-read concluded — the trigger does not change the score, but it changes the candidate's
  standing.** The published result is a **neighbour of the candidate's mechanism, not the candidate's own
  relation**: it establishes that the *executor* and its constraints decide whether a flow is price-moving,
  and that a flow routed through a specialised intermediary **with spare capacity** has negligible price
  impact. Read against the candidate, that is **two-sided**, and both sides are recorded:
  1. **Supportive of the candidate's existence premise, in a different asset class and vehicle.** The
     candidate's claim is that a creation/redemption flow is price-moving *more* when the AP — itself a
     specialised intermediary — is balance-sheet constrained. S-331's published principle is that the
     executor's constraints, not the dollar, decide the impact, which is the candidate's logic in general
     form. This is **family-level support**, which by the register's standing rule (Cycle 35/52) does not
     move a rubric dimension.
  2. **A rival explanation for the candidate's effect.** If a flow executed by a specialised intermediary
     moves prices little, then the ETF–NAV widening observed under AP stress need not be a *temporary
     price-pressure dislocation* that a liquidity supplier can monetise; it may be a **mechanical
     consequence of intermediated creation/redemption pricing** — the same family as the published
     fractional-basket account (S-196). The candidate's payoff depends on the *transient* reading, so this
     is a competing account of the candidate's own outcome, and the candidate now has to pre-register a
     discriminator against it.
- **What S-195 adds once it is line-verified (two findings that were invisible while index-rendered).**
  (i) **A published competitor conditioner of the same relation**: "the sensitivity of ETF arbitrage by
  APs is declining in market volatility, bond market illiquidity and costs of trade", with the empirical
  form "a one standard deviation increase in market volatility generates a 10% decline in AP arbitrage" and
  the asymmetry "the decrease in arbitrage sensitivity is asymmetrically larger when the ETF premium is
  negative". This conditioner is **available at decision time** in the candidate's universe, whereas the
  candidate's own state (the AP's regulatory capital ratio) is **not** attachable to a corporate-bond ETF
  from public filings (G-29, G-30) — so the published competitor is *reachable* and the candidate's
  differentiator is not. It is written to the known-baseline table as **row 83**.
  (ii) **A published sign-reversal mechanism**: "ETF arbitrage may go in the opposite direction than what
  would be implied by the initial relative mispricing", i.e. a published instance of the exact risk the
  candidate's rule faces — that the entity the rule supplies liquidity to may trade *against* the
  mispricing it is meant to correct.
- **Two new hard-stop flags.**
  1. *The candidate may not present the ETF–NAV widening as a price-pressure effect without pre-registering
  a discriminator against the published intermediary-attenuation account (S-331) and the published
  mechanical-pricing account (S-196); the transient reading must be separated from the mechanical one
  before any claim on the concession is made.*
  2. *The candidate may not be presented as uniquely state-conditioned: a published competitor conditioner
  of the same arbitrage relation — market volatility, bond-market illiquidity and trading costs (S-195) — is
  available at decision time, while the candidate's own AP-capital state is not attachable in the candidate's
  universe (G-29, G-30).*
- **Gap G-34 opened, with a pre-registered kill condition.** Is there published work that (a) decomposes a
  corporate-bond ETF's ETF–NAV discount into a **transient** (price-pressure, mean-reverting) component and
  a **mechanical** (AP intermediation, basket composition, creation/redemption mechanics) component, and
  (b) identifies a **published, decision-time** state that discriminates between the two **in the corporate-
  bond ETF universe** and **without** the ETF→AP mapping that G-29/G-30 established is unavailable? **Kill
  condition:** if neither the decomposition nor a decision-time discriminating state is published, the
  candidate's residual is **unmeasurable as specified** — the same classification the register applied to
  IDEA-004 in Cycle 75 — and IDEA-010 must be **re-classified (downgraded or parked), not re-specified**
  around a weaker state. **G-34 does not unblock V-010**, which remains non-executable while G-29 and G-30
  stand; its purpose is to decide the candidate's *classification*, not to unlock a validation.
- **What was not done, deliberately.** G-30 was **not** re-probed (no statutory filing re-run, no published
  crowding proxy substituted); the crowding-measure literature was not searched; the frozen and dated items
  (G-28, G-21, G-30, IDEA-014's limb, G-32 limb (c), G-33's 2027 Q3 re-check, P-010's 2029 date) were
  maintained; and no `S-`, `V-` or `R-` identifier was consumed. The register's "evidence-currency sweep
  of the five active candidates is complete for this generation" claim is **restored** for IDEA-010, so it
  stands for all five again — subject to the standing trigger rule.
- **Score: 68.0, unchanged. Rank 2, unchanged.** Rubric unchanged: mechanism 4, evidence 4, novelty 1,
  falsifiability 4, resilience 3, data 4. **The trigger is a published neighbour of the mechanism, not the
  candidate's own relation**, so the novelty floor (1) cannot fall further, the evidence reading does not
  move on family-level support, and neither the falsifiability nor the resilience dimension moves on a
  result that adds a *rival* account of the candidate's outcome — the falsifiability gain (a named
  discriminator now exists to pre-register) and the resilience risk (the transient reading may be wrong) are
  opposite-signed and roughly offsetting, so neither is recorded as a change.

#### Cycle 77 block — IDEA-010 · G-34 executed as a bounded literature operation; the pre-registered kill condition FIRES
- **What was executed, and under what rule.** G-34 was opened in Cycle 76 with a two-part question and a
  **pre-registered decision rule**: is there published work that (a) decomposes a corporate-bond ETF's
  ETF–NAV discount into a **transient** (price-pressure, mean-reverting) component and a **mechanical**
  (AP intermediation, basket composition, creation/redemption mechanics) component, and (b) identifies a
  **published, decision-time** state that discriminates between the two **in the corporate-bond ETF
  universe** and **without** the ETF→AP mapping that G-29/G-30 established is unavailable? **Kill
  condition:** if neither the decomposition nor a decision-time discriminating state is published, the
  candidate's residual is **unmeasurable as specified** and the candidate must be **re-classified
  (downgraded or parked), not re-specified** around a weaker state. G-34 did **not** unblock V-010,
  which remained non-executable because G-29 and G-30 stand.
- **What the bounded search found.** **Nothing on either limb.** No published work decomposes a
  corporate-bond ETF's ETF–NAV discount into a transient and a mechanical component, and no published
  work identifies a **decision-time** state that discriminates between the two in that universe without
  the ETF→AP mapping. What the search did instead return were the **already-registered published
  accounts** of the candidate's outcome, each of which stands on the *mechanical* side and none of which
  supplies a discriminator:
  - the **fractional-basket account** (S-196), in which the discount arises from basket composition with
    no AP constraint involved;
  - the **intermediation-attenuation account** (S-331), in which a flow executed by a specialised
    intermediary with spare capacity has negligible price impact, so the widening need not be a
    transient dislocation at all;
  - the **funding-segmentation account** (S-197), in which the *funding source* rather than the AP's
    bond-inventory capacity drives an arbitrage spread;
  - the **client-liquidity-discount account** (S-199), in which a concession is paid to whoever supplies
    liquidity, whoever that is;
  - the **competitor conditioner** (S-195), which conditions the same arbitrage relation on market
    volatility, bond-market illiquidity and trading costs — all **reachable at decision time** where the
    candidate's own state is not.
- **Verdict.** **The kill condition fired.** The candidate's residual — the *transient* reading of the
  ETF–NAV widening, on which the whole payoff depends — is **unmeasurable as specified**, and the
  candidate's state is **not attachable to its own universe** (G-29: 0 corporate-bond ETFs with a
  published AP set; G-30: exhausted across all four outside routes). **No score changed in Cycle 77**;
  the candidate's **re-classification** was registered as gap **G-35** and executed in Cycle 78.
- **What was not done, deliberately.** G-30 was **not** re-probed, no statutory filing was re-run, no
  published crowding proxy was substituted, and no re-specification was written.

#### Cycle 78 block — IDEA-010 · the kill condition is executed: the candidate is **PARKED (P-014)**, terminal score **55.0**
- **The decision, and why it is *park* rather than *downgrade*.** The Cycle-76 decision rule offered two
  lawful outcomes, "downgraded or parked", and the register's own precedents separate them:
  1. **IDEA-004 (Cycle 75) — the "unmeasurable rather than unclaimed" case.** Nothing published shows the
     candidate's own effect, and the residual is not measurable; the candidate therefore **stayed active**
     at a reduced score, because a claim that is unmeasured but *unclaimed* is still research work.
  2. **IDEA-003 (P-012) — the "known baseline with an unvalidated state overlay" case.** The candidate's
     measurable content was published and its state overlay was not validated; the candidate was
     **parked**.
  3. **IDEA-002 (P-004 → R-038) — the "state not attachable to its own cross-section" case.** Parked in
     Cycle 58 and **rejected** in the same cycle, because the candidate's state cannot be attached to the
     very cross-section the claim is about.
  IDEA-010 now satisfies **case 3** on the state limb (the ETF→AP mapping does not exist publicly for
  any corporate-bond ETF — G-29 coverage 0, G-30 exhausted across four routes) *and* **case 2** on the
  residual limb (the relation is published — S-194, S-195, S-323 — and the transient-versus-mechanical
  discriminator that would make the candidate's own content measurable is not published, G-34). It does
  **not** satisfy the rejection test, which the register applies only when a published result **shows the
  candidate's own effect**: no published work shows that a joint AP-capacity × redemption-flow rule
  predicts an ETF–NAV widening that mean-reverts. **The candidate is therefore parked, not rejected** —
  unsupported and unranked, not falsified.
- **Why not a downgrade instead.** A downgrade would keep a candidate in the active ranking whose two
  distinguishing claims cannot be tested as specified: the *joint state conditioning* (the state is
  unattachable in the candidate's universe) and the *transient-versus-mechanical discrimination* (no
  published discriminator exists, and the mechanical reading cannot be excluded). Keeping it ranked would
  price as research priority a claim that the register has just recorded as unmeasurable — which is the
  substance of the Cycle-75 IDEA-004 distinction the Cycle-76 rule invoked, and which here resolves the
  other way because the state limb has failed outright rather than remained an open measurement question.
- **The terminal rubric, dimension by dimension.**
  - **Mechanism 4 → 4.** The mechanism is published (S-195, now partly line-verified; S-323) and the
    Cycle-76/77/78 evidence does not change it.
  - **Evidence 4 → 4.** The published evidence family is unchanged (S-194, S-195, S-323 and the five
    rivals). Per the standing Cycle-35/52 precedent, family-level support does not move this dimension,
    and the decision does not rest on a new source.
  - **Novelty 1 → 1.** Already at the floor; the candidate's measurable content was published in Cycle 32.
  - **Falsifiability 4 → 2.** The dimension has two parts — *clarity of the predictions* (which survives:
    the predictions are sharp and the kill conditions are explicit) and *feasibility of a clean test*
    (which now fails in the candidate's own universe: the state cannot be attached, and the discriminator
    does not exist). This is the register's IDEA-014 precedent — a state failure costing falsifiability
    points — applied to a state failure of the stronger kind.
  - **Resilience 3 → 2.** Cycle 76 declined to move resilience because the falsifiability gain (a named
    discriminator to pre-register) roughly offset the resilience risk (the transient reading may be
    wrong). That offset is gone: the discriminator is not merely unnamed, it is **unpublished**, so the
    mechanical reading (S-196, S-331) cannot be excluded in advance, and the published sign-reversal
    mechanism (S-195) stands as a live failure mode.
  - **Data 4 → 2.** The data dimension was raised to 4 in Cycle 32 *because* the state construct is
    publicly reconstructable from regulatory filings. That rationale is **false for the candidate's own
    universe**: the capital state is public, but the ETF→AP mapping is not (G-29: 0 corporate-bond ETFs
    with a published AP set; G-30: exhausted). The candidate cannot be constructed at all, which is a
    two-point fall, not a one-point fall.
  - **Weighted total: (4×25 + 4×20 + 1×15 + 2×15 + 2×15 + 2×10) / 5 = 275 / 5 = 55.0.** The candidate is
    **unranked**; 55.0 is its terminal research-priority reading, not a probability of profit.
- **Effect on the artifacts.** (i) The dossier is at **v1.3 (terminal)**, status **parked (P-014)**.
  (ii) The parking record **P-014** is written in `rejected_and_parked.md`, with its unblocking condition
  and its prohibitions. (iii) **V-010 is marked never-to-execute** — it was never executed here and must
  not be executed while the candidate is parked. (iv) The shortlist entry moves to the parked section at
  55.0. (v) The mechanism is written into the **known-baseline table** as a rediscovery guard. (vi) The
  **Cycle-77 block above is backfilled** into this dossier: it had been executed and recorded in the
  handoff and the ledger's Cycle-77 summary, but never written into the dossier body.
- **The unblocking condition, in one line.** IDEA-010 could be re-admitted only if published work
  (i) attaches a capital or capacity state to a **corporate-bond** ETF by a route that does not require
  the ETF→AP mapping — a sponsor-published participant list, a venue or regulatory disclosure
  requirement, a non-US issuer's prospectus, or a validated public proxy of AP/dealer capacity — **and**
  (ii) identifies a **published, decision-time** state that separates the transient reading of the
  ETF–NAV discount from the mechanical one, **or** publishes a null on the transient reading in that
  universe (which would reject the candidate outright). Until then the candidate stays parked, and it
  must **not** be re-specified around a weaker state.

