### Cycle 109 — P-3 screen: is the "first adopter versus repeat adopter" classification available at the adoption date? (bounded negative on the prospective arm; bounded positive on the historical comparator; logged as R-057)

- **The question, pre-registered, and what it is not.** This is **not** a re-run of G-21 (the literature
  search is exhausted and the comparator being unrun and unused is settled) and **not** a re-run of G-32
  limb (a). It is a **state-availability** question in the form R-056 established, asked of the
  candidate's own **discriminator**: the candidate's claim is a **comparability clock** — the drift in
  reported-earnings information quality is larger in **first-time adopters** of a standard whose transition
  creates a comparability break than in **repeat (second-time) adopters** — so the question is whether that
  classification is **determinable at the decision date** or only with hindsight. The **falsification
  condition was fixed in advance**: a **published, prospective adoption calendar that fixes first-adopter
  status before the effective date for the candidate's jurisdictions** would overturn a negative; and the
  pre-registration named in advance the three things that do **not** count — a mandatory application date
  (a deadline, not a status), a voluntary early-adoption permission (self-selected and announced
  firm-by-firm after the fact), and a status directory (status in principle, no dated schedule).
- **Limb A — POSITIVE at the framework level, NEGATIVE at the standard level — and the register's own
  record was too pessimistic on the first.** A **route form the register had not tried in thirteen cycles**
  produced the evidence: the IFRS Foundation's profile *pages* are client-side rendered, and each
  profile's *content* is served as a per-jurisdiction **PDF** at
  `https://www.ifrs.org/content/dam/ifrs/publications/jurisdictions/pdf-profiles/<jurisdiction>-ifrs-profile.pdf`.
  Four were fetched directly and text-extracted — European Union (**HTTP 200, 149,740 bytes, 8 pages,
  19,713 characters**), Saudi Arabia (**HTTP 200, 126,760 bytes, 6 pages, 14,921 characters**), Nigeria
  (**HTTP 200, 119,901 bytes, 6 pages, 12,870 characters**), Ghana (**HTTP 200, 115,469 bytes, 4 pages,
  9,175 characters**) — and they carry a **dated framework-level first-adoption fact**: the EU "adopted
  IFRS Standards as the required financial reporting standards … **effective in 2005**" (profile last
  updated **2 October 2024**); Ghana's Institute "adopted IFRS Standards in place of Ghana National
  Accounting Standards" in **2007**, effective **1 January 2007** (last updated **16 June 2016**); Nigeria's
  Federal Executive Council "approved **1 January 2012** as the effective date for adoption of IFRS Standards
  in Nigeria" on **28 July 2010** (last updated **16 June 2016**). So the **historical** first-time-versus-
  repeat label is **published, dated, standard-setter material** — the register's S-254 caveat is
  **narrowed in place** accordingly. But **no profile carries an IFRS 18 date, or even an IFRS 18 mention**
  ("IFRS 18" **0 hits** in all four), and the profiles' own currency dates (**16 June 2016**, **28 July
  2022**, **2 October 2024**) predate or barely postdate IFRS 18's April 2024 issuance; the **Saudi Arabia**
  profile — the register's own group-A candidate — gives **no** first-adoption date at all, its "Additional
  comments" field reading "**Not applicable**". **G-32's limb (a) closure therefore stands, on a stronger
  ground**: the negative is now a property of the instrument, not of the way the instrument was read.
- **Limb B — NEGATIVE for the prospective arm.** The only published instrument that partitions *firms* in
  the candidate's live window is **Saudi Arabia's CMA early-adoption permission (S-270)**, and its own text
  settles the question: listed joint-stock companies "**may early adopt** IFRS 18 … during 2026 **by
  announcing financial statements prepared in accordance with that Standard and the effect of such early
  adoption on the Saudi Exchange (Tadawul) website**". The arm is **self-selected**; the **only** disclosure
  is an **ex-post announcement of the early-adopted financial statements**; there is **no pre-announcement
  list, no registry of intent and no declaration date**. Which firms will early-adopt is therefore **not
  knowable at any decision date before 1 January 2027**. The **supervisor's own instrument** is no better:
  the **ESMA public statement (S-267)**, re-fetched directly this cycle (**HTTP 200, 390,994 bytes, 10
  pages, 36,978 characters**), is jurisdiction-uniform and contains **no** first-time/repeat language at all
  ("first time" **0 hits**, "first-time" **0**, "early adopt" **0**, "repeat" **0**, "voluntar" **0**,
  "jurisdiction" **0**, "calendar" **0**): it fixes the **deadline** ("effective date of application of the
  Standard is 1 January 2027 with earlier application [permitted]"), exactly as the pre-registration said
  it would. A second structural fact reinforces the negative: where a jurisdiction is a genuine first-time
  adopter of the IFRS *package* in the IFRS 18 era, the whole package arrives at once (**S-271**, Saudi
  Arabia's SOCPA), so the **standard-specific** comparability break is **not separable** from the package
  break.
- **Limb C — POSITIVE for the framework-level classifier, NEGATIVE for the firm-level voluntary flag.** The
  framework-level classifier is published, dated and free. The firm-level "who is a repeat adopter"
  *voluntary* flag is self-selected and ex-post.
- **The historical comparator is bounded POSITIVE, with one named residual — and this is the finding that
  matters for the register's open work.** The discriminator the *unrun* comparison (G-21) needs is the label
  "had this firm already applied IFRS before the new standard arrived?", and for the 2005 event that label
  is derivable **at the decision date** from two published sources: the **jurisdiction's own dated
  first-adoption fact** (above) and **each firm's own published prior-year basis-of-preparation note**,
  which states the framework applied in the comparative period. So the G-21 comparison can be specified
  **without a hindsight-classification objection**, and the specification should say so explicitly. The
  **named residual**, which lies **outside this cycle's pre-registered execution bounds** and was therefore
  **not examined**: the IASB's **published list of entities permitted to apply IFRS early in 2005**, which
  would fix the firm-level "repeat adopter" flag *prospectively* rather than by derivation.
- **Effect on the candidate and on the artifacts.** **No score change (69.0), no rank change, no tie-break
  change, no hard-stop flag change, no gap opened or closed, no re-screen triggered, and no validation
  specification changed.** The screen is logged as **R-057** in `rejected_and_parked.md` with its
  re-screen conditions; the evidence lands in **S-254** (upgrade and scope correction) and in the
  re-reads of **S-267** and **S-270**.
- **Dated re-screen condition (restated in full in R-057).** Re-screen only if (a) the **IFRS Foundation
  publishes an IFRS 18 adoption calendar** with application dates; or (b) a **prospective early-adoption
  registry** is published before 1 January 2027; or (c) the **IASB's 2005 early-adopter list** is obtained
  and read, converting the historical comparator's positive limb from "derivable from published accounts" to
  "published prospectively".
- **What this screen does *not* touch.** The candidate's **live** conditioning state — the enforcement
  regime and the reporting-quality environment in the transition year — is separately and already
  decision-time observable (the venue's inspection and enforcement regime; the audit-committee changes in
  the published 2026 interim reports, S-281 … S-286). The screen bears on the **discriminator**, not on the
  state the candidate trades on.

### Cycle 111 — Priority 1 audit: the R-057 residual is closed (R-058); the dossier's comparator wording is reviewed and left unchanged

> **Cycle-112 label repair (21 October 2026).** This block was executed in **Cycle 111**, not Cycle 112:
> its own text says "The Cycle-110 handoff named … Cycle 110 executed that search and logged the outcome as
> R-058", and R-058 is the Cycle-110 rejection. The heading's "Cycle 112" was an off-by-one labelling error,
> made in the same session that wrote the block. The heading is relabelled in place, with the body untouched.
> This is defect 1 of the five label-and-header defects repaired in Cycle 112 (see `cycle_notes.md`).

- **What this cycle did.** The Cycle-110 handoff named the IASB's published list of entities permitted to
  apply IFRS early in 2005 as the P-3 screen's one residual. Cycle 110 executed that search and logged the
  outcome as **R-058** in `rejected_and_parked.md`, but the result was **never written into this dossier**,
  so the dossier still described the residual as open. This cycle writes it in.
- **The outcome, as recorded in R-058.** **Bounded negative.** Routes tried: web search over three query
  forms ("IASB published list entities permitted apply IFRS early 2005", "IASB early adopters 2005 list",
  "IFRS 1 early adoption list 2005"); inspection of IFRS Foundation historical publications (the 2003 Annual
  Report, the 2005 Annual Report), IOSCO 2005 statements, and the IFRS 1 standard page and history; and
  Wayback/archive checks for IASB pages from 2004–2005. **No published firm-level early-adopter registry
  surfaced.** The framework-level first-adoption facts are published (the dated jurisdiction facts in
  S-254), but the prospective firm-level list for 2005 could not be located.
- **Effect on this candidate.** The historical comparator's positive limb is therefore **final**: the
  "had this firm already applied IFRS before the new standard arrived?" label is derivable **at the
  decision date** from the jurisdiction's own dated first-adoption fact and **each firm's own published
  prior-year basis-of-preparation note**, and there is **no third instrument** that would fix it
  prospectively. The comparator remains **specifiable without a hindsight-classification objection**, which
  is the property the G-21 hand-off to validation needed, and it is now **closed** rather than open with a
  named residual. **No re-run of the literature search** (G-21 is exhausted; the register rule forbids
  re-running exhausted routes).
- **V-009 review — one superseded-in-place defect found and annotated, no specification amended.** The
  Cycle-52 block of V-009 item 4 still instructed a validator working an IFRS 18 instance to **pre-register
  the group A/group B partition**, an instruction the Cycle-56 amendment closed and the Cycle-59 hand-off
  superseded; the block carried no annotation, so a reader following the specification as written would
  pre-register a partition the register has rejected. Annotated in place in `validation_handoffs.md` with a
  dated correction notice. **V-009 is not amended** — the executable set, the pre-registrations, the
  hypothesis, the cost model and the kill criteria are unchanged, and **no `V-` identifier was consumed**.
- **What this audit does not change.** **Score 69.0, rank 1, unchanged.** No gap opened or closed, no
  re-screen triggered, no validation specification created, amended or withdrawn, and no source minted.
  The candidate's **live** state (enforcement regime, reporting-quality environment in the transition year)
  is untouched, as is the Cycle-56 prospective arm A and its G-33 dependency.

#### Cycle 113 block — IDEA-011 · the Priority-2 post-2010 re-read returns a **null on the score**, with three new entries, one **adverse premise reading of the already-registered S-267**, one **documented competing conditioner**, and a new dated monitor; **no score change (69.0, rank 1)**; one new hard-stop condition and one new V-009 control

- **What was executed.** The Cycle-113 pre-registration named a bounded re-read of this candidate's nearest
  published test, on three routes: (i) the nearest published post-2010 test of the *accounting-information*
  content of an IFRS-18-style reclassification; (ii) citations to, and extensions of, **S-253** (the
  register's 2025–2026 systematic review of the accounting-adoption literature); and (iii) anything that
  bears on the candidate's *premise* rather than on its implementation. Three new sources were registered
  (**S-406, S-407, S-408**), and the third route produced a finding inside an **already-registered** source.
- **Route (i) — the nearest published test: S-406 (Cui, Lin & Yang 2026, *Accounting Horizons*, DOI
  10.2308/horizons-2025-080; line-verified via the Crossref API including the publisher's abstract).** The
  study "examines whether classifying earnings into operating, investing, and financing categories, based on
  the IFRS 18 framework, improves earnings informativeness for U.S. firms", using "Compustat data from 2003
  to 2023" and reclassifying "GAAP-based earnings components into IFRS 18 categories … while holding
  recognition and measurement constant". Its result: the classification "yields **modest improvements** in
  out-of-sample forecasts of future performance and returns" and "produces **statistically significant,
  although economically small**, improvements in explanatory power for stock returns, analyst forecast
  accuracy and dispersion, as well as higher cross-firm comparability". **Verdict: adverse to the premise's
  *magnitude*, and it is the best kind of evidence for the register's own purposes — a peer-reviewed,
  post-2010, DOI-bearing test of the nearest thing to the candidate's mechanism — but it is NOT a null and
  NOT a sign reversal, and the Cycle-66 precedent (which is the register's applied rule) moves a cell only on
  a published null or a sign reversal. The score therefore does not move.** Two further facts constrain how
  far this source can bear: its design is a **reclassification of historical GAAP data**, so it is *not*
  post-implementation market-effect evidence, and it is a test of the *informativeness of the subtotals*,
  not of a post-adoption *price drift*.
- **Route (ii) — two new deposits, one of which is a competing-conditioner record.** **S-407** (De Melo
  Gaspar Neto 2025, Harvard Dataverse; line-verified) is a multi-jurisdiction econometric study: a "hybrid
  sample of **425 companies (85 per country)**" from "the United Kingdom, Australia, Canada, South Korea,
  and Brazil" over "2015–2024", using "a multilevel econometric strategy combining triple fixed-effects
  panel models with difference-in-differences approaches, instrumental variables, and mediation analysis", and
  reporting that "the adoption of IFRS 18 is associated with a **42.3 % reduction in cross-country
  dispersion of operating profit** (p < 0.01), an effect mediated by **65 %** through the quality of
  institutional enforcement", with effects "**73 % larger** in jurisdictions with high-quality enforcement", a
  "**58.7 % contraction** in heterogeneous Management Performance Measures (MPMs) intensity" and a "**34.5 %
  increase** in the value relevance of standardized operating profit". **S-408** (GECAT 2026, Harvard
  Dataverse; line-verified) is a newly deposited **protocol-level systematic review** of the *sibling* IFRS
  15 literature, carrying "Search and screening protocols used in the systematic review process", "Coding
  instruments and extraction frameworks", "Comparative summaries of prior literature" and "Study
  classification and identification diagnostics" — the concrete content of this route, and a reusable frame
  rather than evidence. **Both are grey literature** (author-deposited, single-file or unrefereed, no citation
  record), and **S-407's sample (2015–2024) precedes IFRS 18's 1 January 2027 effective date**, so its
  "adoption" is a proxied one; the register therefore treats it as **mechanism-level and as a
  competing-conditioner record**, not as a test of the candidate's conditional claim.
- **The finding that matters most for this candidate is inside S-407: institutional enforcement quality is
  now a *documented competing conditioner* in precisely the multi-jurisdiction design this candidate's
  comparator would use.** The candidate's first-adopter/second-adopter contrast (G-21) assumes that the
  *difference* in post-adoption drift is attributable to the *information-quality* difference between
  first and repeat adopters. S-407 reports that the effect of the same standard is **65 % mediated by
  institutional enforcement quality** and **73 % larger** where enforcement is strong. A first-adopter
  jurisdiction and a repeat-adopter jurisdiction will differ in enforcement quality as well as in the
  candidate's conditioner, so the contrast is **confounded unless enforcement is controlled for**. This is
  registered as a **V-009 control requirement**, and it is *not* a score move: it changes the
  **interpretation** of the comparator, not the existence of the premise, and the register's G-12 ordering
  treats a conditioner that changes size as a control question rather than a falsification.
- **Route (iii) — the adverse premise reading, which falls on the ALREADY-REGISTERED S-267 and is recorded
  there rather than as a new source.** ESMA's public statement requires issuers to disclose "the expected
  material effects from the application of IFRS 18 in the interim and annual financial reports for periods
  ending before 1 January 2027" (S-267). That mandate is **constructive** — it is the register's existing
  basis for IDEA-011's prospective arm A and for **G-33**, because it makes the premise's *size* dated,
  firm-level and measurable — and it is **adverse**, because it publishes the *structure* of the accounting
  break **before** the event, which pre-empts the candidate's slow-diffusion premise. The mitigation is in the
  same source: the **mandated** content is qualitative — a description of the changes in the structure of the
  statement of profit or loss, the assessment of which activities are "specified main business activities",
  and the MPM definitions — while the *quantified* expected operating-profit change is only **encouraged**.
  So the pre-emption is **partial and structural, not quantitative**, and it weakens the premise in the
  **size** dimension, not the sign/existence dimension. Under the register's own G-12 ordering, and by the
  direct precedent of the Cycle-108 **S-387** finding (an adverse premise finding that moved no cell), the
  score does not move.
- **New hard-stop condition attached** (standing, and independent of the score): **the slow-diffusion
  premise must survive the ESMA-mandated pre-adoption disclosure.** If the market's information about the
  accounting break is published in advance of the effective date in a form that lets the *sign* of the
  comparability surprise be anticipated, the premise is consumed and the candidate's edge is a re-description
  of a known disclosure, not an information-diffusion event. This is now the candidate's **third** hard-stop
  condition, and it is the one the new source (S-406) makes urgent: if the accounting break is worth only
  "modest" and "economically small" amounts of information, there is very little to diffuse slowly.
- **V-009 gains one control requirement and one reporting arm, and is amended rather than re-created.**
  (i) **Competing-conditioner control:** the multi-jurisdiction comparator must control for institutional
  enforcement quality — the register's own comparator work already uses the enforcement-quality conditioner
  (S-252), and S-407 makes it mandatory rather than optional. (ii) **Pre-emption reporting arm:** the
  specification must report the event-window returns around the **first** disclosure of the accounting break
  — the **2026 interim reporting season** (periods ending **30 June 2026**), per S-267's timing note — as an
  explicit **pre-emption test**, and a significant pre-event drift must be reported as evidence that the
  slow-diffusion premise has **already been consumed** (a *kill* signal for the candidate), **not** as a null
  on the effect. No `V-` identifier is consumed and the executable set, pre-registrations, hypothesis, cost
  model and kill criteria are unchanged.
- **A new dated monitor, and it is the earliest observation date in this dossier.** S-267 expects the
  mandated pre-adoption-effect disclosures "in issuers' interim and annual financial reports for periods
  ending before 1 January 2027", and an issuer finalising its assessment in H1 2026 is expected to disclose
  in the interim statements for the period ending **30 June 2026**. The first ESMA-expected IFRS 18
  pre-disclosure cycle therefore lands in the **2026 interim reporting season** — earlier than the
  **2027 Q3** G-32 limb (c) / G-33 re-check, and earlier than the 2027 effective date. This is opened as a
  monitor in its own right.
- **G-21 remains unrun, deliberately.** The Cycle-52 corpus is exhausted and the register's rule forbids
  re-running exhausted routes; G-21 is a **validation** task on V-009, not a literature task, and this
  cycle's re-read was bounded to the three pre-registered routes.
- **Effect.** **No score change (69.0, rank 1 unchanged); no gap opened or closed** (G-21 unrun, G-32 limb
  (c) and G-33 dated, and the new monitor added); **no `IDEA-` identifier consumed**; **no `V-` identifier
  consumed**; three `S-` identifiers consumed (S-406, S-407, S-408).
#### Cycle 114 block — IDEA-011 · the 2026 interim reporting season has now happened: the third hard-stop condition is **carried forward unresolved**; the mandated limb is delivered in **sign-bearing but unquantified** form and **0 of 13** issuers quantify; a **new pre-emption channel** opened that S-267 did not name; **no published market reaction was found**; **no score change (69.0, rank 1)**; one new V-009 reporting requirement and one new dated monitor

- **What was executed.** The **Cycle-113 monitor** — "the 2026 interim reporting season (periods ending 30 June 2026), the earliest observation date in this dossier" — was run, on the three routes the Cycle-113 block implies: **(i)** the mandated pre-adoption-effect disclosures themselves; **(ii)** whether the **quantified** expected operating-profit change — the object of **G-33** — has been published at firm level; and **(iii)** any **published** market reaction or pre-event drift in the affected names. Five `S-` identifiers were consumed (**S-434 … S-438**), the already-registered **S-267** was re-fetched (a re-issued file, 379,979 bytes against 390,994 bytes, identical passages, no content correction) and annotated, and **no new `IDEA-` or `V-` identifier** was consumed.
- **Route (i) — the mandated limb is being delivered, and it is delivered in exactly the form S-267 predicted.** The register now holds **thirteen** first-observed disclosures from the 2026 interim season: **nine** filed with the US SEC as Form 6-K exhibits (**S-434** — On Holding AG, Rio Tinto Ltd, B2Gold Corp, Ballard Power Systems, Equinox Gold, Eco Wave Power, Western Copper & Gold, Kamada Ltd, SaverOne 2014 Ltd, all fetched directly from `www.sec.gov` and read) and **four** EU-side first observations already registered (**S-409** Bayer AG, **S-410** Novartis AG, **S-411** Nestlé Capital Corporation, **S-412** HSBC Holdings). The disclosures **exist** and they are **sign-bearing**: On Holding states that "certain income and expense amounts are expected to be reclassified within the consolidated statements of income" and that "**These expected presentation changes will not affect reported net income**"; Rio Tinto states "**Based on analysis to date, we expect that the adoption of IFRS 18 will change the current structure of the consolidated income statement, and how the operating profit is calculated**", naming the directions (net interest on pensions and healthcare moving to financing; interest income on receivables moving to operating; sub-lease income moving to investing; finance income and FX on bank balances moving to investing); Bayer states that the assessment is "**largely complete**" and that "**foreign exchange differences … will largely have to be reclassified from the financial result to the operating category**"; Nestlé Capital Corporation states that "**Operating profit for the Company is expected to remain broadly consistent with the current Operating profit figure**". **So the *direction* of the operating-profit effect is public, per issuer, before the effective date — which is the pre-emption S-267 warned of, and it is confirmed rather than newly discovered.**
- **Route (ii) — and the *quantified* limb is not delivered: 0 of 13.** **No issuer in the thirteen discloses a quantified expected effect on operating profit or on any other subtotal.** In **six of the nine** US-filed disclosures the assessment is explicitly **not concluded** — B2Gold: "**The Company is still continuing to assess and quantify the effect of IFRS 18 on our consolidated financial statements**"; Ballard: "**The impact of adoption of IFRS 18 is currently being assessed … Based on a preliminary assessment**"; Equinox: "**The Company is in the process of assessing … Based on its preliminary assessment, the Company has identified the following possible impacts**"; Eco Wave: "**The Company is currently assessing the new requirements of IFRS 18**"; Western Copper & Gold: "**Management is currently assessing the effect of this new standard on our financial statements**"; Kamada: "**The Company is currently assessing the impact of the Standard on its financial statements. As of June 30, 2026, the Company is currently evaluating the impact of this new standard.**" — a **seventh** (On Holding) is "**currently finalizing its assessment**". The register's Cycle-58 reading of G-33 was "**0 of 6** quantified"; **this cycle's observation extends it to 0 of 13, across two filing routes and two continents, and it is the direct evidence that the premise's *size* is still not published at firm level.** B2Gold's phrase is the sharpest single fact in the sample: the issuer that has reached the "**assess and quantify**" step has not published the quantity.
- **The dates, line-verified, and they are the load-bearing finding.** EY's transition calendar for a December year-end reporter (**S-435**, fetched directly) marks "**Q2 2024: IFRS 18 published**", "**01/01/2026: First comparative period impacted**", "**30/06/2026: First comparative reporting period end**", "**01/01/2027: Effective date**", "**30/06/2027: First interim reporting period end**", "**31/12/2027: First annual reporting period end**", "**03/2028: First annual financial statements issued**", with "**Comparative periods in the interim and annual financial statements are to be restated**" and "**IAS 34 requires presentation of each heading expected to be used and the subtotals required in IFRS 18**". Read with S-267's own statement that "the first IFRS 18 reporting cycle is the **2027 interim** reports, not the 2028 annual reports", this fixes the candidate's clock precisely: **the 2026 interim season was the *pre-adoption disclosure* season, and the first IFRS 18 financial statements for the mandatory population are the 2027 interim reports (periods ending 30 June 2027, published July–August 2027), with the first IFRS 18 annual statements in March 2028.** **The candidate's slow-diffusion window is therefore still open, and the premise is NOT consumed by the 2026 season on the route the hard-stop condition named.**
- **But a NEW pre-emption channel opened in 2026 that S-267 did not name, and it is a *magnitude* channel.** The early-adopter path publishes, twelve months ahead of the mandatory effective date, a **full IFRS 18 half-year report with the comparative period restated and audited** (**S-437**, index-rendered): "**First published IFRS 18 half-year report — 12 months ahead of mandatory adoption. Full comparative period restatement disclosed and audited.**" That is a **measured** operating-profit effect, in the primary statements, in the adopting subset, before 1 January 2027 — materially stronger pre-emption than S-267's *structural* pre-emption, and the register records it as **a new channel that the third hard-stop condition must now cover**. It is not established here that this channel pre-empts anything in price terms; it is established that the **information exists publicly, early, and in magnitude**. **The hard-stop condition is therefore re-specified** below.
- **Route (iii) — no published market reaction was found, so the kill signal has not fired — and this repository cannot test it.** Searches for published analyst, press or academic reaction to the 2026 IFRS 18 pre-adoption disclosures returned **anticipatory professional commentary only** (EY's transition deck, KPMG's "First Impressions" and bank/insurer guides, BDO's IFRB 2026/03 scenarios, PwC's in-brief, FTI Consulting's "IFRS 18: A New Era for Financial Disclosure" of 7 October 2026, which notes that "**IROs might expect that analysts and institutional investors will use the new reconciliation disclosures to scrutinise APMs more rigorously**"), and **no measurement of a price reaction, no drift estimate, and no published study of the 2026 disclosure season's market effect**. **This is a recorded negative on the literature, not a null on the effect** — the register's rule is that a *published* null is what moves a cell, and no published null or sign reversal exists. The market-side test is a **validation** task on V-009 and is **out of scope for this repository**, which does no market-data work.
- **A live monitor that the candidate's premise depends on and that was not previously on the register's list: the standard is still moving.** The IASB's July 2026 meeting discussed amendments to IFRS 18 on the classification of non-income-tax charges outside IAS 12, and the IFRS Interpretations Committee finalised an Agenda Decision in March 2026 that such charges fall in the operating category (**S-436**, index-rendered). A standard whose categories are still being amended is a standard whose "new numbers" are **less stable**, which lengthens rather than shortens a re-learning window but also adds a **revision risk** to any re-specification of the candidate's event. Recorded as a monitor, not a score input.
- **Third hard-stop condition, re-specified and carried forward (standing, and independent of the score).** As originally attached in Cycle 113: "**the slow-diffusion premise must survive the ESMA-mandated pre-adoption disclosure**". As re-specified by this cycle, the condition has **two limbs**, and the 2026 season settles the first and opens the second:
  - **Limb 1 (the mandated structural disclosure) — PASSED, on the quantity route.** The mandated limb is delivered and it is **structural and sign-bearing**; the **quantified** expected operating-profit change is **not** delivered by any of the thirteen issuers, so there is still **no published, decision-time, firm-level expected operating-profit change** to pre-empt the drift with. On the Cycle-113 wording this limb is **not consumed**.
  - **Limb 2 (the early-adopter restated comparative) — OPEN, and newly dated.** If the *measured* IFRS 18 operating-profit effect, published in early adopters' audited restated comparatives during 2026, produces a **pre-event drift** in the affected names, the premise is **consumed** and the candidate is a re-description of a known disclosure. The condition is **carried forward** and the observation date is set by the season, not by the effective date.
- **New dated monitor.** **The 2027 interim reporting season (periods ending 30 June 2027, published July–August 2027)** — the *first IFRS 18 reporting cycle* for the mandatory population, and the first date on which the candidate's slow-diffusion window can close on the mandated population. Alongside it, a **standing monitor on the early-adopter restated comparatives** published during 2026, which is the limb-2 pre-emption channel.
- **V-009 gains one reporting requirement and one reporting arm, and is amended rather than re-created.** (i) The **quantification rate** of the mandated pre-adoption disclosure becomes a **standing reported covariate**, to be re-estimated at each pre-adoption season: the value observed in the 2026 season is **0 of 13**, and a validator working arm A must report the rate in the season it uses, because **arm A's state construction depends entirely on that rate** (V-009's existing G-33 dependency). (ii) The **pre-emption reporting arm** is **split into two windows** — the **2026 interim disclosure window** (the filing dates of the 2026 season, 31 July – 14 August 2026 in the sample held at S-434) and the **2027 interim IFRS 18 window** — and a significant pre-event drift in either window must be reported as evidence that the slow-diffusion premise has **already been consumed** (a *kill* signal), **not** as a null on the effect. No `V-` identifier is consumed; the executable set, pre-registrations, hypothesis, cost model and kill criteria are unchanged.
- **G-21 remains unrun, deliberately**; **G-32 limb (c) and G-33 are not closed** — G-33's quantification-rate object is now **measured at 0 of 13** for the 2026 season and remains dated to the 2027 season.
- **Effect.** **No score change (69.0, rank 1 unchanged); no gap opened or closed; no `IDEA-` identifier consumed; no `V-` identifier consumed; five `S-` identifiers consumed (S-434 … S-438)** and one already-registered source re-verified (S-267).
#### Cycle 123 block — IDEA-011 · the early-adopter limb is now **attached to a named issuer** (S-437 line-verified, flatexDEGIRO) and the population-rate limb gains **two published population statistics** (S-452 DRSC DAX 40 survey; S-453 Norwegian FSA thematic review of a random sample of 50) — **no score change (69.0, rank 1)**; one prior-art flag added; one V-009 reporting requirement

- **What was executed.** Priority 3, the dossier's **two open limbs**: **(a)** the early-adopter restated comparative (S-437), which until this cycle was the register's weakest item on this dossier — **index-rendered, issuer unidentified, not line-verified**; and **(b)** the **2026 interim pre-adoption disclosure population rate**, the object of G-33. **Two `S-` identifiers were consumed (S-452, S-453)** and **one existing source was upgraded in place (S-437)**; **no `IDEA-`, `V-`, `R-`, `P-` or `G-` identifier was consumed.**
- **Limb (a) — S-437 is now line-verified, and the issuer is named.** The PDF was fetched directly (**HTTP 200, 742,791 bytes**, 14 pages) and its text layer was extracted and read. The three phrases the Cycle-114 block quoted are **confirmed in the document's own text** (re-checked space-insensitively against the extracted layer, because the extractor splits words at kerning boundaries): the timeline slide's "**Our adoption — Half Year 2026 — Early adoption ahead of peers — Jan 2027 Mandatory effective date — Jun 2027 Mandatory adoption — first financial statements under IFRS 18**"; the "**Strategic First Mover — Early adopters gain 18+ months to refine reporting systems before the rest of the market must comply**" claim; and the H1 2026 milestone, "**H1 2026 — Go Live — First published IFRS 18 half-year report 12 months ahead of mandatory adoption. Full comparative period restatement disclosed and audited.**" **The issuer is flatexDEGIRO** (Frankfurt am Main), identified from the document's own contact block — "**Investor Relations Contact flatexDEGIRO SE — Address: Omniturm, Große Gallusstraße 16 – 18, 60312 Frankfurt/Main, Germany**", with named IR contacts at `flatexdegiro.com` addresses, and the presenter "**Dr. Thomas Lindner, Head of Group Finance & Regulatory Reporting**". **Two new facts of record for this dossier.** (i) The early-adopter channel of the third hard-stop condition's **limb 2** is therefore attached to a **named issuer with a named document**, not to an anonymous deck: an early adopter published an IFRS 18 half-year report with a full comparative-period restatement, disclosed and audited, twelve months ahead of the mandatory effective date. (ii) **The issuer markets early adoption as a transparency and first-mover signal** — the deck's own framing is "**Investor Confidence — Demonstrating proactive transparency signals management**" — which is relevant to the candidate's **diffusion** side: if a first mover advertises the change, the information is pushed rather than merely diffused.
- **Limb (a) — the caveats, which travel with the finding.** The document is an **issuer's own IR deck**, not a filed financial statement, so it evidences that the early-adopter path *produces* the artefact, **n = 1**, and it says **nothing about any price response** — the pre-emption test itself remains a V-009 matter. The "audited" qualifier refers to the **comparative-period restatement**, not to an assurance opinion on the pre-adoption effect. The deck is a **December year-end reporter's** interim (H1 2026 against a 1 January 2027 effective date), so the restated comparative covers H1 2025. The PDF's document-metadata `Author` field reads "Monod, Giscard", which is **inconsistent with the document's content** and is recorded as a template artefact; the issuer identification rests on the document's own text, not on the metadata.
- **Limb (b) — G-33's object is now populated at population level, in two jurisdictions, one of them a random-sample supervisory review.** **(i) S-452 — DRSC's DAX 40 short survey** (survey fielded 21 April – 4 May 2026; 28 responses, a 70 % response rate; line-verified from the PDF's own text): "**a quarter**" of respondents expect "**rather strong**" or "**very strong**" effects of IFRS 18 on reported operating profit, while "**61 %**" expect only minor effects and "**11 %**" expect none; and on communication timing, "**43 %**" expect to inform the capital market during 2026, "**10 %**" have already done so, and "**23 %**" will not inform before 2027. **(ii) S-453 — the Norwegian FSA's thematic review** (21 October 2026; line-verified from the page's own text): of a "**random sample of 50 listed companies**", "**more than half**" of the 2025 annual reports "**either did not provide information on expected effects of IFRS 18, or only provided standardised disclosures**"; "**only 42 % provided company-specific information**"; "**few companies quantified the expected effects**"; and "**in the 2026 half-year reports only 8 % of companies had updated**" the information. The supervisor states the duty it expects to be met — "**Under IAS 8.30–31, companies must disclose the effects of published accounting standards that are not yet in force**" — and its own reading, that the reporting "**may not adequately reflect information companies already know or can reasonably estimate**".
- **Limb (b) — what this does and does not do.** It **converts G-33 from a convenience sample ("0 of 13" issuers quantifying, across two filing routes) into two published population-level statements in two jurisdictions**, one of them a supervisor's own **random-sample** review — the closest thing the register has yet held to the population census the limb asked for. It does **not** close G-33: the **ESMA-supervised population** rate is still unpublished, the **firm-level quantified** pre-adoption effect remains unpublished (**0 of 13** in the filed sample, "few quantified" in Norway, and the DRSC's figures are *expectations*, not realised breaks), and the register now holds **three different objects** under one limb name — *expected-impact distribution* (S-452), *disclosure incidence and timing* (S-453), and *firm-level quantification* (S-434's sample) — which **must not be pooled**. The register makes **no claim about any price response** to any of it.
- **Effect on the premise, stated in both directions.** The new evidence **supports** the candidate's "not pre-priced" side: if more than half of a random sample of listed companies publish no company-specific information, and only 8 % updated it by the 2026 interim season, then the **accounting break is not in the public record** in quantified form for the population, which is the state the candidate's diffusion clock is about. It **qualifies** the arm-A route: the *published* pre-adoption state now available is the **German survey's expectation distribution**, which is **aggregate** (it cannot be attached to a security), **population-specific** (DAX 40), and about *expectations* rather than realised breaks — so it is a **prior** on the arm, not the arm's state. A candidate may not treat it as a firm-level state.
- **New prior-art flag (standing, independent of the score).** **The candidate may not present the dispersion of pre-adoption impact as unstudied.** Two published population statistics now exist (S-452, S-453). What remains unstudied is the **price response** to the adoption and to the disclosure, not the disclosure practice itself.
- **V-009 gains one reporting requirement, and is amended rather than re-created.** Any arm-A state construction must **name which population's statistic it uses** and **what object that statistic measures** — expected-impact distribution (S-452, DAX 40, aggregate, expectations), disclosure incidence and timing (S-453, Norway, random sample of 50, disclosure), or firm-level quantification (S-434's filed sample, 0 of 13) — and must not pool them. No `V-` identifier is consumed; the executable set, pre-registrations, hypothesis, cost model and kill criteria are unchanged.
- **Effect.** **No score change (69.0, rank 1 unchanged) — and the reason is stated, because it is a rule and not a convenience:** the new evidence bears on **disclosure practice**, not on the **price-diffusion response** that the candidate's mechanism is about; the mechanism's own evidence base (the transition-window comparability study, and the G-21 comparator which is a **validation** task on V-009) is **unchanged**; and the register's precedent is that a cell moves on evidence about the **mechanism**, not on evidence about an adjacent observable. **No gap opened or closed** (G-21 unrun; G-32 limb (c) and G-33 remain dated to the 2027 season, with the population-rate limb now populated in two jurisdictions); **no `IDEA-` identifier consumed; no `V-` identifier consumed; two `S-` identifiers consumed (S-452, S-453)** and **one existing source upgraded in place (S-437)**.



