## IDEA-013 — EU-ETSCOMP: the surrender-deadline concession in the EU ETS, and the published banking position that decides whether it exists

- **ID / short name:** IDEA-013 / EU-ETSCOMP
- **Date added:** 2026-10-19 · **Cycle:** 5 · **Status:** **parked (P-008)** · **Version:** 1.1
- **Change notes:** v1.0 — first dossier. New mechanism family for the register: a **statutory,
  date-bound purchase obligation** with a monetary penalty, in a market whose decision-time state is
  published by a regulator. The *effect* (a surrender-month premium) is published (S-101); the
  candidate claims only the two things the published work does not cover — the **regime change**
  (the deadline moved from 30 April to 30 September from 2024, outside the published sample) and the
  **existence-flipping state** (a pre-deadline published aggregate compliance balance). The full
  text of the published work (NBER WP 35356 / arXiv 2606.03767) **must be read before this candidate
  is retained**; see "Remaining uncertainty".
  **v1.1 — the required full-text check was performed, and the candidate is parked (P-008).** Three
  findings, all verified in this cycle. (i) **The check is resolved in the candidate's favour and
  against it at once:** S-101 does **not** condition the premium on the aggregate surplus (there is
  no occurrence of "TNAC" in the paper; the aggregate balance appears only as a state variable
  *inside the model*, and the paper's "cumulative surplus" measure is used to *construct*
  operator-level shortfalls, not to explain premium variation) — so the candidate is not a
  re-description of published work. But the published model already makes the benchmark price affine
  in the aggregate bank, so the state-conditional structure is **implied by the published model**; what
  the candidate claims is an *empirical* conditional structure that the published work does not
  estimate. (ii) **The published sample does not support the existence-flipping hypothesis and mildly
  cuts against it:** the paper states that "This delayed and frictional trading pattern persists into
  the most recent phase of the EU ETS, indicating limited improvement over time", and its most recent
  phase is Phase IV (2021) — a period of very large aggregate surplus (TNAC 1 134 794 738 for 2022
  and 1 111 736 535 for 2023, S-121). The paper does not report the April premium's magnitude by
  year, so the hypothesis is neither supported nor refuted by the published sample; what the sample
  shows is that a large surplus did **not** switch the effect off. (iii) **The decision-time state
  was, until the regime change, published *after* the deadline it was supposed to inform** — the
  Commission publishes TNAC for emissions year Y in late May or June of year Y+1 (S-121), so under
  the pre-2023 regime (surrender deadline for emissions of year Y: 30 April of year Y+1) the aggregate
  state for the compliance year was published **after** that deadline. Decision-time availability
  begins only with the 2023 compliance year (deadline 30 September 2024), which leaves **three
  decision-time observations to date** (2023, 2024 and 2025 compliance years) and **no observation in
  a deficit year**. This is recorded as the new gap **G-13** and as a new screening condition in the
  state-proxy note: *a published state is usable as a decision-time variable only if it is published
  before the decision.*
  **Why parked rather than retained or rejected.** The effect is published (S-101), so this is not a
  discovery; the claimed residual is not refuted, but the candidate cannot be *validated* in any
  decision-time form: the state switches sign too rarely for a conditional claim to be testable, the
  published model implies the conditional structure rather than leaving it open, and the published
  sample contains a high-surplus year in which the effect persisted. Re-specifying around a weaker
  state (operator-level bank-adjusted shortfalls, which are public only *ex post* in the EUTL) would
  convert the idea into a disguised baseline. The honest position is a **park** with a dated
  unblocking question, not a retention and not a rejection.

**One-sentence hypothesis.** The EU ETS obliges every covered installation, aircraft operator and
shipping company to surrender allowances for the previous year's verified emissions by a statutory
date, penalising failure at €100 per tonne — an obligation that is therefore almost perfectly
price-inelastic — and the resulting deadline-bound demand creates a surrender-month premium paid by
delayed compliance officers to their counterparties; the candidate's claim is that this premium is
**not a standing feature** but an **existence-conditional** one, appearing only in years when the
aggregate compliance balance is short, a balance that is knowable *before* the deadline from
regulator-published data — though, as v1.1 records, that property holds only in the current regime,
from the 2023 compliance year onwards — and that the published result covers only the *old* deadline
regime.

**Plain-language description.** Every year, an installation that emitted a tonne of carbon last year
must hand over one allowance to the government by a fixed date, or pay €100 for every tonne it
fails to cover — roughly the price of the allowance itself, so the obligation is effectively hard.
A firm that has been sitting on its allowance budget all year cannot wait for a better price; it
must buy in the deadline month, and it buys from whoever is willing to sell — usually a bank or a
fund that has been holding the allowance. The buyers pay up. But this only happens when the system
as a whole is *short*: if firms are sitting on a large surplus of banked allowances, nobody is
forced to buy anything in the deadline month and the premium does not exist. Whether the system is
short is not a secret — the regulator publishes, every May or June, the total number of allowances
in circulation, which is a direct read on the surplus. **But** (v1.1) that publication arrives in
late May or June of the year *after* the emissions year, so before the 2023 compliance year it
arrived *after* the surrender deadline it was meant to inform, and the "knowable before the
deadline" property of this state is a property of the **current regime only**.

**Mechanism (causal chain).**
1. **The obligation is statutory and hard.** Under Article 12(3) of Directive 2003/87/EC, "by
   30 September each year" the operator of each installation, each aircraft operator and each
   shipping company must surrender allowances equal to its verified emissions for the preceding
   calendar year (S-102). Failure to surrender on time attracts a penalty of **€100 per tonne** for
   the missing allowances, and the **names of penalised operators are made public** (S-101). A
   penalty of that order relative to the allowance price makes the demand curve almost perfectly
   inelastic in the deadline window — the textbook condition for a concession.
2. **The calendar is published and the timing is a policy variable.** Emissions must be verified by
   an accredited verifier by **31 March**; the surrender deadline was **30 April** under the
   original regime and was moved to **30 September** by Directive (EU) 2023/959, applying from the
   2024 compliance year onwards (S-102, S-103). The market monitors have already observed that the
   shift "spread out demand" and that "December saw the highest volumes, as is typical" (S-104) —
   so the *aggregate* relocation of the pressure is documented; what is not documented is the
   *conditional* structure below.
3. **The published effect.** Borri, Liu, Tsyvinski and Wu find that "purchases concentrate in April
   when returns are systematically high, and operator flow predicts future returns"; that "this
   concentration of compliance demand is associated with systematically high April returns, averaging
   about 10%"; and they estimate "the implied April premium paid by delayed buyers from this
   surrender pattern at about €5 billion" — while being careful that the premium "should be
   interpreted as a financial premium paid in April rather than as a direct measure of aggregate
   resource costs, because part of the higher April price is paid to counterparties" (S-101). Their
   model makes the mechanism explicit: "access determines residual compliance demand, intermediary
   constraints translate residual demand into a surrender-month premium, and the premium feeds back
   into access incentives."
4. **The published sample predates the regime change.** The empirical work covers **2005–2021**
   (S-101). The deadline moved to 30 September for the 2024 compliance year. The published effect
   therefore has **not** been re-established in the regime that is now law, and the market
   structure around it has changed: the MSR has been withdrawing allowances into the reserve since
   September 2024, and the 2027 auction calendars were published on 1 October 2026 with the
   2027 volumes already adjusted for the reserve and for the Social Climate Fund allocation
   (S-105, S-106).
5. **The state is published before the deadline — but only in the current regime — and it flips the
   sign of the effect.** The
   Commission publishes, every year, "the total number of allowances in circulation (TNAC) for the
   previous year" under Article 1(4) of the MSR Decision; the market monitors describe TNAC as "an
   indicator for the surplus" (S-107). The Commission published TNAC for 2023 — 1,111,736,535 — on
   **3 June 2024**, which is after the March verification deadline and before the September
   surrender deadline, and thereby determined that 266,816,768 allowances would be withheld from
   the auction volumes from 1 September 2024 to 31 August 2025 (S-107). Verified emissions, free
   allocation and the cross-sectoral correction factor are also published (S-108, S-109). TNAC,
   the auction calendar and the verified-emissions data therefore allow the **aggregate compliance
   balance for the year whose deadline is approaching** to be estimated before that deadline —
   **in the current regime only** (v1.1: the TNAC publication calendar is late May or June of the
   *following* year, so under the pre-2023 April deadline the balance for the compliance year was
   published after that deadline; see the timing finding below and gap G-13).
6. **The sign discipline.** If the aggregate balance is short, the deadline creates a forced,
   price-inelastic *buyer* and the allowance price should be pushed up into the deadline, with the
   concession partly unwound afterwards as the forced demand disappears. If the aggregate balance is
   long, there is no forced buyer, the deadline effect should be **absent**, and the residual
   pressure in the year should appear elsewhere (the December volume pattern the market monitor
   already reports, S-104). The candidate's prediction is therefore *two-sided*, and the negative
   case is as informative as the positive one: **a deadline premium that appears in every year is
   evidence against the candidate**, because it means the effect is not state-conditional.
7. **The counterparty side is not a compliance entity.** The seller into the deadline is a
   non-compliance holder — a bank, a fund or another non-compliance entity — and the market
   monitor's own data show the "number of entities invested in EUA futures and not regulated under
   the EU ETS Directive" rising to around 900 on average in 2024, a jump of about 15% year on year
   (S-104). This is the "who is mispriced" leg the prompt requires: the compliance officer is
   *forced* to transact and pays a premium; the non-compliance holder *chooses* to transact and
   collects it.

**Expected return source.** A compensation for supplying allowances to a date-bound, penalty-backed
mandated buyer — a *liquidity-provision premium*, not a risk premium for systematic exposure, and
not the carbon-price trend itself. The premium exists because the obligated buyer's demand is
insensitive to price near the deadline; the provider is compensated for the price risk of holding
the allowance into the window and for the concentration of the exit.

**Originality.**
- *Closest known work:* Borri, Liu, Tsyvinski and Wu (S-101) — a NBER working paper with a formal
  model and 2.7 million registry transactions, and the direct statement of both the effect and the
  mechanism; the earlier versions of the same project (S-111) are the same work, not separate
  evidence. The market-structure and pricing background is in the European Commission's own
  auctioning documentation (S-105), the Commission's TNAC communications (S-107), the national
  authority's auction-calendar notices (S-106) and the annual independent market report (S-104).
  The venue and rule documentation is S-102, S-103.
- *What is already known:* that purchases concentrate in the surrender month; that returns in that
  month are systematically high (about 10% on average); that the implied premium is of the order of
  €5 billion; that operator flow predicts future returns; that roughly 40% of operators do not
  trade in a given year; and that the premium is a payment to counterparties rather than a resource
  cost.
- *Substantive differentiator claimed here:* (i) **the regime** — the published sample ends in 2021
  and the deadline that is now law is a different deadline, so the published result is not a
  statement about the current regime; (ii) **the state** — the premium is predicted to be
  *existence-conditional* on the aggregate compliance balance, which is knowable before the
  deadline from regulator-published data (TNAC, verified emissions, the auction calendar and the
  MSR decision), so the condition is decision-time observable rather than an ex-post
  classification; (iii) **the falsification structure** — the candidate can be killed by a
  deadline premium that appears in surplus years as well as deficit years, which is the outcome
  that the published framing would lead one to expect.
- *Honest classification:* **published effect, with an unclaimed conditional structure and an
  unexamined regime.** This dossier must never be described as a discovery of the surrender-month
  premium — that is S-101's result. What is claimed is that the premium is conditional and that the
  condition is published in advance. The condition is a *conjecture*; nothing here establishes that
  the premium is state-conditional, and the kill conditions below are written so that it can fail.
- *Confidence in the comparison:* **moderate, and now checked.** The full text of S-101 (arXiv HTML
  rendering, v2) **has been read** in Cycle 5. The verdict: the paper does **not** condition the
  surrender-month premium on the aggregate surplus, so the candidate's central claim is not
  pre-empted; but the paper's *model* already makes the equilibrium price affine in the aggregate
  bank of allowances, so the state-conditional structure is an implication of the published model
  rather than an unclaimed idea about the mechanism; and the paper's own robustness construction
  works from **operator-level, bank-adjusted** shortfalls, which implies the aggregate balance is
  not the object that determines residual demand. The full text also supplies two facts that the
  v1.0 dossier did not have: the statement that the delayed-trading pattern "persists into the most
  recent phase of the EU ETS" (Phase IV, 2021 — a high-surplus year), and the fact that the paper's
  own counterfactual analysis of **staggered surrender deadlines** already reduces the premium by
  about 59%, which is directly relevant to the regime change the candidate relies on. **What remains
  unclaimed** is an *empirical* estimate of how the premium varies with the published aggregate
  balance across the regime change.

**Target scope.** EU allowances (EUA) on the ICE futures and options venue and, at lower frequency,
the EEX auction clearing price; the compliance window from the verification deadline (31 March) to
the surrender deadline (30 September from 2024; 30 April in the earlier regime); annual frequency
with one observation per year, and a pre-2024 sub-sample with one per year at the April deadline.
Regime conditions: exclude the ETS2 sector expansion (buildings and road transport, surrender
deadline 31 May from 2028) from any early sample; exclude years in which the *policy* cycle
dominates (the October-November annual review, the MSR decision and the free-allocation
adjustment) from the estimation window, and treat them as controls rather than as signal.

**Observables (knowable at decision time, conceptually).**
- The statutory calendar: verification deadline 31 March, surrender deadline 30 September from 2024
  (S-102, S-103) — published, statutory, not a forecast.
- The aggregate compliance balance: TNAC for the compliance year (published by the Commission in
  late May or June of the year of the deadline — **available before the deadline only from the 2023
  compliance year**), the verified-emissions dataset, the free-allocation and
  cross-sectoral-correction-factor decisions, and the EEX auction calendar with its volumes
  (S-105, S-106, S-107, S-108, S-109, S-121).
- The MSR's effect on the auction supply in the compliance window (the reserve's withholding
  schedule, which the Commission and the national authority both publish) (S-106, S-107).
- The published market data used as the mechanism check: EEX daily auction results and quarterly
  auction reports including participant counts (S-105).
- *Timing hazards to flag for the validator:* the surrender and compliance data for year Y are
  published by 6 October of Y+1 (S-110) — that is **after** the deadline, so they can only be used
  for an ex-post check of the state, never for the decision; the auction calendar and the MSR
  adjustment are published in advance but their *interpretation* is not; and the sample has one
  observation per year, so the effective sample is very small and every additional control
  (year fixed effects, the policy cycle) costs degrees of freedom.
- **The decisive timing finding of Cycle 5 (G-13):** the aggregate state itself has a publication
  calendar that must be checked against the decision date. The Commission publishes TNAC for
  emissions year Y in **late May or June of year Y+1** — TNAC for 2022 on 15 May 2023, for 2023 on
  3 June 2024 (S-121). Under the pre-2023 regime the surrender deadline for emissions of year Y was
  **30 April of year Y+1**, i.e. the aggregate balance for the compliance year was published
  **after** the deadline it was meant to inform, and the state a rule could have used before that
  deadline was the balance for year Y−2. Under the post-2023 regime (deadline 30 September of year
  Y+1) the balance for year Y is published **before** the deadline. **Consequence:** the
  *decision-time* form of this candidate can only be run from the **2023 compliance year** onwards —
  three observations so far (deadlines of 30 September 2024, 2025 and 2026), all in a period of large
  aggregate surplus, with **no deficit observation**. The *ex-post* form can be run over the whole
  published sample, but an ex-post state is a decomposition, not a rule.

**Rule sketch (descriptive, non-executable).** Once a year, after the Commission has published the
TNAC figure for the compliance year (which, in the current regime, happens in late May or June of
the year of the deadline) and the auction calendar for that year, and before the surrender
deadline: estimate the aggregate compliance balance for the year whose deadline is approaching as
(verified emissions for that year, net of the free allocation and the CSCF adjustment) minus the
allowances the system is holding net of what it has already surrendered — a quantity the published
TNAC series and the surrender data jointly bound. If the balance is short, form a long position in
the front-of-deadline allowance future, held to the deadline; if the balance is long, form no
position. Size the position by the *estimated shortfall* relative to the *published auction volume*
for the compliance window, and cap it at a pre-specified fraction of the contract's average daily
volume. Exit on the settlement of the deadline-week contract. Exclude any year in which the
October-November policy cycle or a link to the Swiss ETS produced a repricing, and record those
years as excluded rather than as failures. **Feasibility note (v1.1):** this rule can only be run
from the **2023 compliance year** onwards, because before that the TNAC for the compliance year was
published after its deadline (G-13) — three observations to date, none in a deficit year, so the
rule as written cannot yet be evaluated on a state switch.

**Persistence thesis.** The obligation is statutory and the penalty is a multiple of the allowance
price, so the deadline demand cannot be avoided; the compliance calendar is published a year ahead
in the directive; and the market's structure around it (a small number of non-compliance sellers
and a fragmented set of obligors) changes slowly. A premium that is paid annually to the party
that provides the allowance in the window is a structural transfer, not an anomaly.

**Constraints.** Cost/liquidity: the EUA futures market is liquid enough for institutional size but
is an order of magnitude smaller than rates or equity futures, and the spot market is thin; the
*effect* is large (about 10% in the published April sample) but so is the *noise* from the policy
cycle, so a small net edge after costs is possible and must be checked rather than assumed.
Capacity: a non-compliance participant is needed on the *supply* side of the trade, and a
participant that is not a compliance entity can be on either side; a pure compliance officer cannot
short. Market impact: in a one-year sample, a single position can move the price it is predicting.
Venue/operational: EUA futures are cash-settled on ICE with a published contract specification
whose expiry list changed when the deadline moved (S-102) — the contract calendar must be
reconstructed for the pre-2024 sample, because the April deadline and the September deadline imply
different expiries. Shorting constraints: shorting an allowance future is available but the
position carries the risk of an unscheduled policy announcement (the "carbon price crash" risk).

**Regime sensitivity, tail exposure, crash behavior, hidden beta.** Regime: the candidate is
*defined* by the compliance regime and by the annual policy cycle. Tail: the EUA market has
repeatedly had policy-driven 30–60% drawdowns, and a position held into the deadline is exposed
to them. Crash behaviour: the position is *long* the allowance into a window in which the policy
calendar is quiet, so it is exposed to a negative policy shock arriving without warning (a
re-based cap, a lower auction volume, a link to a cheap foreign market). Hidden beta: the long
allowance position is a long *carbon-transition* position, so it loads on the regulatory-trend
factor and on the energy complex, and the "premium" may be compensation for exactly that exposure
rather than for liquidity provision — a confound that the state-conditioning does not remove.

**Falsifiable predictions and rejection conditions.**
1. *Existence-conditionality (the primary test):* the deadline premium is present in years when
   the pre-deadline published aggregate balance is short and absent (or of the opposite sign) in
   years when it is long. **Kill:** the premium is of similar size in surplus and deficit years —
   in which case the effect is unconditional, the state adds nothing, and the candidate is a
   re-description of S-101. **Cycle-5 status: untestable in decision-time form, and partly
   pre-answered.** The decision-time test has at most three observations, none in a deficit year
   (G-13); and the published sample contains a high-surplus year (Phase IV, 2021) in which the
   delayed-trading pattern persisted, which is evidence against the sign flip. Both facts are
   recorded here rather than buried, and they are the reason the candidate is parked.
2. *Regime discontinuity:* the effect's month moves with the statutory deadline — April in the
   pre-2024 regime, September thereafter — and is not a fixed calendar month. **Kill:** the premium
   remains concentrated in April after the legal change, which would mean the April pattern is
   driven by something other than the surrender obligation.
3. *Mechanism check with published data:* the EEX auction volumes and participant counts rise in the
   compliance window of deficit years and not in surplus years (S-105). **Kill:** the published
   auction data show no such difference, which would undercut the demand-concentration mechanism.
4. *Reversal:* after the deadline, part of the pre-deadline concession unwinds (the price gives
   back part of the deadline move) in deficit years. **Kill:** there is no post-deadline reversal,
   which would mean the move is a permanent repricing rather than a concession, and there is no
   liquidity-provision premium to capture.
5. *Cost floor:* the estimated pre-deadline premium minus the round-turn cost in the EUA future
   (spread, commission, financing) is positive. **Kill:** it is not, in which case the candidate is
   a description and not a trade.
6. *Pre-registration:* the *rule* — long the allowance future if and only if the published balance
   is short — must be fixed before the outcome data are examined, and it must be evaluated on the
   pre-2024 April regime and the post-2023 September regime as two separate samples, with the
   sign-flip prediction stated in advance for both.

**Main confounders and rival explanations.**
- *The policy cycle* — the October-November review, the MSR decision, the free-allocation
  adjustment and the cap decisions move the allowance price far more than a deadline month
  normally does; the deadline is inside the calendar year but the *effect* may be an artefact of
  the surrounding announcements.
- *The energy complex* — EUA prices co-move with gas and power prices, so a "deadline premium"
  may be a power-sector hedging demand story: the fuel-switching channel is identified in the
  literature as a major driver of allowance prices.
- *Speculative positioning* — the ECB's own economic bulletin documents the role of non-compliance
  participants and of speculative flows in allowance price moves, which cuts both ways: it is an
  alternative explanation for the deadline premium and a supply-side explanation for who collects it.
- *Free allocation and the CSCF* — the aggregate balance depends on free allocation, which is
  adjusted ex post and may dominate the surrender obligation itself.
- *The banking constraint* — operators that bank a surplus in one year are the ones that can afford
  to miss the deadline in another; the "40% of operators do not trade" finding (S-101) is a
  reminder that the *marginal* obligor is not the average one.
- *Small sample* — one observation per year is a very weak basis for a conditional claim, and this
  is the most serious design weakness in the candidate.

**Data requirements and feasibility for a future independent validation.** (Specification only —
nothing is acquired or processed here.) Public: the statutory calendar (S-102, S-103); the TNAC
communications (S-107); the verified-emissions and free-allocation data and the Union Registry
public data and the EEA data viewer (S-108, S-109); the EEX auction calendars, daily auction
results and quarterly auction reports (S-105); the national authority's notices (S-106); the annual
market report (S-104). Commercial or venue-priced: the EUA futures price history (ICE settlement
data) and the EUTL transaction-level files (public but bulk, and the transaction data are
commissioned-research-grade). Feasibility: **medium-high** for the state and the calendar, and
**medium-low** for the price series and the sample size. The binding constraint is not data
availability but the **number of independent observations**: one per year against a state that
must be estimated, not observed — and, after the Cycle-5 timing finding (G-13), **only three
years in which the state was published before the decision at all**.

**Theory/evidence status.**
- *Conceptual rationale:* supported — the mechanism, including the "intermediary constraints
  translate residual demand into a surrender-month premium" step, is formalised in S-101, and the
  penalty-backed near-inelasticity of the obligated demand is a direct reading of the statute and
  the penalty schedule.
- *Mechanism supported by sources:* **yes** — the obligation, the penalty, the calendar, the
  auction calendar and the TNAC publication are all documented (S-102 … S-109).
- *Prior empirical support reported by others:* the surrender-month premium, the April
  concentration, the flow-return predictability and the €5 billion estimate are the authors' own
  (S-101) and are **not** reproduced here.
- *Unresolved:* whether the premium is state-conditional; whether it survives the deadline change;
  whether any part of it is a policy-cycle or fuel-switching artefact; and whether the published
  work already answers the state question.

**Remaining uncertainty / confidence.** Confidence that the obligation, the penalty and the
published state exist: **high**. Confidence that the deadline premium exists in the current
regime: **moderate** — the published result is strong but its sample ends in 2021, and the market
monitors' own reading of the shift is that demand was "spread out", which is the opposite of a
sharp deadline premium. Confidence that the premium is *state-conditional*: **low** — this is the
candidate's conjecture; the full text of S-101 (read in Cycle 5) neither tests it nor supports it,
and its Phase IV persistence statement shows that a very large surplus did not switch the effect
off. Confidence that it is tradeable after costs: **low-to-moderate** — thin market, small sample,
large policy-event risk. **Feasibility of the decision-time rule: low** — the state is published
before the decision only from the 2023 compliance year, leaving three observations, none of them in
a deficit year (G-13). Priority score **58/100** (down from 70: the first check was resolved
favourably, but the model-implied conditional structure, the contradicting persistence statement
and the decision-time sample cut the other way); rank 10 of 10 retained-or-parked candidates at the
time of writing. **Status: parked (P-008).** **Concrete next question (dated, and it is a
data-accumulation question, not a research question):** the unblocking event is the accumulation of
enough post-2023 compliance years for the *ex-post* balance to move across a sign change with at
least one observation on each side. The next published TNAC is for emissions year 2026, expected in
late May or June 2027 (S-121's calendar); the corresponding surrender deadline is 30 September
2027. **Before 2029, the candidate cannot be validated in any decision-time form, and the register
should not spend further effort on it.** A secondary question, worth one search only: whether the
published literature has yet replicated the April premium in the post-2023 September regime — if a
replication exists, the *regime* residual is closed too and the candidate should be rejected rather
than parked.

---

## IDEA-014 — RIN-DEADLINE: the US Renewable Fuel Standard's compliance-deadline obligation, and the published net position that decides its sign


### CYCLE 115 — PRIORITY 1: MECHANISM-MIS-IDENTIFICATION SCREEN (applied to live book)

**Pre-registered before fetch.** For IDEA-014, identify the instrument that actually sets the price the claim is about; classify the candidate's state as upstream/downstream/orthogonal; record hard-stop if orthogonal.

**Price-setting instrument (from published sources):** RIN prices are determined by supply/demand for compliance obligations under the RFS. The compliance obligation is set by EPA regulations (annual percentage standards under 40 CFR 80.1405); RINs are tradable credits. Prices reflect compliance costs/supply-demand; deadlines affect timing/costs but the underlying price-setter is the compliance market.

**State classification:** Candidate's state is compliance deadline timing (and the net obligated position). This is **downstream/timing mechanism** affecting compliance costs, not an upstream price-setter of RINs.

**Hard-stop flag:** **No orthogonality.** Only live claim is state-free existence of deadline discontinuity (V-012 executable, null kills). Detectability constrained by cost floor (need 1.0-1.8 ¢/RIN measurable on weekly VWAPs; round-trip cost floor 0.75-1.5 ¢/RIN). Deadlines passed but testability tight.

**Per-candidate block (IDEA-014):**
- Price setter: RIN market supply/demand for compliance obligations.
- Candidate state relation: **downstream/timing mechanism** (deadline affects costs/pricing).
- Hard-stop: **none on orthogonality**; **state-free existence kill-test only** (V-012) with stated detectability bound.
- Evidence: S-393, S-394, S-404, S-405, S-439, S-440, S-443, S-444.

- **ID / short name:** IDEA-014 / RIN-DEADLINE
- **Date added:** 2026-10-20 · **Cycle:** 6 · **Status:** candidate (**rank 3 of 4**; the *conditional* version
  is retired with a hard stop; a **state-free** specification now exists and its cost floor is the published
  RIN round-trip cost) · **Version:** 1.3
- **Change notes:** v1.0 — initial dossier. New mechanism family for the register: a statutory,
  date-bound purchase obligation on a *credit* in a US regulatory market, screened as frontier
  family 1 ("statutory, date-bound obligations outside the EU ETS"). The candidate is the
  **state-conditioned sign** of the deadline effect; the EU ETS version of the same template is
  published (S-101) and is a baseline, not a discovery.
  **v1.1 (Cycle 35) — G-24 and G-25 closed as bounded negatives for the conditional version:** the
  claimed RGGI replacement state and candidate IDEA-016 were never written in any artifact, so the
  RGGI lead is an unwritten lead rather than a candidate; the conditional version stays retired and
  the state-free existence limb is retained. No score change (52.0).
  **Cycle 52 — no score change (52.0), rank 5 unchanged.** The state-free existence limb was checked for
  any 2025–2026 source bearing on the RFS compliance deadline, the RIN price and its pass-through, or a
  pre-deadline RIN state: **nothing was found**, so the limb **remains inconclusive** — neither a
  positive finding nor a refutation — and **P-011 stands**; no gap was opened on it. One point was
  sharpened for a future reopening: a pre-deadline state must satisfy **both** halves of the requirement
  independently — **published before the deadline** *and* **sign-determining rather than merely
  informative** — and the RIN analogue fails the second half because the published projection is set
  against the agency's own supply projection, so the projected net position is near zero by construction
  (S-184, S-201).
  **v1.2 (Cycle 112) — two agency documents located on two unused route forms; no score change (52.0); the
  state-free existence limb remains inconclusive; V-012 written as the candidate's first *state-free*
  specification; G-38 opened.** **S-393** (GAO-23-105801, November 2022) and **S-394** (EPA-420-R-22-038,
  December 2022) are the register's first entries from the **legislative-audit** and **agency-impact-analysis**
  route forms on this candidate. They establish, on the agencies' own audited EMTS transaction data, that
  **the compliance obligation is priced** (the smaller obligated party paid or received prices "2.4 percent
  higher or lower … on average" and "2.9 percent" between refineries — GAO's own words — with EPA's central
  estimate at "1.1 % (1.2 ¢) more per RIN" over the daily average and D4 at 2.7 %, against EPA's stated
  sensitivity band of 5.3 % less to 7.5 % more) and that **the deadline's timing structure is as the
  candidate assumes** (compliance dates "generally March 31 of the year following the compliance year";
  current- or prior-year vintages both usable; exemption decisions issued after the 90-day deadline 89 % of
  the time, so refineries "often do not know whether they need RINs until close to, or after, the March 31
  compliance deadline"). They **do not** establish a deadline-specific price effect, so the **state-free
  existence limb stays inconclusive** — neither a positive finding nor a refutation — and the conditional
  version stays retired with its hard stop. Under the Cycle-35/52 precedent the sources bear on the
  mechanism and the cost floor, not on the conditional claim, so **no cell moves**. The register's Cycle-6
  specification rule is **amended, narrowly and on the record**: a **state-free** specification is now
  permitted (G-15 is closed on the published-literature side, the state-free test needs no signed state by
  construction, and the two new entries supply the public transaction-price dataset and the published
  friction anchor the Cycle-7 search had recorded as unavailable), while **the bar on any state-conditioned
  specification is re-affirmed in full**. **V-012** is written. **G-38** is opened for the one input still
  missing: a published estimate of the RIN market's bid-ask spread or round-trip cost.
  **v1.3 (Cycle 113) — gap G-38 is CLOSED on two published cost anchors, and the closure is ADVERSE to the
  candidate's required effect; the state-free existence limb stays inconclusive; V-012 amended to carry the
  cost anchor and to tighten the pass bar to it; no score change (52.0), rank 3 of 4 unchanged.** **S-404**
  (NERA Economic Consulting, "Ethanol RIN Market Analysis and Potential Reforms", 18 October 2018, filed in
  EPA docket EPA-HQ-OAR-2018-0775) and **S-405** (Lade, Lin & Smith, "Ex Post Costs and RIN Prices under the
  RFS", Resources for the Future Discussion Paper 15-22, June 2015) are the register's first **published RIN
  cost** entries. On the **Roll (1984)** model of the effective spread, the RIN market's frictions are
  **several times** those of comparable energy futures: RIN spreads reach **up to five times** comparable energy
  markets' spreads, transaction costs are of the order of **five times**, market **liquidity is of the order
  of one-tenth**, and RIN **price volatility is five to ten times** that of the comparable energy markets.
  **Both anchors are index-rendered** (the docket document was read through the search index; the RFF landing
  page and PDF refused a direct fetch with HTTP 404 and HTTP 403) and the first is a **consultancy paper
  prepared for a party with a commercial interest in the reform**, so the register relies on the **order of
  magnitude** and not on the point estimates. **Why the closure is adverse, and why it is a closure rather
  than a null:** the register's own dispersion anchors are **small in percentage terms** (GAO's 2.4 %–2.9 %,
  EPA's 1.1 % central premium against EPA's own −5.3 % to +7.5 % band), and a market whose round-trip cost is
  several times that of comparable energy futures places a **few-percent price difference inside the cost
  band, not above it** — so G-38 closes with a **tighter** cost floor than the dispersion anchors alone
  implied, a material change to the candidate's kill test even though it changes no score. **The state-free
  existence limb stays INCONCLUSIVE**: G-38 asked for the *cost*, not for the *effect*, and no published source
  estimates a deadline-specific pre-/post-31-March RIN price pattern — per the register's Cycle-52 rule, an
  absence may not be upgraded to a refutation and a cost finding may not be read as an effect finding. **The
  candidate is not parked.** **V-012 is amended, not re-created:** the cost floor is restated against the
  published RIN cost anchor and the pass bar is **tightened** to it, so a result that clears the dispersion
  anchors but not the market-cost floor is a **price-discontinuity** result and not a tradeable-return result;
  **the bar on any state-conditioned specification is re-affirmed in full** and no `V-` identifier is consumed.
  The candidate's **dated re-screen conditions are narrowed**: the cost limb is satisfied and closed, and the
  two remaining limbs are a published estimate of a pre-/post-31-March RIN price pattern and the EPA's own
  publication of a transaction-price series long enough to span a moved deadline (the two moved 2026
  deadlines, **S-131**, remain the natural test and are unchanged).

**One-sentence hypothesis.** Obligated parties under the US Renewable Fuel Standard must retire
Renewable Identification Numbers (RINs) against their annual renewable volume obligations by a
statutory, published compliance deadline; because the obligation is not price-sensitive, because
its size is fixed in advance by published regulation, and because the industry is either a net
buyer or a net seller of RINs depending on published generation against published obligations, the
*sign* of the deadline effect is determined by a state that is published **before** the decision —
so the concession is paid by the obligated party into the deadline and reverts after it.

**Plain-language description.** The US Renewable Fuel Standard forces refiners and importers to
blend a specified volume of renewable fuel each year, and they meet that obligation by retiring
"RINs" — tradable credits issued by the EPA for each gallon of qualifying renewable fuel. EPA
sets each year's volumes in a rule published months ahead, and the annual compliance report (and
the RIN retirement it evidences) is due on a deadline that the regulation itself fixes and
publishes. In the last weeks before that deadline, any obligated party that has not yet covered
its obligation must either buy RINs (if it generated too few) or sell them (if it generated too
many) — and it must do so on a schedule that is not price-sensitive. If the published generation
data say the industry is short, the deadline creates forced *buying*, the RIN price is pushed up
into the deadline, and the forced buyer pays a premium that is expected to unwind afterwards. If
the published data say the industry is long, the sign reverses. Which of those two worlds you are
in is **published before you have to take a position** — which is the property this candidate is
built on.

**Mechanism.** A price-insensitive, date-bound flow (here: a regulated party's obligation to
retire credits by a statutory deadline) meets a credit whose price is set by a small, specialist
market. The template is published in another venue — the EU ETS surrender-month premium
(S-101: surrender-month returns "averaging more than 10% in Aprils") — so the mechanism is borrowed
and is not claimed as new. The RIN price itself is a studied object in the peer-reviewed
literature (S-134 on RIN prices and the ex-post cost of the programme; S-135 on RIN-price
pass-through into refined fuels). What makes this instance different is not the mechanism but the
**calendar and the state**: the obligation's size is codified in advance (S-128), the deadline is
published by regulation (S-129), and the direction of the obligated flow is computable from
published standards and published generation data.

**Expected return source.** Compensation for supplying (or, in the net-long state, demanding)
liquidity to a forced, dated, price-insensitive participant at the compliance deadline; the
concession is expected to revert after the deadline as the obligation is satisfied and the
forced flow ends.

**Originality.** The EU ETS instance of this template is published (S-101) and is in the register's
baseline set. Within the US RIN market, **no located work states the compliance-deadline effect
with its sign determined by the published net obligated position**. What is published is: the price
and its pass-through (S-134, S-135); the market's quality, liquidity and transaction costs, and
the documented tendency to price near-expiry vintages *above* longer-lived ones (S-133); and EPA's
own published acknowledgement that market participants need time "to adjust their compliance
strategies … and for the RIN market to react" to exemption decisions before the deadline (S-131).
The last is an institutional statement that the market reacts to compliance news — with no sign
and no magnitude. Differentiator: a **two-sided, state-conditioned deadline claim** in a market
where the sign is an empirical question, plus a tradeable futures venue (S-136) and a published
price series (S-132). Honest classification: informed synthesis of a published mechanism template
in an unclaimed instance, with the sign untested.

**Target scope.** US RFS D3–D6 RIN vintages; compliance years 2010–2025 (with 2026 and 2027
partially observable); annual decision windows centred on the published compliance deadline;
cross-section across RIN types and across the net-position state; holding 1–10 sessions into and
out of the deadline.

**Observables (decision time).** The annual percentage standards, codified and including two
future years (40 CFR 80.1405 — S-128); the annual rule setting the applicable volumes and its
effective date (S-130); the **published** annual compliance reporting deadline (40 CFR 80.1451(f)
— S-129) and any published extension of it (S-131); EPA's published RIN generation data by fuel
type and D code (weekly, lagged); EPA's published weekly volume-weighted-average RIN prices by
transfer date (S-132); small-refinery-exemption decisions and reallocations (published; S-131
shows they move deadlines); ICE D6 RIN vintage futures and the CME D4/D6 (OPIS) futures (S-136).

**Falsifiable predictions and rejection conditions.**
1. *The sign follows the published net position.* In compliance years where published generation
   falls short of the published obligation net of carryover and exemptions, the obligated party is
   a net buyer: the relevant vintage's price rises into the deadline and falls back after it. In
   years where it is long, the sign reverses. **Kill:** the pre-deadline/post-deadline difference
   has the same sign in both kinds of year — i.e. the published state does not determine the sign.
   This is the candidate's core test and it is two-sided.
2. *It is a deadline effect, not a level effect.* The pre/post-deadline difference is larger for
   vintages that expire at the deadline than for vintages that do not, in the same week.
   **Kill:** no difference by expiry vintage.
3. *Magnitude follows the obligation, not the market.* The size of the move scales with the
   published obligation shortfall relative to published generation, not with RIN market turnover.
   **Kill:** no relation to the shortfall.
4. *The moved deadline is a test, not an exception.* The 2025 compliance deadline moved from
   1 September 2026 to 1 October 2026 by published rule (S-131); the 2026 deadline is 31 March 2027
   (S-129, S-131). A date that moves at short notice is a *stronger* test than a fixed one, and a
   **no-response result around either date is informative in both directions** — it would weaken
   the deadline mechanism rather than merely fail to support it.
5. *Cost floor.* The gross pre/post-deadline difference must exceed the round-trip cost in the venue
   actually used. **Kill:** the difference is smaller than the cost. Given the published
   market-quality evidence (S-133), this is the most likely kill.

**Confounders.** The "blend wall" and vintage-specific scarcity (a D6 can be scarce for reasons
unrelated to the deadline); EPA rule changes and waivers, including the partial waiver of the 2025
cellulosic requirement in the same rule that set the 2026–27 standards (S-130); small-refinery
exemptions and reallocations, which move the deadline and the obligation (S-131); the carryover
mechanism, which changes the net position; the market's documented opacity and squeeze history
(2013), which can dominate any deadline effect; seasonal ethanol and diesel price moves; and the
filters and truncation in the published price series itself (S-132), which can manufacture
artefactual level shifts in the very series the test would use.

**Data requirements.** (Specification only — nothing is acquired or processed here.) EPA's
published weekly RIN price series by D code and transfer date (S-132); EPA's published RIN
generation data by fuel type (weekly, lagged); the codified standards (S-128); the annual rules and
their effective dates (S-130); the published compliance deadlines and any extensions (S-129,
S-131); ICE and CME futures prices and open interest for the vintages (S-136). Feasibility:
**medium** — every *input* is published, but the published price series is a lagged weekly
volume-weighted average from EPA's own moderation system with published filters and truncation,
the generation data's lag leaves the last weeks of the compliance year unpublished before the
deadline, and the venue's liquidity is not documented in the public record.

**Theory/evidence status.**
- *Conceptual rationale:* supported by analogy to a published mechanism in another venue (S-101)
  and to the general literature on price-insensitive, dated flows; the RIN credit has a studied,
  economically measurable price (S-134, S-135).
- *Mechanism supported by sources:* yes for the participant, the constraint, the dated obligation
  and — decisively — the **publication calendar** (S-128, S-129, S-130, S-131). The
  state-conditioned sign claim is an unresolved conjecture.
- *Prior empirical support reported by others:* for the components only. **No located work states
  the deadline effect with the sign determined by the published net obligated position.**
- *Unresolved:* the sign and size of the deadline effect (gap **G-15**) and the cost floor (S-133).

**Constraints.** Cost/liquidity: the published market-quality evidence runs against the candidate —
roughly one-tenth the liquidity of comparable commodity futures and transaction costs peaking at
five times theirs (S-133) — and the futures venue's own liquidity is undocumented publicly
(S-136), so the cost model must be justified independently rather than assumed. Capacity: bounded
by the size of the obligated party's net requirement and by venue depth, and the other participants
who supply the same liquidity see the same published state. Financing/margin: in the net-short
industry state the trade is *short* a credit with a documented squeeze history (2013), and margin
is set on a thin market. Venue/operational: the futures are physically delivered into EPA's
Moderated Transaction System (S-136), so exit depends on EMTS processing and on the delivery
calendar. Shorting constraints: the position's sign is short precisely in the states most likely
to squeeze. Model risk: the state requires a *net* obligated position, reconstructed from published
generation, published obligations, carryover and exemptions; an error in that reconstruction flips
the sign of the trade.

**Regime sensitivity, tail exposure, crash behavior, hidden beta.** Regime: the effect exists only
near compliance deadlines, and the net-position state that carries it changes slowly. Tail: the
2013 RIN squeeze is the tail case, and deadline concentration multiplies it. Crash behaviour: a
regulatory shock — a waiver, an exemption wave, a reallocation — can remove the obligation after
the fact, and EPA has moved deadlines at short notice (S-131). Hidden beta: a long RIN position is
a long biofuel-policy position; a policy change that reduces the obligation removes the state and
the signal together. Correlated risks: all RIN types share one obligation and one deadline, so the
book is a single policy bet.

**Remaining uncertainty / confidence.** Confidence that the obligation, its size, the deadline and
the state inputs are all **published before the decision**: **high** (S-128, S-129, S-130, S-131)
— this is the candidate's defining property and the exact property IDEA-013 lacked. Confidence that
a harvestable concession survives the cost floor: **low-to-moderate**, because the published
market-quality evidence is directly against it (S-133). Confidence that the sign is state-determined:
**unverified — this is the candidate's first empirical test** (G-15). Priority score **60/100**;
rank 7 of 7. **Cycle 13: score unchanged at 60.0; rank 7 → 6.** **Concrete next question:** does the published weekly RIN price series show a
systematic pre-deadline/post-deadline difference whose sign follows the published net obligated
position, and is that difference larger than the venue's round-trip cost?

**Cycle 6 screen (G-13 publication timing; G-12 sign or existence).**
- **G-13 — the state is published before the decision: PASS, and this is the candidate's defining
  property.** Three independent regulatory facts carry it: the obligation's **size** is codified in
  the CFR for two future years (40 CFR 80.1405: 2026 total renewable fuel standard 15.50%, 2027
  15.78%; amended 91 FR 16487, 1 April 2026 — S-128); the **deadline** is not merely knowable but
  *published by regulation* — "The annual compliance reporting deadline will be calculated in
  accordance with paragraph (f)(1)(i) of this section and published on EPA's website" (40 CFR
  80.1451(f)(1)(iii) — S-129); and where the deadline has moved, it moved **by published rule**
  (the 2025 deadline moved from 1 September 2026 to 1 October 2026 — S-131), with the next deadline
  stated in the same instrument (31 March 2027). This is the candidate IDEA-013 could not be.
- **G-12 — the conditioning changes the sign or the existence of the effect: PASS on the sign
  limb.** The published EU ETS instance of this template (S-101) has no such state: the candidate's
  claim is that the deadline effect is **two-sided** — a net-buyer state produces a rise into the
  deadline and a fall after it, a net-seller state the reverse — so the published state determines
  the sign of the trade, and the claim is falsifiable in both directions. That is a sign claim, not
  a size claim, and it is what makes the candidate more than a re-description of S-101.
- **Effect on the dossier: new candidate at 60.0, status `candidate` (retained, not shortlisted).**
  The two conditions it must clear before it can be validated are already named: **G-15** (does the
  published price series show the state-determined pre/post-deadline difference, and is it larger
  than the cost) and the cost floor from S-133. No validation specification is written in Cycle 6.

**Cycle 13 screen (G-13 publication timing; G-12 sign or existence) — no score change.**
- **G-13 — PASS, unchanged.** The obligation's size (codified), the deadline (published by
  regulation) and the deadline's own history of moving by published rule are untouched by Cycle 13.
- **G-12 — PASS on the sign limb, unchanged.** The two-sided, state-determined sign claim is
  untouched; no published work found in Cycle 13 claims the state-determined sign.
- **Cycle-13 frontier screen result relevant to this candidate.** The register screened the sibling
  cap-and-trade family — **California Cap-and-Invest** — for a second instance of the deadline
  template (S-163, S-164, S-165, S-168, S-173, S-174). The verdict was *no candidate*, for a
  reason worth recording next to IDEA-014: California's own price-containment reserve (a
  state-triggered, date-bound supply valve whose trigger is the published preceding auction's
  settlement price against published tier thresholds, and whose schedule sits in the regulation)
  has **never been used** in the modern era (S-163), and the annual surrender obligation is met with
  near-100% compliance, so the price-insensitive demand at the deadline is *satisfied in advance*.
  The template is therefore not automatically portable: what makes IDEA-014's version interesting is
  the *state* (the published net obligated position) that decides whether the deadline demand is
  tight — and no analogous published state exists in the California program.
- **Effect on the dossier: no score change (60.0); rank 7 → 6** (rank change is an artefact of
  IDEA-001's rejection and the re-scores of IDEA-004 and IDEA-009). **G-15 remains open** and no
  validation specification is written.

**Cycle 30 G-15 advance — the deadline's movement is rule-determined, and the sign-determining state
exists before the deadline only in *projected* form; no score change (60.0); G-15 sharpened into G-20.**
- **What was searched.** G-15 asks whether the published RIN price series shows a state-determined
  pre/post-deadline difference. This cycle did not look for a price pattern — that is a validation
  question and is out of scope here — but for the **state's publication status before the deadline**,
  because that determines whether a two-sided sign test can be run at all.
- **Finding 1 — the deadline's movement is rule-determined (S-184).** The EPA's 16 October 2026 proposed
  rule states the **automatic extension mechanism** at 40 CFR 80.1451(f)(1)(i)(C): if the EPA neither
  finalises nor withdraws the proposed revision to the 2026 RFS standards within 12 months of its
  publication, the next quarterly reporting deadline moves. The candidate's prediction 4 ("a moved
  deadline is a test, not an exception") therefore rests on a **rule** rather than on discretion, which
  strengthens the design, and it creates a **second dated test event** — the 2026 compliance year,
  currently 31 March 2027 — whose trigger is published rather than discretionary.
- **Finding 2 — the sign-determining state is only *projected* before the deadline (S-184, S-185).** For
  the 2025 compliance year the EPA *projected* an exempted Renewable Volume Obligation of **990 million
  RINs**; the proposed rule states that the relief leaves obligated parties with obligations **770 million
  RINs fewer** than the EPA projected in the Set 2 Rule; and the rule states explicitly that **"reported
  2025 exempted fuel volumes will not be available until after the 2025 compliance reporting deadline of
  October 1, 2026"**. The *realised* net obligated position — the state that decides the sign — is
  therefore published **after** the deadline it informs, while the *projected* position is published
  before it. EPA's public data estate does publish the obligation side as a **named data category**
  ("Renewable Volume Obligations"), monthly generation and volume-production files on roughly a
  one-month lag, and a participant-level **RIN Holdings** report by balance year and balance quarter
  (S-185) — so a *projected* state is constructible in principle, but it is an inference from published
  projections and holdings, not a published net-obligated-position series.
- **Effect on the dossier: no score change (60.0); G-15 stays open but is now a precise question; one
  new gap.**
  - **G-15 (sharpened, still open):** does the published RIN price series show a state-determined
    pre/post-deadline difference, *conditional on the projected* net obligated position? The projection
    is the only pre-deadline state, so the two-sided sign test must be run on it, and the realised
    position is a post-hoc control, never an input.
  - **New gap G-20:** is the *projected* net obligated position a sufficient sign-determining state, or
    does projection error swamp it? The projection has already been revised once — from the Set 2 Rule's
    projection to the 770 million RIN relief — and if the revision band exceeds the state that decides
    the sign, the candidate's sign test is not runnable and the candidate must be re-specified or parked.
  - Scores unchanged: mechanism 4/5; evidence 2/5 (no published price-pattern work located; the new
    sources are legal and institutional, not empirical); novelty 3/5; falsifiability 3/5 (the
    rule-determined deadline strengthens the design, but the **cost floor** (S-133) remains the binding
    kill condition and the sign test now carries a projection-error caveat); resilience 2/5 (unchanged —
    squeeze risk on the short side); data 4/5 (unchanged — the obligation side is published in principle,
    but the realised state is post-deadline, which is a constraint the dossier already carried).
  - **No validation specification is written.** IDEA-014 has never had a validation specification, and
    none may be written for it until G-24 is closed. *(Cross-reference corrected in Cycle 32: the Cycle-30
    text of this line read "V-008 stays withdrawn", which was wrong — V-008 belongs to IDEA-009 PERP-FUND,
    which is active and executable. IDEA-014 has no V-number at all.)* The candidate remains at the
    bottom of the ranking, unchanged in score and rank.

### Cycle 32 (2026-10-20) — G-20 closed against the state as specified; the candidate is re-scored to 52.0
and takes a hard-stop flag on its sign-determining state

- **The gap is closed, and the answer is that the projection error does swamp the state.** G-20 asked
  whether the *projected* net obligated position is a sufficient sign-determining state. It is not, and
  the register can now say so quantitatively rather than as a worry.
  - **The projected net position is near zero by construction.** The Set 2 final rule (S-201) sets the
    volume requirement against the agency's own supply projection — its statutory factor analysis works
    from the "Analyzed Volumes" and states that "the total quantity of renewable fuel we project will be
    supplied to the U.S. to meet these volume requirements". So the *projected* net obligated position —
    the quantity the candidate wants to read the sign from — is a small residual of two large projections
    that the agency has already made consistent.
  - **The one signed component of that projection has been measured, and the error is +78%.** The EPA
    projected a 2025 exempted RVO of **990 million RINs** in the Set 2 Rule (S-201); the actual 2025
    exempted RVO, from the 31 August 2026 small-refinery-exemption decisions recorded in S-184, is
    **1,760 million RINs**. **Projection error: +770 million RINs, +78%** (underlying fuel volume: 7.55
    billion gallons projected against 13.38 billion actual, **+5.83 billion gallons, +77%**). The error is
    *structurally* one-sided: the projection is "a three-year average of the actual exempted fuel volumes
    from 2022 through 2024" (S-201) — a lagged average of past decisions, not a forecast of this year's
    exemption wave, which the EPA decided in a single batch on 31 August 2026.
  - **The scale makes the swamp concrete.** The 2026 and 2027 total applicable volumes are **26.81** and
    **27.02 billion RINs**, with SRE reallocation volumes of **0.99** and **1.04 billion RINs** (S-203). A
    770-million-RIN projection error is **78% of the entire annual SRE reallocation volume** that the
    agency itself treated as material enough to revise the standards; and it is roughly **2.9% of the
    total applicable volume**, i.e. of the same order as the net position a projection error of that
    size would have to resolve.
  - **A projected shortfall is institutionally absorbed, not expressed as a binding market obligation.**
    The one category where a projected shortfall actually occurred is instructive: for 2025 cellulosic the
    EPA projected 1.21 billion gallons against a 1.38 billion RIN requirement — "a shortfall of
    approximately 13 percent" — and the response was "implementation of the **cellulosic waiver
    authority** for 2025" (S-201), i.e. the obligation was waived, not bought. The RFS's architecture
    (waiver authority, SRE reallocation, carryover RINs) buffers mismatches between obligation and
    supply; a shortage state is not allowed to express itself as a forced market purchase.
  - **The realised state is published only after the deadline** (S-184: "reported 2025 exempted fuel
    volumes will not be available until after the 2025 compliance reporting deadline of October 1,
    2026"), so the *realised* net position is unusable as a decision-time state, and the *projected* one
    does not determine the sign.
- **Effect on the dossier: score 60.0 → 52.0; G-20 CLOSED against the state; a hard-stop flag; G-24
  opened; the candidate's testable core is restated.**
  - **Mechanism 4 → 3.** The chain "negative net obligated position → forced deadline-bound, price-
    insensitive buying → a concession the deadline rents" has a broken first link: the projected position
    is calibrated to zero, and where a projected shortfall does occur the obligation is waived rather
    than bought. The deadline-cluster *existence* claim (a statutory deadline concentrates a compliance
    flow) is untouched; the *state-determined sign* link is not.
  - **Falsifiability 3 → 2.** Prediction 2 of the dossier ("the published net obligated position
    determines the sign") is now known to be untestable as stated, not merely untested: the projected
    position does not determine the sign, and its only signed component carried a +78% error in the one
    year in which it has been measured. One of three predictions is dead as written.
  - **Evidence 2 → 2 (unchanged), novelty 3 → 3 (unchanged), resilience 2 → 2 (unchanged), data 4 → 4
    (unchanged).** The new sources are regulatory documents, not price-pattern evidence, so the
    evidence dimension does not move — the register records this rather than inflating it with
    institutional material. The *inputs* are public; what fails is the informational content of the
    state, which is priced in the mechanism and falsifiability dimensions.
  - **Hard-stop flag attached:** *the sign-determining state as specified requires look-ahead information
    (the realised position) or is uninformative by construction (the projected position); the candidate
    must not be specified, ranked as a state-conditioned candidate, or presented as a top opportunity
    until a replacement state is found.*
  - **The candidate is retained, not parked**, because its **existence** limb remains testable without any
    state: G-15's first half — does the published weekly RIN price series show a systematic pre-deadline /
    post-deadline difference at all, and is it larger than the venue's round-trip cost (S-133)? — is a
    state-free test of the deadline-cluster's existence and size. What is suspended is the *conditional*
    version. *(A reader who prefers to park the candidate outright can do so without loss: the register's
    own rule would then apply — a parked candidate keeps its score but sits outside the ranking and
    writes no specification. The register does not park it because parking would discard a testable
    existence claim that the register has no other candidate covering.)*
  - **New gap G-24.** Is there a published, **pre-deadline**, **signed** state for the RIN deadline
    cluster that is **not** the outcome itself — for example the carryover-RIN balance, or the
    pre-deadline RIN price and positioning (S-185) — such that the test can be run without look-ahead and
    without circularity? If none exists, the candidate's state-conditioned version is unrunnable and the
    candidate is a state-free existence test only.

### Cycle 35 (2026-10-20) — G-24 and G-25 closed as bounded negatives for the conditional version; the claimed RGGI replacement state turns out never to have existed

- **The claim that has to be corrected.** The Cycle-33 source-ledger header stated that G-24 was closed
  **in IDEA-014's favour** because "a replacement state exists in the RGGI CO₂ allowance market,
  producing new candidate **IDEA-016 RGGI-COMPLIANCE**", and the Cycle-34 handoff repeated it. **No
  such candidate, source or verdict exists in any artifact** — checked file by file and against the full
  git history on 2026-10-20. IDEA-016 was never written; the "Cycle 33 sources" and "Cycle 33 gap
  verdicts" blocks were never in the ledger; the identifier is **reserved and unused**.
- **What survives, and on what basis.** The *Cycle-34 conclusion* — that no **pre-deadline**, **signed**,
  **non-circular** replacement state was found for the RIN cluster, so the conditional version of the
  candidate is retired to a state-free existence test — is independently supportable on its own terms and
  is what Cycle 35 records. **G-24 and G-25 are closed as bounded negatives for the conditional
  version.** The RGGI lead is logged as an **unwritten lead** (no dossier, no source, no verdict) and is
  **not** a candidate: a later cycle may pursue it only by writing it from primary sources under the next
  free IDEA identifier, which is **IDEA-017** (IDEA-016 stays reserved).
- **What is unchanged.** IDEA-014 retains its **state-free existence limb** — the G-15 existence test
  (is there a post-deadline difference at all, and is it larger than the venue's round-trip cost,
  S-133?) — which remains runnable without look-ahead. The score stays at **52.0**, the hard stop on the
  conditional version stays, and the candidate stays **retained, not parked**, because the existence
  claim is a testable residual the register has no other candidate covering. **Rank 5, unchanged.**
- **Score: 52.0, unchanged.** Mechanism 3 → 3, evidence 2 → 2, novelty 3 → 3, falsifiability 2 → 2,
  resilience 2 → 2, data 4 → 4. No new source bears on the candidate this cycle.

#### Cycles 36–51 consolidated note — IDEA-014 · no source bearing on this dossier; the state-free existence limb stays **inconclusive**
- Across Cycles 36–51 the register ran the family screens **R-018 … R-031** (the Rule 201 short-sale price
  test, the ex-dividend family, the closing-price family and the rest, each a bounded negative), the
  **G-29** execution and **G-30** exhaustion on IDEA-010, the **G-21** null maintenance and the Cycle-52
  groundwork on IDEA-011, and the register-wide evidence-currency passes. **None of these produced a
  source bearing on IDEA-014.** Two things remain true and must keep being stated precisely:
  1. The candidate's **state-free existence limb** (the G-15 test — is there *any* post-deadline difference,
     and is it larger than the venue's round-trip cost, S-133?) is **inconclusive**. It is neither a
     positive finding nor a refutation, and it must never be described as either.
  2. The **conditional version is retired**: G-24 and G-25 are closed as bounded negatives (no
     pre-deadline, signed, non-circular replacement state exists), and the RGGI lead remains an **unwritten
     lead** — no dossier, no source, no verdict — with the next free identifier **IDEA-017** (IDEA-016
     stays reserved).
- **Score: 52.0, unchanged. Rank 5, unchanged.** The candidate stays **retained, not parked**, because the
  existence claim is a testable residual the register has no other candidate covering.

#### Cycle 52 block — IDEA-014 · the existence limb stays inconclusive, and Cycle 52 sharpened *what a future pre-deadline state would have to be*
- **What Cycle 52 did.** A bounded check for any 2025–2026 source bearing on the US Renewable Fuel
  Standard's compliance-deadline obligation, on the RIN price and its pass-through, or on the pre-deadline
  RIN state. **Nothing was found.** The Cycle-52 corpus (S-252 … S-266) is entirely IFRS-adoption
  literature and venue-halt / circuit-breaker literature; neither touches the RFS. **The existence limb
  therefore remains inconclusive, and P-011 (IDEA-015) stands.** No new gap is opened on this limb, and
  the conditional version stays retired.
- **What Cycle 52 *did* add — and it is a specification-sharpening point, not a candidate.** The R-032
  family screen produced the register's clearest published example of the **opposite** situation: a
  decision-time state that is **published before the decision, in real time, and continuously** — the LULD
  price bands are "republish[ed] … every 30 seconds" to the public (S-260) — and that example is useful
  here precisely because it *fails* for a reason that mirrors the RFS candidate's problem. For LULD, the
  state is public but carries **no sign**: the band is a symmetric constraint, and the tradeable
  consequence is a *cost* (wider spreads, higher realised volatility, volume concentration in the least
  liquid names) rather than a *direction* (S-259, S-266). For the RFS deadline, the candidate's problem is
  the mirror image: the state that would carry a **sign** (the net obligated position) is published only
  **after** the deadline (S-184), and the published pre-deadline proxy (the agency's own projection)
  carries **no sign**, because it is set against the agency's own projection of supply, so the projected
  net position is near zero by construction (S-201). **Both halves of the register's standing requirement
  are therefore jointly necessary and independently missing**: a pre-deadline state must be (i) published
  before the deadline *and* (ii) sign-determining rather than merely informative. A future G-24 reopening
  must look for a state that satisfies **both**, and any candidate that proposes only a published
  pre-deadline state will fail the second half — which is the RIN analogue of the "no sign" objection that
  closed R-031 and, in the case of a market-wide trigger, the "beta" objection that closed R-032.
- **Score: 52.0, unchanged.** Mechanism 3 → 3, evidence 2 → 2, novelty 3 → 3, falsifiability 2 → 2,
  resilience 2 → 2, data 4 → 4. **Rank 5, unchanged.** No validation specification is written for
  IDEA-014, and none may be written until a pre-deadline, signed state is identified.

#### Cycle 70 block — IDEA-014 · evidence-currency sweep: EPA RFS proposed rule reallocates 2025 SREs; deadline-extension mechanism confirmed; no score change
- **What Cycle 70 did.** Ran an evidence-currency sweep on IDEA-014 (RIN-DEADLINE) against 2025–2026 literature and EPA
  rulemaking. Located one new source: **S-324** — EPA proposed rule "Renewable Fuel Standard (RFS) Program: Reallocation of
  Additional 2025 Small Refinery Exemptions" (91 FR 65699–65711, published 2026-10-16, RIN 2060-AX09, Docket EPA-HQ-OAR-
  2024-0505). The proposed rule reallocates additional 2025 small-refinery exemptions (actual exempted volume 1.76B RINs
  vs Register-projected 990M, +78% above projection) either equally across 2026–2027 (~1.4B each) or entirely to 2027
  (~1.81B), and triggers the automatic compliance-deadline extension under 40 CFR 80.1451(f)(1)(i)(C) — the 2026 deadline
  (currently 31 March 2027) may move. Verified via direct FederalRegister.gov fetch.
- **Assessment.** No published work conditions the candidate's effect on a second variable, tests the candidate's sign
  claim, or changes the baseline. S-324 reinforces the G-20/G-24 conclusions (projected net position carries no sign;
  net obligated position published only after the deadline). G-15 remains inconclusive. G-24 stays closed as bounded
  negative; G-20 stays closed against projected state. No gap opened.
- **Score: 52.0, unchanged.** Rank 5, unchanged. No validation specification is written or amended for IDEA-014.

### Cycle 100 (2026-10-21) — provenance only: the RFS regulation's own text is now line-verified, and one
source of the register's arithmetic is corrected

- **The regulation itself is now read, not inferred (S-202).** 40 CFR 80.1405 was fetched directly
  through the **eCFR renderer endpoint** — which returns the section's full text where the versioner
  endpoint (HTTP 406) and the human-facing page (navigation chrome only) do not — and read: **HTTP 200,
  18,171 bytes, 4,763 characters of section text**. Confirmed verbatim: "Table 1 to Paragraph
  (a)—Annual Renewable Fuel Standards" with the columns "Cellulosic biofuel standard (%) | Biomass-based
  diesel standard (%) | Advanced biofuel standard (%) | Renewable fuel standard (%) | Supplemental total
  renewable fuel standard (%)"; the equation block "Except as specified in paragraph (c) of this
  section, EPA will calculate the annual renewable fuel percentage standards using the following
  equations"; the defined terms **"GEi = Amount of gasoline projected to be exempt for year i, in
  gallons, per §§ 80.1441 and 80.1442"** and **"DEi = Amount of diesel fuel projected to be exempt for
  year i, in gallons, per §§ 80.1441 and 80.1442"**; and the reallocation terms "SRERV BBD,i = Small
  refinery exemption reallocation volume for biomass-based diesel for year i, in gallon-RINs".
- **One correction to the register's sourcing.** The dossier's arithmetic in the Cycle-32 record is in
  **billion RINs** (26.81 and 27.02), and those numbers come from the **agency's summary (S-203)**, not
  from the regulation: the string "billion RINs" returns **zero** hits in the fetched section, which
  states the standards as **percentages** (2026: 0.79 / 5.24 / 6.42 / 15.50; 2027: 0.84 / 5.37 / 6.61 /
  15.78) and the volume terms in **gallon-RINs**. The **numbers are unchanged**; their **attribution** is
  now precise, and the S-202 entry carries the correction in place.
- **A dated fact that supports the dossier's publication-timing claim.** The current section text
  carries standards **2010 → 2027** and the string "2028" returns **zero** hits — so the 2028 standards
  are **not yet** in § 80.1405 at the fetch date. The instrument that publishes the next compliance
  year's requirement is therefore the one to watch, and the **state is published before the compliance
  deadline**, exactly as the dossier's route claims — which is a *fact about the route*, not a rescue of
  the candidate: G-20 stays **closed against the projected state** and G-24 stays a **bounded
  negative**. **No gap is opened, closed or re-scoped; no score changes; IDEA-014's 52.0 and its rank are
  unchanged.**

### Cycle 109 — P-6 audit: is the sign-determining net obligated position published before the compliance deadline? (bounded negative on the state as specified; the architecture is public, the sign-determining quantity is not)

- **The question, pre-registered.** R-056's three-limb form: (A) are the obligation, the deadline and the
  generation data **published instruments**? (B) is the **sign-determining** quantity — the industry's net
  obligated position — **fixed by information published before the compliance deadline**? (C) is the state
  **free** of a private or forecast input? The pre-registration forbade re-opening G-20 and forbade
  re-running the **RFS-deadline price-effect existence limb** (inconclusive, and no new source bearing on
  it was sought).
- **Limb A — POSITIVE.** The obligation, the deadline and the generation data are published instruments:
  the annual RFS standard and its compliance date (40 CFR 80.1405 and 80.1451(f), S-128, S-129), the
  agency's own volume tables with the set-aside and waiver mechanics (S-130, S-131), the RIN price and
  generation series (S-132, S-136), and the final rule itself (S-201). The register's route claim that
  "the **state is published before the compliance deadline**" is therefore **true of the architecture** — and
  the distinction between the architecture and the sign-determining quantity is the audit's whole point.
- **Limb B — NEGATIVE for the sign-determining quantity, and the reason is structural rather than
  accidental.** The *projected* net obligated position is near zero **by construction**: the rule's
  statutory factor analysis uses the "**Analyzed Volumes**" (the agency's own projected supply) and states
  that the projected supply "are very similar to the quantities we projected would be supplied to meet the
  proposed volume requirements" — so the RVO is calibrated to make the *projection* meet it (S-201). The
  one signed component therefore carried a **+78 % error** (990 million RINs projected for 2025 against
  **1,760 million** actual), and where a shortfall did arise the agency **waived** the volume rather than
  letting a binding obligation form: 1.21 billion cellulosic RINs projected against the 1.38 billion needed
  is "a shortfall of approximately 13 percent", which "triggers the need for implementation of the cellulosic
  waiver authority for 2025". The **realised** net position publishes only **after** the deadline, and the
  weekly generation data are **lagged**.
- **Limb C — NEGATIVE.** The only inputs that can determine the sign are **projections**: the agency's own
  supply forecast, the "**three-year average of the actual exempted fuel volumes from 2022 through
  2024**" (7.55 billion gallons for 2025) and the small-refinery-exemption reallocations (0.99 billion RINs
  for 2026, 1.04 billion for 2027). A forecast with a published error is a **construction**, not a published
  measurement.
- **Effect on the candidate and on the artifacts.** **No score change (52.0), no rank change, no hard-stop
  flag change, no gap opened, closed or re-scoped, no re-screen triggered, and no validation specification
  changed.** G-20's closure is **confirmed in the three-limb language this cycle used**: the architecture is
  public and the *sign-determining* quantity is not published before the deadline. The audit also states
  plainly what it **cannot** do: the **price-effect existence limb** (whether the RFS deadline moves prices
  at all) needs a **price** observation and not a state, so an R-056-form audit **cannot** settle it and
  was not run against it.
- **What would change the verdict.** A **published, pre-deadline** measure of the industry's net obligated
  position sufficient to determine the sign — for example a final obligated-position estimate published by
  EPA before the compliance date, or a published carryover-and-waiver ledger that binds before the
  deadline. An agency projection calibrated to its own supply forecast does **not** qualify (S-201 makes
  that failure structural, not a timing accident).

---

#### Cycle 112 block — IDEA-014 · two agency documents bear on the mechanism, the cost floor and the deadline's timing; **no score change (52.0)**; the state-free existence limb stays **inconclusive**; **V-012 written** (the candidate's first *state-free* specification); gap **G-38** opened

- **What the screen was.** A pre-registered search for published evidence that the **RFS compliance
  deadline** moves **RIN prices**, using two route forms the register had not used in nineteen cycles of
  work on this candidate: (i) the **agency's own rulemaking and impact analyses** (the EPA memorandum
  EPA-420-R-22-038 and its rulemaking companions), and (ii) the **legislative audit literature** (GAO
  reports on the RFS small-refinery-exemption programme), together with (iii) the academic RIN-price
  literature. The falsification rule was fixed in advance: the limb's subject is the *deadline's price
  effect*, not the obligation's existence and not the sign of any state; an absence of evidence may not be
  upgraded to a refutation, and a positive finding may not be inferred from a mechanism finding.
- **What was found.** Two new entries, both agency publications and both **index-rendered** (search
  extraction of the published PDFs; neither PDF was fetched and text-extracted directly):
  - **S-393 — GAO-23-105801 (November 2022), "Renewable Fuel Standard: Actions Needed to Improve
    Decision-Making in the Small Refinery Exemption Program".** On the **price** side: GAO analysed EPA's
    EMTS transaction data and found that "companies that tended to trade lower quantities of RINs (likely
    smaller refineries) were either **paying more to buy RINs or receiving less when they sell RINs**,
    relative to larger companies from 2013 through 2021"; for the transactions with the largest size
    differences the small party paid or received prices "**2.4 percent higher or lower** for RINs, on
    average", and between refineries "**2.9 percent higher or lower**"; the estimate is 2.9 % for the
    ethanol (D6) RIN and **0.8 %** for the biomass-based diesel (D4) RIN; "**This effect is statistically
    significant**". On the **timing** side: EPA "has issued decisions after the 90-day deadline **89 percent
    of the time** (190 out of 214 decisions)"; because decisions land late, "small refineries that apply for
    exemptions often **do not know whether they need RINs until close to, or after, the March 31 compliance
    deadline**, which creates financial uncertainty"; and this "may result in either an **excess or a
    shortfall of RINs**".
  - **S-394 — EPA-420-R-22-038 (December 2022), "An Analysis of the Price of Renewable Identification
    Numbers (RINs) and Small Refineries"** (the agency's final analysis in response to GAO's Recommendation
    1, over **2.2 million RIN transactions for approximately 140 billion RINs**, 2013–2021, EMTS data). On
    the **price** side: the 24 small refineries that traded on a facility-specific basis "paid on average
    **1.1 % (1.2 ¢) more per RIN** when buying separated RINs when compared to the average daily price and
    **0.5 % (0.6 ¢) more per RIN than the largest 20 refiners**" (D6: 0.6 % above the daily average; D4:
    **2.7 %** above the daily average and **2.6 %** above the largest 20 refiners), and by vintage the
    premium is 1.3 % for the same vintage, 0.5 % for the prior-year vintage and 0.9 % *less* for the
    two-years-prior vintage; EPA's own sensitivity statement is that under the alternative modelling
    assumptions imposed by the GAO Report "these 24 refineries paid anywhere from **5.3 % less to 7.5 % more**
    for RINs when compared to the daily average price". On the **timing** side: "**Compliance dates are
    generally March 31 of the year following the compliance year**, unless otherwise extended"; obligated
    parties "can meet compliance obligations using RINs generated in the compliance year and prior year";
    and "it is common for companies to transact RINs in the year after they are generated, or even two years
    after they were generated".
- **What the findings do and do not establish.** They establish, on the agencies' own audited transaction
  data, that **(i) the RFS compliance obligation is priced** — the constrained obligated party pays a
  measured premium over the daily average price, and the premium is larger for the biomass-based diesel
  credit — and that **(ii) the deadline's timing structure is as the candidate assumes** (a fixed 31 March
  compliance date; current- or prior-year vintages both usable; exemption decisions landing at or after the
  deadline; purchase demand therefore deferred into the compliance year). They **do not** establish that RIN
  prices move systematically around the deadline: no source located in this cycle estimates a pre-/post-
  deadline price pattern, and the price differentials that are documented are **counterparty-size** effects,
  not **deadline-timing** effects. The candidate's conditional claim is therefore **unchanged and
  unclaimed**, and its **state-free existence limb remains inconclusive** — not refuted, not closed.
- **Score: no change (52.0).** The Cycle-35/52 precedent governs: the new sources bear on the **mechanism**
  and on the **cost floor**, not on the conditional directional claim, so no cell moves. Evidence stays at
  2 because the sources are agency publications about the *obligation's* pricing and timing rather than
  about the *deadline's* price effect, which is the thing the candidate's claim is about; novelty stays at
  3 because the conditional claim remains unclaimed in the literature; falsifiability stays at 2 because the
  candidate's sign remains unasserted and unpredicted.
- **A published cost anchor now exists for the existence test — and it is a hard one.** G-15's first half
  asks whether any pre-/post-deadline difference is "larger than the venue's round-trip cost". The register's
  only cost evidence had been S-133's market-quality note. The two new entries give the first
  **agency-audited friction estimates**: the observable dispersion of transaction prices around the daily
  average is **1.1 %** (EPA's central estimate for the 24 small refineries), **2.4–2.9 %** (GAO's
  size-differential estimate), with EPA's own sensitivity band of **−5.3 % to +7.5 %**. Those figures are
  dispersion between counterparties of different size, **not** a bid-ask spread, and the register does not
  treat them as a cost estimate; they are, however, the only published, audited measure of price friction
  around the RIN daily average, and they set the order of magnitude any deadline effect must exceed to be
  tradeable. The register opens **G-38** for the one thing that is still missing: a published estimate of the
  RIN market's **bid-ask spread or round-trip cost**.
- **V-012 is written — the candidate's first *state-free* specification.** The register's Cycle-6 rule reads
  "IDEA-014 … has no specification and must not be given one until gap G-15 … is closed". **G-15 is closed
  on the published-literature side** (Cycle 7) and the register's own gap table lists it as closed; the
  dossier's operative formulation ("none may be written until a pre-deadline, signed state is identified")
  was written for the **conditional** version, which is retired with a hard stop and which is the only
  version that would need a signed state. **The amendment is narrow and is recorded as an amendment:** a
  **state-free** specification is now permitted for IDEA-014 because (i) the candidate's only live claim is
  the state-free existence test, (ii) that test requires **no signed state by construction** — it asks
  whether any deadline discontinuity exists in the price data at all, before any conditional claim is
  entertained — and (iii) the two new agency entries supply the two inputs the Cycle-7 search said were
  unavailable: a **public, agency-audited transaction-price dataset** (EPA's EMTS, 2013–2021) and a
  **published friction anchor** (the price dispersion above). **The bar on any state-conditioned
  specification for IDEA-014 is re-affirmed in full**: the conditional version stays retired with its hard
  stop, G-20 stays closed against the state as specified, and no specification may be written for the
  conditional claim. V-012's specification, its controls, its cost floor and its kill conditions are in
  `validation_handoffs.md`.
- **New gap G-38 (opened)** — the published RIN round-trip cost; see the ledger's gap-verdict table.
- **What would change this verdict (dated re-screen conditions).** A published estimate of a pre-/post-31-March
  RIN price pattern, from any source; a published RIN bid-ask or round-trip cost estimate; or the EPA's
  own publication of a transaction-price series long enough to span a moved deadline (the two moved 2026
  deadlines, S-131, remain the natural test). Absent one of these, the limb stays inconclusive, the candidate
  stays at 52.0, and V-012 stands as the candidate's kill test.

#### Cycle 113 block — IDEA-014 · gap **G-38 is CLOSED** on two published cost anchors, and the closure is **adverse to the candidate's required effect**; **no score change (52.0)**; the state-free existence limb stays **INCONCLUSIVE**; **V-012 is amended** to carry the cost anchor

- **What was executed.** **G-38** asked for "the published round-trip cost of a RIN trade". Its pre-registered
  routes were the CFTC/economic-analysis route form (for a cost anchor on the underlying obligation) and the
  agency-and-audit route form (for a cost anchor on the RIN market itself). Two published cost anchors were
  located and registered: **S-404** (NERA Economic Consulting, "Ethanol RIN Market Analysis and Potential
  Reforms", 18 October 2018, filed in EPA docket **EPA-HQ-OAR-2018-0775** as an attachment to an industry
  submitter's comment) and **S-405** (Lade, Lin & Smith, "Ex Post Costs and RIN Prices under the RFS",
  Resources for the Future Discussion Paper 15-22, June 2015). **Both are index-rendered** — the docket
  document was read through the search index and the RFF landing page and PDF refused a direct fetch
  (HTTP 404 and HTTP 403 respectively) — and the first is a **consultancy paper prepared for a party with a
  commercial interest in the reform**, so the register relies on the **order of magnitude** and not on the
  point estimates.
- **What they say.** On the **Roll (1984)** model of the effective bid-ask spread, the RIN market's frictions
  are **several times** those of comparable energy futures: RIN spreads reach **up to five times** comparable
  energy markets' spreads, transaction costs are of the order of **five times**, market **liquidity is of the
  order of one-tenth**, and RIN **price volatility is five to ten times** that of the comparable energy
  markets. The academic anchor (S-405) points the same way, from an author with no commercial interest in
  the reform.
- **Why the closure is adverse, and why it is a closure rather than a null.** The register's own dispersion
  anchors are **small in percentage terms**: GAO's size-differential estimate of **2.4 %–2.9 %** (S-393) and
  EPA's small-party premium of **1.1 % (1.2 ¢) per RIN**, with EPA's own stated sensitivity band of
  **−5.3 % to +7.5 %** (S-394). A market whose round-trip cost is **several times** that of comparable
  energy futures therefore places a **few-percent price difference inside the cost band, not above it** — a
  pre-/post-deadline price difference of the order the dispersion anchors describe is **below** the cost
  floor rather than above it. So G-38 closes, and what it closes with is a **tighter** cost floor than the
  dispersion anchors alone implied, which is a *material* change to the candidate's kill test even though it
  changes no score.
- **The state-free existence limb stays INCONCLUSIVE, and that is unchanged by the closure.** G-38 asked for
  the *cost*, not for the *effect*. No published source estimates a deadline-specific pre-/post-31-March RIN
  price pattern; the two cost anchors bear on what a detectable pattern would have to clear, not on whether
  one exists. Per the register's Cycle-52 rule, "an absence of evidence may not be upgraded to a refutation,
  and a positive finding may not be inferred from a mechanism finding", and the same holds here from a cost
  finding. **The limb is not closed and the candidate is not parked.**
- **V-012 is amended, not re-created.** The specification's cost floor is restated against the **published
  RIN cost anchor** rather than against the dispersion anchors alone: the floor is now the round-trip cost of
  the RIN market itself, whose frictions are **several times** those of comparable energy futures (S-404,
  S-405), and the pre-/post-deadline difference that the specification would have to detect is therefore
  **of the order of a few percent at minimum** to clear the floor — which is at or above the dispersion
  anchors the register already holds (S-393's 2.4 %–2.9 %, S-394's 1.1 % and its own −5.3 %/+7.5 % band).
  The **bar on any state-conditioned specification for IDEA-014 remains re-affirmed in full**: the
  conditional version stays retired with its hard stop, G-20 stays closed against the state as specified, and
  no specification may be written for the conditional claim. No `V-` identifier is consumed.
- **Effect.** **No score change (52.0, rank 3 unchanged); G-38 closed; no new gap opened; no `IDEA-`
  identifier consumed; no `V-` identifier consumed; two `S-` identifiers consumed (S-404, S-405).** The
  candidate's **dated re-screen conditions are narrowed**: the "published RIN bid-ask or round-trip cost
  estimate" limb is now **satisfied and closed**, and the two remaining limbs are a published estimate of a
  pre-/post-31-March RIN price pattern, and the EPA's own publication of a transaction-price series long
  enough to span a moved deadline (the two moved 2026 deadlines, **S-131**, remain the natural test and are
  unchanged).
#### Cycle 114 block — IDEA-014 · the three remaining limbs are answered: limb (i) is a **bounded negative** (no published pre-/post-31-March RIN price estimate exists), limb (ii) is **constructible on coverage and vintage but NOT on the public price series' form** — a new constraint on V-012 — and limb (iii) is a **stated detectability bound** in which the **cost floor, not statistical power, is the binding constraint**; **no score change (52.0, rank 3)**; the state-free existence limb stays **INCONCLUSIVE**; **V-012 is amended** to re-specify its data requirement

- **What was executed.** The Cycle-113 handoff left this candidate with **two remaining re-screen limbs** — "a published estimate of a pre-/post-31-March RIN price pattern" and "EPA's own publication of a transaction-price series long enough to span a moved deadline" — plus the third limb this cycle added, the **detectability bound** implied by the cost anchor G-38 had just closed on. Nine new sources were consumed (**S-439 … S-447**), and two already-registered sources were **upgraded in place** with corrections (**S-404**, **S-394**).
- **Limb (i) — a published pre-/post-31-March RIN price pattern: a BOUNDED NEGATIVE.** The routes are exhausted for this shape. The nearest published items are: **S-442** (EIA, 13 June 2013, line-verified) — "**The sharpest increase in corn ethanol RIN prices occurred near the February 28, 2013 reporting deadline** for obligated parties to satisfy their compliance requirements for the 2012 RFS program year", with the chart note "**February 28, 2013 = Deadline for obligated refiners and importers to submit 2012 compliance demonstration reports**" and the levels "**$0.01 per gallon to $0.05 per gallon**" before 2013 rising to "**highs of about $1.00 per gallon in early March**"; **S-404** (NERA, line-verified this cycle) on **volume** rather than price; **S-445** (AFPM, 22 November 2024, line-verified) — the **D3 −15 % to $2.70** response to the *proposed* 2024 cellulosic waiver and deadline extension; and **S-447** (Reuters/Platts, 24 August 2026, **index-rendered**; the Reuters URL returned **HTTP 401**) — RINs "**fallen 5% on Friday**" on the 2025 deadline-delay announcement. **None of these is a pre-/post-31-March price estimate**, and the reasons are structural rather than accidental: the 2013 statement is for a **28 February** deadline, is **qualitative**, attributes the move jointly to the blend wall, the bank finalisation and the deadline, and publishes **no post-deadline reversion figure**; the two announcement studies are responses to the *announcement*, not to the *window*; and the volume study is about volume. **The register's own state-free existence limb therefore stays INCONCLUSIVE, and the register records this as an absence of a published estimate, not as a refutation of the effect** — the Cycle-52 rule applies unchanged.
- **Limb (ii) — coverage and vintage: CONSTRUCTIBLE; the public price series' FORM: NOT, and this is a new constraint.** *Coverage:* "**EPA began collecting data on July 1, 2010. Data going back to this date are available through this website**" (**S-439**, line-verified), which comfortably spans both moved deadlines. *Moved deadlines:* the 2024 compliance year's deadline was extended to "**the first quarterly reporting deadline after the effective date of this action revising the 2024 cellulosic biofuel standard**" (90 FR 29751, effective **5 September 2025**), i.e. **1 December 2025**; the 2025 compliance year's deadline was extended "**from September 1, 2026, to October 1, 2026**" (91 FR 56780, effective 4 September 2026) (**S-444**, all three rules line-verified from *govinfo*). *Vintage:* the EMTS transaction instructions state that "**During all other RIN transactions, the RIN Year will be reported**", with Appendix G defining RIN Year as "**The year in which the RINs were generated reflecting the year of the production date**" (**S-441**, **index-rendered** — both `nepis.epa.gov` routes return a redirect stub and a 31-byte file, and the register records the failure), corroborated by the agency's own price page, whose dataset is "**specific to RINs starting from year 2010**" and whose sales report is "**grouped by RIN year and RIN type (D-code)**" (**S-440**, line-verified). **The new obstacle, and it is material:** EPA's **public** price product is "**Weekly volume weighted average RIN prices (updated monthly)**" and "**Weekly aggregated RIN transaction volumes**" (**S-439**, S-440) — **transaction-level EMTS prices are not published.** The transaction-level series the agency itself analysed (2,245,685 usable standard trades) is confined to **2013–2021** (**S-394**, now line-verified), and the trade-type decomposition the candidate's forced-flow identification needs — spot, term contract and consignment — "**[is not] available … Before 2020**" (**S-394**). **So the register's position is exactly this: both moved deadlines are post-2020, so the decomposition *is* available for them; but the *public price data* are weekly VWAPs, so V-012's requirement to use a transaction-level series is NOT satisfiable from what EPA publishes today, and the *agency's own* transaction-level evidence stops four years before the first moved deadline's pre-window.**
- **Limb (iii) — the detectability bound, stated from published numbers only, and it lands on the cost floor.** **(1) Measurement floor:** EMTS prices are recorded to **two decimals** (**S-441**, index-rendered), i.e. **1.0¢ per RIN**; at the published D6 level of **75.375¢/RIN** (S-443, 25 March 2025) that is **1.33 %** of price, so any pre/post difference below 1¢ is unresolvable in the published series at any sample size. **(2) Statistical MDE on EPA's published weekly series** — a 60-day window is **n ≈ 9** weekly observations per (D-code × RIN-year) cell, so `MDE = 2.80·σ_w·√(2/9) = 1.32·σ_w`; substituting the *published* D6 dispersion proxies (the same-day traded range of **0.75¢** and the published D6 vintage spread of **1.375¢**, both from S-443) gives **≈ 1.0 – 1.8 ¢/RIN, i.e. 1.3 – 2.4 % of D6**. **(3) Statistical MDE on the transaction-level series** — n ≈ **41,100** per 60-day window from the published 2,245,685 trades over 2013–2021 (S-394), so `MDE = 0.0196·σ_tx`; substituting the published price-differential scale (1.2¢–4.7¢, S-394) gives **≈ 0.02 – 0.09 ¢/RIN (0.03 – 0.13 % of D6)**. **(4) Cost floor:** the minimum published same-day traded range is **0.75¢/RIN** in D6 (75.00–75.75), **1.50¢** in D4 (80.00–81.50) and D5 (79.75–81.25) and **1.00¢** in D3 (243.00–244.00) (**S-443**), and the Roll-model work implies the *stress* round trip is up to about **5×** comparable-market cost, with liquidity "generally only about one tenth as liquid" and volatility "five to ten times" as great (**S-404**, line-verified). **The conclusion: statistical power is not the binding constraint — the cost floor is.** A transaction-level test could detect a pre/post difference 10–50× smaller than the minimum round trip; EPA's *published weekly* series can just barely detect a cost-clearing effect (1.3–2.4 % of D6) and nothing smaller. **A deadline effect of the order the published dispersion anchors describe is therefore detectable in principle and unharvestable in practice, and the register says so precisely rather than in the order-of-magnitude language of the Cycle-113 block.**
- **Two published magnitudes are larger than the cost floor, have the OPPOSITE sign to the candidate's expectation, and therefore dominate a naive moved-deadline window.** The **announcement effect**: D3 **−15 % to $2.70** on the 2024 waiver-and-extension *proposal* (S-445) and **−5 %** on the 2025 delay announcement (S-447, index-rendered) — of the order of **3.8¢** and **36¢** against a ~75¢ D6, i.e. **four to eleven times the cost floor**, with the "pre" window carrying the negative announcement shock and the "post" window carrying the forced-buying window. And the **expiry confound, of the same order as the whole cost floor**: NERA's line-verified finding that RINs closer to expiry traded **above** longer-lived RINs "**over dozens of weeks**" (S-404), and the published 25 March 2025 vintage spread of **1.375¢** in D6 (S-443) — the same order as the 0.75¢ floor — so **any pre/post comparison that does not hold vintage fixed is measuring the expiry, not the deadline.**
- **Two further adverse complications, both newly line-verified.** (i) **The candidate's mechanism requires forced buying into the deadline, and the published evidence is that liquidity falls into the deadline and price discovery deteriorates precisely there**: "**transaction volume as a percentage of outstanding RINs declines as compliance expiry approaches for that vintage. This trend is observed for all RIN vintages between 2015 and 2018**", and "**illiquidity may impede efficient price discovery in the RIN market when it is most needed**" (**S-404**). (ii) **The forced flow is real but concentrated, which changes what an effect would be**: EPA's own statement, quoted in the 2019 docket submission, that "**on one day, one party held 18 percent of the 9.9 billion D6 separated RINs available that day … at a time when other obligated parties were retiring hundreds of millions of RINs in single EMTS transactions for the upcoming compliance deadline. This activity dropped the total available RINs in the market suddenly and drastically**" (**S-446**, line-verified) — which supports the *existence* of deadline-adjacent forced buying and simultaneously shows that the price effect would be a **liquidity-event** effect attributable to a handful of transactions, not a general one.
- **A register-level precision correction, made in place at S-404.** The Cycle-113 block and the prior S-404 entry paraphrased NERA's cost anchor as "transaction costs are of the order of five times" and "liquidity is of the order of one-tenth". The document's own sentence is "**have estimated economic transaction costs that *peak* five times higher** than oil, ethanol, and natural gas futures", with liquidity "**generally only about one tenth as liquid**" and volatility "**generally five to ten times more volatile**". **There is no published cents-per-RIN round-trip magnitude anywhere in the document** — the cost estimates are figure-only — which is why this cycle's cost floor is anchored on the *published traded ranges* (S-443) and not on a modelled number. The correction is recorded at S-404 and in the cycle notes.
- **V-012 is amended, not re-created.** (i) **The data requirement is re-specified**: the specification may not require a transaction-level price series from EPA's public data, because **none is published**; if the validator runs on the **weekly VWAP** series, the specification must (a) report the **quantification** of the identification under the VWAP's own averaging, (b) report the **vintage-confound** magnitude (the published D6 vintage spread of 1.375¢ is of the same order as the cost floor) as a pre-registered control, and (c) treat the **announcement-effect contamination** (−5 % and −15 %, of 4–11× the cost floor) as a **pre-registered exclusion or adjustment** rather than as part of the treatment. (ii) **The detectability bound is carried into the kill criteria**: on the public weekly series a pre/post difference must be **at least 1.0 – 1.8 ¢/RIN (1.3 – 2.4 % of D6)** to be measurable at all, and **at least 0.75 – 1.5 ¢/RIN** to clear a minimum round trip, with the stress multiple from S-404 on top; a specified effect smaller than the **upper** of those bounds is a **specified null** and the specification must say so in advance. (iii) **The two moved deadlines — 1 December 2025 and 1 October 2026 (S-444) — are confirmed inside EMTS coverage and confirmed post-2020, and they remain the natural test.** No `V-` identifier is consumed, and **the standing bar on any state-conditioned specification for IDEA-014 remains in full**: the conditional version stays retired with its hard stop, G-20 stays closed against the state as specified, and no specification may be written for the conditional claim.
- **Effect.** **No score change (52.0, rank 3 unchanged); no gap opened or closed; no `IDEA-` identifier consumed; no `V-` identifier consumed; nine `S-` identifiers consumed (S-439 … S-447) and two already-registered sources upgraded with corrections (S-404, S-394).** The candidate's **dated re-screen conditions are narrowed to two, and both are now precisely stated**: (a) "a published estimate of a pre-/post-31-March RIN price pattern" — **bounded negative, routes exhausted**; the re-screen condition survives only if a **quantified** estimate appears, and the register's own absence finding is recorded as an absence; and (b) "EPA's own publication of a transaction-price series long enough to span a moved deadline" — **narrowed to the *form* of the series**, since coverage and vintage are now line-verified as sufficient: the re-screen condition survives only if EPA begins **publishing transaction-level RIN prices** (or the specification is accepted onto the weekly VWAP series with the identification cost that entails, which is a specification decision and not a re-screen trigger).



## IDEA-015 — PLAN-SUPPLY: pre-announced, non-discretionary insider supply as a dated, published, price-insensitive shock

- **ID / short name:** IDEA-015 / PLAN-SUPPLY
- **Date added:** 2026-10-20 · **Cycle:** 30 · **Status:** **parked (P-011)** · **Version:** 1.1
- **Change notes:** v1.0 — new dossier, entered as a *screened family* result of Cycle 30. The
  register's first candidate whose decision-time state is published by **corporate disclosure law**
  rather than by a market operator, a regulator or an exchange. Entered parked because the nearest
  published evidence is about a *different event* (plan adoption around earnings, S-183) and the
  effect's size is most likely below the cost floor for most names.

**One-sentence hypothesis.** Officers and directors who sell under a Rule 10b5-1 plan sell on a
schedule they chose months earlier and cannot revise without resetting a regulatory cooling-off
period; because the plan's adoption, duration and maximum aggregate size are disclosed quarterly and
the remaining capacity is reported on Form 4, a security's *future scheduled insider supply* is a
published, decision-time state — and the price pressure that supply creates is a dated concession.

**Plain-language description.** An executive who wants to sell shares signs a plan months in
advance: the plan fixes roughly how much, over what window, and usually at what prices. Since
December 2022 the SEC has required the company to publish the plan's adoption date, its duration and
its maximum aggregate size every quarter, and the insider's Form 4 reports each sale with a
"10b5-1 plan" box and the plan's adoption date — sometimes with the shares the plan still has left.
The seller cannot change course mid-window without restarting a 90-day cooling-off period. So the
market can *see* the supply coming. The candidate's question is whether seeing it is worth anything.

**Mechanism (causal chain).**
1. *Rule and safe harbour.* Rule 10b5-1(c) offers an affirmative defence to a trade made under a
   plan adopted while the insider is not in possession of material non-public information, which
   specifies the amount, the price or price range and the timing, and over which the insider
   exercises no subsequent influence; the 2022 amendments extend the defence, impose a mandatory
   cooling-off period (the later of 90 days after adoption and two business days after the filing of
   the 10-Q/10-K covering the quarter of adoption), bar overlapping plans, and require plans to be
   adopted and operated in good faith (S-182).
2. *The flow is scheduled and non-discretionary.* A plan's amount and timing are fixed in advance and
   the insider cannot accelerate into a price move without invalidating the safe harbour and
   restarting the cooling-off period (S-182). This is the register's recurring "dated,
   price-insensitive, knowable-in-advance" structure, with the difference that the participant's
   constraint is a *legal safe harbour* rather than a regulatory quota or an index rule.
3. *The state is published.* Item 408(a) of Regulation S-K requires quarterly disclosure in the
   10-Q/10-K of trading arrangements, their adoption and termination dates, their duration and the
   **aggregate number of securities to be sold or purchased**; Form 4 reports each plan sale within
   two business days with the plan checkbox and the plan's adoption date, and in at least some
   filings the plan's **remaining** capacity (S-182, S-187).
4. *Expected price pressure.* Scheduled supply arrives against finite liquidity; liquidity providers
   who know the schedule can pre-position, so the concession is paid before or during the execution
   window rather than at a fixed instant.
5. *Reversal.* When the plan's capacity is exhausted or its window closes, the scheduled supply stops
   and the price pressure unwinds — unless a new plan rolls in.
6. *Hence the claim.* Scheduled insider supply is a dated, published, price-insensitive supply shock
   whose price pressure is predictable from the published schedule.

**Expected return source.** A liquidity concession paid by informed liquidity providers to
pre-position ahead of scheduled, non-discretionary supply that the market can see but cannot
accelerate away; the return is the pre-window price pressure (or its absence) net of borrow and
execution costs.

**Originality.**
- *Closest known work:* the insider-trading literature and the buyback/insider-purchase effect
  rejected as a known baseline in R-005; the SEC's own adopting release and economic analysis
  (S-182) and its 2022 proposed release (S-183), whose literature review records that a study of
  Form 144 data on plan trades (January 2016 – May 2020) documents plan-based selling behaviour, and
  that work by Hugon and Lee on plans and strategic trading around earnings announcements finds
  abnormal returns of roughly **−2% to −3%** for plans that execute a sale in the window between a
  plan's adoption and that quarter's earnings announcement, **no price drop** after the earnings
  announcement, and no such effect where the cooling-off period exceeded 60 days.
- *What is already known:* (i) insider transactions predict returns — a known baseline (R-005);
  (ii) plan-based sales dominate the modern insider-selling population (S-187, a vendor statistic,
  unaudited); (iii) **plan timing has published price effects attached to the *adoption* window and
  to information events, not to the mechanical execution of a disclosed schedule** (S-183); (iv) the
  rule, the disclosure regime and the cooling-off period are published law (S-182).
- *Substantive differentiator claimed here:* the **state** — a security's *remaining scheduled
  insider supply*, disclosed quarterly under Item 408(a) and updated on Form 4 — used as a
  decision-time gate on a supply-pressure trade, with the trade's direction determined by whether the
  remaining supply is being executed into strength or weakness. This is not R-005's "insider buys →
  returns" channel (different participant constraint: no discretion, a fixed maximum size, a
  published schedule) and it is not S-183's "plan adoption is strategically timed" channel (different
  event: execution, not adoption).
- *Honest classification:* **an incremental variant of a documented family with a new, published state
  variable.** The family is a known baseline; what is claimed as new is the scheduled-supply state
  and the trade built on it.
- *Confidence in the comparison:* **low-to-moderate.** The plan literature is recent, the located
  price-effect evidence concerns a different event, and no paper was located that constructs a
  "remaining scheduled supply" state.

**Target scope.** US-listed equities with a Section 16 officer or director; small- and mid-cap names
in which scheduled insider sales are a non-trivial share of median daily dollar volume; horizons from
days (pre-window positioning) to weeks (the window itself); exclude sell-to-cover sales tied to
vesting and tax withholding, which are mechanically linked to compensation events rather than being
scheduled discretionary supply.

**Observables.** Item 408(a) quarterly tables (adoption date, termination date, duration, aggregate
maximum shares, plan or not, from 10-Q/10-K); Form 4 filings (plan checkbox, plan adoption date,
transaction date, size, price, and where reported, remaining plan capacity); daily prices and
volumes; short interest and borrow cost for the short leg; a pre-specified liquidity benchmark
(trailing median dollar volume) to scale the supply.

**Rule sketch (descriptive, non-executable).** Each quarter, parse Item 408(a) tables to build, per
security, the set of live plans with adoption date, duration and maximum aggregate size; subtract the
plan sales already reported on Form 4 to obtain remaining scheduled supply; scale by trailing median
dollar volume to obtain a supply-to-liquidity ratio; where remaining scheduled supply exceeds a
pre-registered threshold relative to liquidity and the price has not already moved, take the short
leg in expectation of pressure from the scheduled supply, with a pre-registered exit at the plan's
window end or when remaining capacity is exhausted.

**Persistence argument.** The 10b5-1 plan is the default legal route for insider sales, because the
affirmative defence is available only for pre-arranged trades (S-182); the 2022 amendments make the
plan *more* central, not less. Plan-based sales are a structural feature of US equity supply,
refreshed each quarter as compensation vests and each filing cycle produces new plans, so the
pressure is repeated rather than one-off.

**Constraints and capacity.** Scheduled insider sales are a small fraction of market volume for most
names, so the effect is plausibly above the cost floor only in smaller names — which are also the
most expensive to borrow and the least liquid. The trade consumes exactly the liquidity it prices, and
informed liquidity providers compete for the same concession.

**Regime sensitivity.** The effect should be strongest when the security is small relative to the
plan size, when the plan's window is short and front-loaded, when the tape is rising (plan sales are
usually market or limit orders that execute more readily into strength), and when the window is not
coincident with an information event. It should be weakest around earnings announcements, where plan
sales cluster with information events (S-183) and the sign is confounded.

**Falsifiable predictions and kill conditions.**
1. *Cross-sectional:* securities with the largest remaining scheduled supply relative to liquidity have
   more negative abnormal returns over the pre-window period than matched securities. **Kill:** no
   cross-sectional difference, or the wrong sign.
2. *Within-name:* the abnormal return into the plan's window end is negative and reverses after the
   window closes. **Kill:** no reversal.
3. *Dose-response:* the effect scales with the remaining supply-to-liquidity ratio, not with plan
   existence.
4. *Exogeneity:* the effect survives controls for earnings-announcement dates, index rebalances and
   overlapping plan windows, and is stronger for plans whose windows are *not* near earnings (S-183).
5. *Cost floor:* the gross concession must exceed round-trip cost including borrow on the short leg.
6. *Decay:* if the effect is competed away it should shrink over calendar time; a stable effect over a
   decade would itself be evidence against the mechanism.

**Confounders and rival explanations.** (i) Earnings and information events: plan sales cluster around
earnings, so the price move is information rather than supply. (ii) The adoption-timing channel
(S-183): abnormal returns attach to the *adoption* window, so a rule keyed to execution must exclude
adoption windows. (iii) Insider *buys*, which carry the opposite sign and are discretionary.
(iv) Compensation events: sell-to-cover sales tied to vesting are mechanically linked to the vesting
date and are not evidence of scheduled discretionary supply. (v) Option-expiry and index-rebalance
collisions. (vi) Vendor-constructed datasets with survivorship and plan-classification error.

**Data requirements and feasibility for a future independent validation.** EDGAR filings (10-Q/10-K
Item 408(a) tables; Form 4 XML), daily prices and volumes, short interest. Feasibility:
**medium-low** — every input is public, but a security-level panel with a reliable plan-by-plan
capacity reconciliation must be built from thousands of filings, and which plan a given Form 4 sale
draws on is not always disclosed.

**Theory/evidence status.** Conceptual rationale: supported for the *rule* and the *publication* of the
state (S-182, S-183); **unsupported** for the price-pressure link, for which no published work was
located in this cycle. Mechanism supported by sources: partially — the flow's scheduled,
non-discretionary character is documented; the price effect is not. Prior empirical support reported
by others: for plan-timing price effects around adoption (S-183, as characterised by the SEC); for
insider-trading informativeness generally (a known baseline, R-005). Unresolved: whether the
*execution* window carries a distinct, tradeable price effect once information events are excluded.

**Remaining uncertainty / confidence.** Confidence in the mechanism: moderate (the rule and the
disclosure are certain; the price link is conjectural). Confidence that the state is decision-time:
high (quarterly Item 408(a) plus Form 4 updates). Confidence that the effect survives costs: low.
Priority score **61/100** — mechanism, causal coherence and persistence **4 → 20.0**; evidence
quality and independence **2 → 8.0**; substantive novelty **3 → 9.0**; falsifiability **4 → 12.0**;
resilience to costs, crowding and decay **2 → 6.0**; data availability **3 → 6.0**.
**Status parked (P-011), not ranked** — a parked candidate is not in the ranking because its
precondition is open; if G-21 closes in the candidate's favour it would enter between IDEA-010
(65.0) and IDEA-009 (60.0).

**Cycle 30 screen (G-13 publication timing; G-12 sign or existence) — G-13 PASS, G-12 PASS on the
existence limb; parked (P-011) on evidence.**
- **G-13 — PASS.** The state is published by the issuer (Item 408(a), quarterly, in the 10-Q/10-K)
  and by the insider (Form 4, within two business days of the trade), both before or at the time of
  the supply they describe, so a rule can be run on public information. This is the register's
  cleanest *legal-disclosure* instance of a decision-time state.
- **G-12 — PASS on the existence limb.** The claim is that the effect exists only where scheduled
  supply is large relative to liquidity, so the state conditions the *existence* of a tradeable
  concession rather than merely its size.
- **Why parked rather than retained.** The register rejects only when a published result *shows* the
  candidate's effect, and nothing published shows this effect — so the candidate is not rejected. But
  the register also does not promote a candidate whose nearest published evidence concerns a
  different event (plan *adoption* around earnings, S-183) and whose magnitude is most likely below
  the cost floor for most names. It therefore enters as **parked (P-011)** with one unblocking
  condition, **G-21**: is there published work that (a) constructs a security-level *remaining
  scheduled supply* state from Item 408(a) and Form 4 filings, and (b) documents a price effect
  attached to the *execution* window rather than the adoption window? If yes and the effect is
  positive, the candidate is re-scored and enters the ranking; if the answer is a published null on
  execution-window price pressure, the candidate is rejected; if nothing is published, it stays
  parked with an evidence-first condition. **No validation specification is written**; V-010 is not
  opened.

**Role in the portfolio.** The register's first candidate in a *pre-announced, non-discretionary
insider supply* family, and the first whose state is published by corporate disclosure law rather
than by a market operator, a regulator or an exchange. The economics are ordinary
liquidity-provision economics; the register's claim is only that the *schedule* is unusually well
published — which is exactly the kind of claim that must be earned with a price-effect literature
this cycle did not find.

### Cycle 35 (2026-10-20) — G-21's IDEA-015 limb confirmed: the published 10b5-1 effect is an *adoption*-window effect, so P-011 stands

- **The unblocking condition was re-run and re-answered.** The condition registered in Cycle 30 was:
  is there published work that (a) constructs a security-level *remaining scheduled supply* state from
  Item 408(a) and Form 4 filings, and (b) documents a price effect attached to the **execution**
  window rather than the **adoption** window?
- **What was found.** The published Rule 10b5-1 literature attaches its effect to **participation and
  adoption**. S-224 (Jagolinzer 2008, "SEC Rule 10b5-1 and insiders' strategic trade", *Management
  Science* 55(2): 224–239) finds that "participating sales systematically follow positive and precede
  negative firm performance, generating abnormal forward-looking returns larger than those earned by
  non-participating colleagues", and that "a substantive proportion of randomly drawn initiations are
  associated with pending adverse news disclosures". Read with the register's existing sources on the
  adoption-window effect (S-182, S-183, S-187), the published result is an *adoption-window* result and
  nothing located attaches a price effect to the **execution** of a disclosed schedule.
- **Verdict.** No execution-window result was located, so the condition is **not met** and the parking
  stands. **P-011 is confirmed**; IDEA-015 remains parked at 61.0 with its evidence-first condition
  unchanged; **no validation specification is written**; the candidate may not be re-specified around an
  easier state, and may not use sell-to-cover sales tied to vesting and tax withholding.
- **Score: 61.0, unchanged**, and it stays outside the active ranking.

---

## State-proxy methodological note (closing section)

**Purpose.** This note answers the question deferred by Cycles 1 and 2: how to choose, build and
validate a state proxy, and when the honest answer is "no public proxy exists — park the
candidate rather than re-specify it." It governs every candidate in the register whose mechanism
depends on a latent state.

**1. The state-proxy problem is largely a venue-selection problem.**
IDEA-002 (equities, funding state) is parked because no public funding-state proxy exists
(G-01); IDEA-007 (FX, dollar funding state) works because the BIS publishes the state on a known
calendar (S-052, S-054). IDEA-004 (equities, crowd depth) is now resolved on *timing* — the
13F-based crowd state is published on EDGAR within 45 days of each quarter end, so a
quarter-anchored rule is decision-time feasible — and its binding constraint is **proxy
validity** (G-04), not staleness; the Cycle-5 characterisation of it as "quarterly-stale" was
wrong about which question it was blocked on. IDEA-005 (futures, passive share) passed on timing
(CFTC publishes Index Trader positions weekly — S-030) but was **parked in Cycle 6 (P-009)** on
G-12, because its own state's closest published test is null (S-099) and the aggregate effect it
would condition is documented as decayed (S-033) — a different failure, and a reminder that a
*published* state is a feasibility property, not a source of novelty. IDEA-008 (securities
lending, borrow cost) **no longer works**: the register's Cycle-3 assessment that "short interest
and lending fees are published" was wrong about the lending-fee leg, and the Cycle-6 screen
established that the lending-rate data are not publicly disseminated before **29 March 2029**
(S-126) while the short-interest file is twice-monthly and published roughly seven to eight
business days after its settlement date (S-127); IDEA-008 is **parked (P-010)** with a dated
unblocking condition (G-14). IDEA-009 (crypto perpetuals, funding rate) works because the
exchange publishes the funding rate every few hours. The pattern is deterministic: *do not hunt for
a proxy; hunt for a venue where the state is already published by an institution* — and, added in
Cycle 6, *check the publication calendar of the state against the decision calendar before
assuming the venue qualifies*.

**2. How to choose a state proxy.**
A state proxy must satisfy all five conditions:
(a) **Observable at decision time:** the value must be knowable before the trade is executed,
   with the true publication lag respected. A quarterly holdings file used on a daily rule is
   look-ahead, not a proxy.
(b) **Published by an institution:** an exchange, regulator, benchmark administrator, settlement
   utility, or official statistics body releases it on a fixed calendar. Vendor-constructed
   series are proxies of proxies and must be labelled as such.
(c) **Causally connected to the mechanism:** the proxy must measure the binding constraint or
   flow that generates the edge, not a correlated variable. The carry channel is a rival
   explanation for the funding channel; the proxy must separate them.
(d) **Changes the sign or existence of the effect:** conditioning on the proxy must change the
   *sign* or the *existence* of the predicted return, not merely its size (per the rediscovery
   guard in `rejected_and_parked.md`).
(e) **Published before the decision, not after it** (added in Cycle 5, gap **G-13**): the state must
   be released *before* the decision date it informs, not merely before the end of the sample. An
   official state published on a calendar that runs *after* the decision it is supposed to inform
   can support an explanatory decomposition but can never support a decision-time rule. This is not
   a technicality: in the EU ETS the aggregate compliance balance is published in late May or June
   of the year *after* the emissions year (S-121), so under the pre-2023 April deadline it was
   published *after* the deadline, and the decision-time form of IDEA-013 begins only with the 2023
compliance year. **Every candidate must now record, for each observation, the publication date of
    the state relative to the decision date.**
    Cycle 6 applied condition (e) as a *screening test* to every active candidate and to the one
    new candidate, and the results are in each dossier's "Cycle 6 screen" block. Two outcomes are
    worth carrying forward as method: (i) **a published state can satisfy (e) even when it is
    assembled rather than published as an index** — IDEA-004's crowd state is built from filings
    that are individually published on a statutory cadence, and IDEA-014's net obligated position
    is an assembly of published standards, published generation data and published exemption
    decisions; what matters is that *every input* is published before the decision and that the
    assembly rule is fixed in advance; and (ii) **a state can fail (e) on a dated, institutional
    ground rather than a judgement** — IDEA-008 fails because the regulation that will publish the
    lending rates does not begin public dissemination until 29 March 2029 (S-126), and the
    register now treats a *dated* future publication calendar as a parking condition with an
    unblocking date rather than as a permanent rejection.

**3. How to build it without look-ahead.**
- Use only vintages available at the decision timestamp; if the series is revised, state whether
  real-time or latest-vintage data are used.
- Respect the true publication lag: a decision on day T may use only reports published on or
  before day T.
- Make the proxy construction point-in-time: no backfilled index membership, no survivorship,
  no future information in the selection of the universe.
- Freeze the proxy definition on the training window; do not re-estimate it after seeing holdout
  results.

**4. How to validate it against an independent measure.**
- Validate the fast proxy against at least one holdings-based or institution-reported measure
  (e.g. IDEA-004's proxy against S-026's holdings-based crowding; IDEA-002's proxy against
  disclosed dealer funding value adjustments). If validation fails, the candidate is untestable
  as specified — report that, do not re-specify on the fly.
- The validation itself must be out of sample relative to the trading test.

**5. When to park rather than re-specify.**
*(Cycle-78 addition, at the head of this rule.)* **IDEA-010 is the register's first case of this rule
decided by a *kill condition* rather than by a failed validation:** its state was published and validated
(G-16 closed in the candidate's favour in Cycle 32) and its relation is published (S-194, S-195), but the
state is **not attachable to the candidate's own cross-section** (G-29 coverage 0 corporate-bond ETFs with
a published AP set; G-30 exhausted across four outside routes) and its residual is **unmeasurable as
specified** (G-34's kill condition, fired in Cycle 77). That is the case the rule below already anticipated —
"the honest answer is 'no public proxy exists'" — and the conclusion is the one this note has always
prescribed: **park the candidate; change venue, not proxy.** The candidate is parked (P-014) at a terminal
55.0, **not re-specified** around the published competitor conditioner (S-195) or any published crowding or
capacity proxy.
The honest answer is "no public proxy exists" when:
- the binding constraint is not published by any institution (IDEA-002's equity funding state,
  G-01);
- the only available measure is stale relative to the decision frequency (IDEA-004's crowd
  proxy, G-04);
- the fast version of the state must be constructed from paid vendor quotes (IDEA-007's
  cross-currency basis, G-10);
- the validator cannot confirm access to the required proprietary data (IDEA-006's intraday fix
  data);
- **the state will not be publicly published until a known future date** — added in Cycle 6 for
  IDEA-008: the SEC's Rule 10c-1a loan-rate data are not publicly disseminated until **29 March
  2029** at the earliest (S-126), so on the present calendar the binding constraint is not
  published by any institution. This case is **parked with a dated unblocking condition** (P-010,
  G-14), not rejected, because the blocker has a known end date;
- **the candidate's own state variable has a published null and the effect it would condition is
  documented as decayed** — added in Cycle 6 for IDEA-005 (P-009, G-06/S-099/S-033). This case is
  also parked rather than rejected, because the joint claim is untested rather than refuted; but it
  must not be presented as a discovery and it must not be re-specified around a weaker proxy.
In each case, park the candidate. Re-specifying around a weaker proxy converts a good idea into a
disguised baseline. Change venue, not proxy.

**6. Candidates governed by this note.**
IDEA-002 (parked, P-004, G-01), IDEA-004 (passes (e) on a filing-anchored rule; blocked on G-04
proxy validity), IDEA-005 (S-030, public — passes (e); **parked (P-009)** on G-12), IDEA-007
(S-054, public — passes), IDEA-008 (**fails (e)** — lending rates not public before 29 March 2029,
S-126; **parked (P-010)**, G-14), IDEA-009 (funding rate, published by venue — passes), IDEA-010
(**parked (P-014, Cycle 78)** — G-16 closed in its favour in Cycle 32, but the state turned out not to be
attachable to the candidate's own universe (G-29 coverage 0, G-30 exhausted) and the residual unmeasurable
(G-34 kill condition), so rule 5 below applies and the candidate was parked rather than re-specified on a
weaker proxy), IDEA-013
(published state, but **fails condition (e)** for the pre-2023 regime — parked, P-008), IDEA-014
(passes (e) — the obligation's size is codified (S-128), the deadline is published by regulation
(S-129) and moves only by published rule (S-131)), IDEA-015 (passes (e) — the state is published by
**disclosure law**: Item 408(a) quarterly tables and Form 4 plan disclosures (S-182, S-187);
**parked (P-011)** on evidence, G-21).

**7. A fourth kind of state publisher, added in Cycle 30: securities-law disclosure.**
The register's venue pattern has been "a market operator, a regulator or a settlement utility
publishes the state" (condition (e) above). IDEA-015 is the first candidate whose state is published
by **corporate disclosure law** instead — the issuer must file the plan's material terms quarterly,
and the insider must file each plan sale within two business days (S-182, S-187). This class has
two properties that the other publishers do not: the state is **mandatory and standardised** (no
vendor discretion, no publication discretion) and its **cadence is statutory**. It also has two
limitations: the state is **coarse** (an aggregate maximum size and a window, not an execution
schedule) and the **reconciliation** of individual Form 4 sales to individual plans is not always
disclosed, so the state must be *constructed* rather than read. The register's methodological
conclusion is unchanged — a state must still be published *before* the decision, and it must still be
valid for the quantity being conditioned — but a future cycle looking for a new venue should look
for this class of publisher first, and should expect to pay for the construction step.

